\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Christian Conservatives Feel Frustrated in a New Political World\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias and Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On a recent Tuesday afternoon, \uc2 Sam Brownback\uc2 , the former governor of Kansas and a champion of socially conservative causes, asked a small crowd of his fellow Christian voters if they were feeling discouraged.\par \par \uc2 Inside this church in Grapevine, Texas, nearly every hand shot up.\par \par \uc2 The response might seem mystifying: These voters had won huge victories, most notably in overturning Roe v. Wade, which had guaranteed the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Brownback sat alongside a former football coach whose victory in another Supreme Court decision allowing prayer on the field symbolized the court's decisive swing in their favor.\par \par \uc2 But with the presidential election closing in, hope suddenly felt thin. Powerful efforts to ''suffocate'' their values seemed to be everywhere, Mr. Brownback said to widespread agreement.\par \par \uc2 He tried to offer reassurance. He recounted the biblical story of Gideon, who triumphed in battle against a vast army with only 300 men by his side.\par \par \uc2 ''God never needs a majority,'' he said. ''All he needs is a faithful remnant.''\par \par \uc2 As opposition to their social agenda grows, particularly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many conservative Christians are experiencing whiplash as they grapple with an uncertain future.\par \par \uc2 For years, many evangelical Christians and Catholics operated with the sense that they were a silent majority. If they could simply motivate enough of their fellow Christians to vote, they believed they could win, and often they did.\par \par \uc2 By all indications, conservative Christians are poised to vote again overwhelmingly for former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in November. But now, they are facing the reality that many of their views are not widely held, and that to advance their goals nationally they need power at the highest levels -- power that Mr. Trump suddenly seems less inclined to give.\par \par \uc2 In that way, the coming election feels like a referendum on the role of conservative Christianity in American public life. And some conservative Christians worry that it is a race that is harder and harder to win.\par \par \uc2 The country is growing more secular and pluralistic by the year, with regular church attendance declining. Many leaders in the Republican Party, their political home for decades, have gone silent about their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and same-sex marriage. And, Mr. Trump, the man once considered to be their strongest champion, is publicly distancing himself from their causes, even as he attacked Democrats in the presidential debate for their support of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For decades, their views have been embraced and pushed by the Republican Party. When Mr. Trump ran for president in 2016, he made a promise -- ''Christianity will have power'' -- to groups unsure of his loyalty to their cause. Even after he lost re-election in 2020, Mr. Trump gave them overwhelming victories through the judicial system he remade. Overturning Roe was the culmination of a generational battle for conservative Christians, delivering a victory that amounted to one of the biggest political resurgences in American history.\par \par \uc2 But in this post-Roe era, the political landscape has grown increasingly fraught for them.\par \par \uc2 ''We had been on offense for 50 years, and now they're on offense,'' said \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2 , a longtime conservative operative who led the Christian Coalition in the 1990s. ''That doesn't mean that we won't ultimately prevail, it just means that we're in a different season in that struggle.''\par \par \uc2 In some cases, their priorities are more visible than ever, particularly in states that are Republican strongholds. The chief justice of the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  invoked their God in a landmark ruling declaring that frozen embryos had rights as children. Oklahoma required that public schools teach the Bible, in an extraordinary blurring of religious instruction and public education.\par \par \uc2 But the end of Roe led to mainstream backlash that they have struggled to navigate. Christian activists' opposition not only to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also to I.V.F. and some forms of contraception, has proved deeply unpopular with voters. Their movement has transformed from an asset to a liability, prompting Mr. Trump and other Republicans to soften their once-staunch opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 It is far from clear that a second Trump administration would abandon their movement.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  of Ohio, the Republican vice-presidential nominee, remains deeply committed to their vision, maintaining his staunch opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and belief in the primacy of the traditional family. Mr. Trump's allies have spent years making detailed plans, outlined in Project 2025, to enact a socially conservative agenda that could transform not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but also marriage, education, transgender rights and the role of Christianity in American public life. And even if Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wins the election, there's no swift way for Democrats to reverse the impact the Trump administration had on the federal judiciary.\par \par \uc2 But conservative Christians are frustrated. Mr. Trump seems ''disinclined'' to reach out to his Christian base, said Cole Muzio, the president of Frontline Policy Action, a group in Georgia that pushes for ''Godly policies'' with a network of conservative Christian organizations.\par \par \uc2 Even the mailers the Trump campaign and the Georgia Republican Party have sent to his house are vexing, Mr. Muzio said.\par \par \uc2 The fliers point out that ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is against Project 2025, which I am for; and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is pro-I.V.F. mandate, which I am against; and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes that some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is good and should be ultimately decided by states, and that's what we've always wanted, and I disagree with that premise,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Bunni Pounds, who heads Christians Engaged, an organization that works with churches to register and influence conservative Christians, said she was disappointed that the new Republican Party platform had removed a reference to marriage being between a man and a woman.\par \par \uc2 ''The Trump campaign has made it harder for people like me who are trying to galvanize the Christian vote,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 At the very least, she said, she was confident that ''Bible-believing Christians'' would not be voting for Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 At the event in Texas, the emcee, Ian Giatti, a reporter for The Christian Post, described the mood this year in his circle as a more extreme version of the ''Hold your nose and vote'' atmosphere of 2016. That year, eight in 10 white evangelicals voted for Mr. Trump, but there was a vocal current of dissent and disappointment. In 2020 the share was similar, but with more enthusiasm given all Mr. Trump had accomplished for them.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Giatti recently produced a podcast episode criticizing the delivery of a Sikh prayer at the Republican National Convention.\par \par \uc2 ''There was this real sense that the Republican Party no longer is the home for conservative Christians,'' he said. ''There's a sea change, and it definitely feels deliberate.''\par \par \uc2 The scope of their communities' core causes is also continuing to widen. In 2020, Wenyuan Wu, a researcher and recent evangelical convert, helped start the Californians for Equal Rights Foundation to fight things like race-conscious admissions and diversity, equity and inclusion programs in the wake of the state's push for affirmative action.\par \par \uc2 Though the political climate, especially in California, seems hostile to her cause, she said, ''it is possible to score small victories.'' Her group sued California in 2021, ultimately resulting in the removal of Aztec and African prayers from the public school curriculum.\par \par \uc2 ''People like me may be in the minority, but we believe that Christianity will stand the test of time,'' she said. ''It is not going to be propped up or hammered down because of an election.''\par \par \uc2 The mobilization effort is also pressing ahead. Wisconsin Family Action, a Christian advocacy group, is on track to put an insert into the Sunday bulletins of at least 700 evangelical churches in the state, urging worshipers to vote. It is a fourfold increase from the 2022 midterms, said Daniel Degner, interim president of the group.\par \par \uc2 He said that he was ''not at all concerned'' about the preservation of conservative Christian values.\par \par \uc2 He thought of the biblical book of Romans. ''If the Apostle Paul was able to influence government for God's purposes and God's designs in Rome, where they were literally killing Christians for being Christians,'' he said, ''I believe we can still influence government for Christian values in America today.''\par \par \uc2 Some conservative Christians feel their beliefs are now seen as liabilities by many Republicans, especially on \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY TIM GRUBER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Former President Donald J. Trump alongside his running mate, Senator JD Vance of Ohio, at a campaign stop in Asheboro, N.C.; Despite their feeling of alienation, conservative Christians are poised to vote again overwhelmingly for Mr. Trump in November. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240918ek9i0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
