\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Mormons in Arizona Rethink Ties to Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning \par \uc2 1,611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Traditionally Republican members of the \uc2 Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints\uc2  balked at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020, helping \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  win a key swing state. Will they do so with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 A group of Mormon men sat around a coffee table at a house in Mesa, Ariz., on one recent September evening, debating a scenario that would have felt implausible just a decade ago: Could thousands of Mormon voters, disillusioned and disgusted by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , help deliver a key battleground state to Democrats in November?\par \par \uc2 It's already happened once. President Biden won 18 percent of Arizona's Mormon voters in 2020, according to exit polls, a small but significant defection from a reliably Republican voting bloc that helped Mr. Biden flip the state blue by just 10,457 votes. Seeking their support for the third time, Mr. Trump appears to be dividing the state's Mormons more than ever, according to interviews with more than a dozen Mormon voters and state political analysts -- which could give Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  an edge.\par \par \uc2 ''People in my moderate circle of friends are watching and saying, 'I can no longer do this,''' said Mike Badgett, one of the men in Mesa who was speculating about a greater shift toward Democrats. ''I think the wheels are coming off of this agreement.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Badgett was referring to the uneasy alliance between Mr. Trump and deeply religious voters. For years, many members of the \uc2 Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints\uc2  supported Mr. Trump, often reluctantly. They were turned off by his vulgarity, his disdain for women and his attacks on immigrants, anathema to the church's pro-immigrant, pro-refugee message.\par \par \uc2 But they had been Republicans for generations, driven by a shared belief in traditional family values and Christian conservatism, and they were encouraged by his pledge to appoint conservative Supreme Court justices who they hoped would overturn Roe v. Wade and end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was one segment of a tacit understanding between Christian voters and Mr. Trump: They would ignore his character defects, and he would advance their policy priorities.\par \par \uc2 Since 2020, though, Mormons say members of their religion have grown even more skeptical of the former president, an idea supported by surveys and other data.\par \par \uc2 They were disturbed by Mr. Trump's involvement in the Jan. 6 Capitol riot and his criminal indictments and felony conviction, which seemed to validate their longstanding concerns about his character. And traditional Mormon attitudes are changing, spurred by a younger and more diverse generation of millennial and Generation Z churchgoers who are more open-minded about issues like L.G.B.T.Q. rights and divorce.\par \par \uc2 ''There's new ideas coming into the house,'' said Brittany Romanello, a faculty associate of anthropology at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  who studies Mormon culture and politics. ''I think that is a huge reason older Mormons are changing their voting patterns.''\par \par \uc2 Even on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , long a bedrock issue for churchgoers, there are signs that some Mormon women are having second thoughts. The church officially opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except in cases of rape, incest or to save the life of the mother, but some Mormon women said the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that some states passed after the overturning of Roe v. Wade ran contrary to their religious principles that emphasize autonomy and agency. (Earlier this year, Arizona's Supreme Court reinstated a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before the State Legislature repealed it, reverting to a 15-week restriction.)\par \par \uc2 Allison Jones, a photographer who lives in Gilbert, east of Phoenix, said she thought a wave of Mormon women would cast their votes for Ms. Harris this November -- silently -- while their husbands would continue to support Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''It's on the D.L.,'' Ms. Jones, 59, said, meaning on the down-low. ''There are going to be a lot of women that are going to get their mail-in ballots, they're going to walk into that booth, and their husbands are never going to know what they did.''\par \par \uc2 Sherry Macnab, 47, a Mesa resident and corporate event manager, said she knew of women who had placed Post-it notes in women's restrooms in churches, reminding them that their husbands would not know whom they voted for.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a big discrepancy between male and female voters right now,'' Ms. Macnab said.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, many Mormons remain firmly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a majority will most likely stick with Mr. Trump in November.\par \par \uc2 Jared Taylor\uc2 , the chief executive of Heritage Academy schools, which are public charter schools, said he knew of fellow Mormons who voted for Mr. Biden in 2020 and were sheepishly returning to Mr. Trump's side, fueled by Republican advocacy for religious liberty.\par \par \uc2 ''President Trump has been very vocal, very supportive,'' Mr. Taylor, 52, said. ''Biden and Harris have been mute'' and haven't spoken out on what he sees as the persecution of Christians.\par \par \uc2 Halee Dobbins, a spokeswoman for the Trump campaign in Arizona, offered a long list of examples of Mr. Trump siding with Christian groups during his presidency, and expressed confidence that the Mormon community was behind him.\par \par \uc2 Tara Fischbeck, 44, of Mesa, said she supported Mr. Trump because he was strong on the border, would protect the freedom of speech and ''believes in the nuclear family.''\par \par \uc2 ''His family is stalwart; they're amazing,'' she said. ''I think that tells a lot about him as a person.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats believe that many Mormons are persuadable. There are nearly 500,000 Mormons in Arizona -- or about 7 percent of the population -- according to the church, though that number includes children.\par \par \uc2 A grass-roots group called Latter-day Saints for Harris-Walz that works with the campaign held a video call in August to generate enthusiasm, and plans to begin in-person events this fall, according to Rob Taber, an organizer.\par \par \uc2 And on Thursday, about 50 people, many of them Mormon, met at a home in a neighborhood in Gilbert to listen to a campaign speech from Representative \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , the Democrat running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  against Kari Lake. Such a gathering might have been unlikely not long ago, but Republican attendees said they felt some of their party's candidates were no longer people they could support.\par \par \uc2 ''My religion helps to guide my moral compass,'' said Hillary Webster, 51, a preschool teacher. She said she wrote in her husband's name for president in 2016, and then supported Mr. Biden. She now supports Ms. Harris. ''Being raised Mormon, I know that the power's within myself to make good choices.''\par \par \uc2 Arizona's most prominent pro-Harris Mormon is John Giles, the Republican mayor of Mesa. Mr. Giles spoke at the Democratic convention in Chicago last month, and he shared a slide show of photos from his trip at the meeting of the Mormon men in Mesa.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump presented an ''interesting dilemma'' for Mormons, Mr. Giles said.\par \par \uc2 ''His lifestyle is so abhorrent to ours,'' he said. ''Yes, he's a Republican, but he's not like us in any other way.''\par \par \uc2 After, Mr. Giles and the others debated whether Latter-day Saints voters would feel the church had given them the permission they felt they needed to potentially flip to Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 A letter from the church's top leaders last year warned against blindly voting down the ballot for one party without carefully considering candidates and issues, calling such an act a ''threat to democracy.'' Some interpreted this as a nudge not to reflexively vote for Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Brian Dille, of Gilbert, said he thought the notice might make a difference for Mormons who had grown sick of Mr. Trump but were struggling to break free of a tradition of Republican support.\par \par \uc2 ''It provides an exit door,'' he said. ''Being a Mormon Democrat in the Intermountain West is lonely -- people won't talk to me. But that's changed.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats may not succeed in turning an enormous number of Mormons into outright supporters of Ms. Harris, with whom they still have substantial policy differences. Even Senator Mitt Romney of Utah, a prominent Mormon critic of Mr. Trump, has not said whether he voted for Mr. Biden in 2020 -- only that he did not vote for Mr. Trump. On Wednesday, he declined to tell reporters in Washington if he would back Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 But there is a sense that a growing number may at least choose not to vote for Mr. Trump, instead selecting a third-party candidate or abstaining.\par \par \uc2 Walking to a church in Mesa on a recent afternoon, Braeden and Amanda Lee said they had traditionally voted for Republicans, but remained undecided this year.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not super enthused about either party,'' Mr. Lee said.\par \par \uc2 Others remain loyal to Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 David Filbrun, who owns a trampoline store in Mesa, said that under President Biden, he had to raise the price of his largest trampoline to $2,400 from $1,600 over the last four years, because the cost of materials had increased so much. Homelessness near his store is on the rise, he added, and he believed Mr. Trump's policies would improve the country.\par \par \uc2 ''To me, it's really whatever or whoever is pro-America,'' Mr. Filbrun said.\par \par \uc2 But he admitted not everyone shared his view.\par \par \uc2 ''The L.D.S. community is divided,'' Mr. Filbrun said, ''which is the first time I've ever really thought that.''\par \par \uc2 Nicole Ludden contributed reporting from Gilbert, Ariz.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Ludden contributed reporting from Gilbert, Ariz.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240918ek9e0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
