\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 North Dakota Judge Overturns Near-Total Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A judge ruled that the State Constitution protected a woman's right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the fetus was viable. The state's attorney general said he would appeal.\par \par \uc2 A North Dakota judge overturned the state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on Thursday, saying that the State Constitution protected a woman's right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the fetus was viable.\par \par \uc2 ''The North Dakota Constitution guarantees each individual, including women, the fundamental right to make medical judgments affecting his or her bodily integrity, health and autonomy, in consultation with a chosen health care provider free from government interference,'' wrote Judge Bruce Romanick of the district court in Burleigh County.\par \par \uc2 The judge, who won his seat in a nonpartisan election, said the ban violated numerous constitutional guarantees, including the right to liberty and to ''pursue and obtain safety and happiness.'' He also said it violated the State Constitution's due process protections because it was too vague in how it defined exceptions to the ban.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Drew H. Wrigley, a Republican, said he planned to appeal. And while the judge's order means that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become legal soon, the procedure will remain largely unavailable because the only clinic in the state, which sued to overturn the ban in 2022, has moved to Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Still, doctors said that it would restore their ability to provide abortions in hospitals, particularly to women with pregnancy complications. Now, they say they often have to send those women out of state, or make them wait until they get sicker out of fear of violating the ban, which punishes doctors with up to five years in prison and a $10,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 ''I've cared for these women, I've been there for all of their pregnancy complications, and the fear has been real,'' Dr. Ana Tobiasz, who was a plaintiff in the suit, said after the ruling. ''Nobody wants these bans -- not patients, not doctors, not families.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wrigley, who was named as the lead defendant in the lawsuit filed by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the clinic and four doctors, said in a statement that while he had ''appropriate regard'' for the district court, ''a careful reading of Judge Romanick's decision reveals flaws in his analysis and interpretation of controlling precedent.''\par \par \uc2 The statement added: ''Judge Romanick's opinion inappropriately casts aside the law crafted by the legislative branch of our government and ignores the applicable and controlling case law previously announced by the North Dakota Supreme Court.''\par \par \uc2 It is the second time in two years that Judge Romanick has overturned the state's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the legislature passed as a trigger ban to take effect if the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, which it did in 2022. Last year, the judge ruled that the State Constitution protected a fundamental right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect a pregnant woman's health or life, but stopped short of saying whether there was a broader right.\par \par \uc2 After the State Supreme Court upheld that ruling, the legislature repealed the previous ban and enacted an almost identical law that included some exceptions, to ''prevent the death or health risk to the pregnant female'' or before six weeks of pregnancy, if that pregnancy resulted from incest or ''gross sexual imposition.'' That law took effect in April 2023.\par \par \uc2 In his 24-page ruling on Thursday, Judge Romanick said it did not matter that the framers of the State Constitution, which was enacted in 1889, did not specifically mention protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is that 'individuals' did not draft and enact the North Dakota Constitution. Men did,'' he wrote. ''And many, if not all, of the men who enacted the North Dakota Constitution, and who wrote the state laws of the time, did not view women as equal citizens with equal liberty interests.''\par \par \uc2 Only in 1984, he noted, did the legislature amend the constitution to say that it applied to ''individuals'' instead of ''men.''\par \par \uc2 He warned that giving the government the authority to tell women when they could not have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also grant it the power to tell them when they must have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Those choices belong to the individual, not the government,'' he wrote. ''That is the essence of what liberty and happiness require.''\par \par \uc2 Viability is the point in pregnancy when a fetus can survive outside the uterus, around 24 weeks. Until that point, the judge wrote, ''a woman must retain the ultimate control over her own destiny, her own body and ultimately the path of her life.''\par \par \uc2 Meetra Mehdizadeh, a staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called the decision ''monumental'' but said that even if it made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal, most people seeking one wouldn't be able to get it.\par \par \uc2 ''Clinics are medical facilities that need to acquire doctors, staff, equipment,'' she said. ''They can take years to open, like most health care centers. The destructive impacts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are felt long after they are struck down.''\par \par \uc2 The Republican-controlled legislature can, and very likely will, try to enact another ban when it convenes again in January.\par \par \uc2 Tammi Kromenaker, the director of the clinic that moved, Red River Women's Clinic, said in a statement that she looked forward to a ''new future in North Dakota'' so long as legislators ''finally give up on their crusade to force pregnancy on people against their will.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240913ek9d0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
