\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ballot Issue On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Restored In Missouri\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court ruled hours before the state's deadline for printing ballots for absentee voters, reversing the secretary of state who had invalidated the measure weeks after certifying it.\par \par \uc2 The Missouri Supreme Court on Tuesday rejected a bid to strike a question on the November ballot that will ask voters whether to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The seven-member court handed down its one-page ruling less than three hours before the state's deadline for printing ballots for absentee voters. The ruling capped a furious few days of legal maneuvers, as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and state Republicans made a last-ditch effort to stop the ballot amendment.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision is a victory for both direct democracy and reproductive freedom in Missouri,'' Rachel Sweet, the campaign manager for the coalition backing the measure, Missourians for Constitutional Freedom. ''This fight was not just about this amendment, it was about defending the integrity of the initiative petition process and ensuring that Missourians can shape their future directly.''\par \par \uc2 The four anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates who tried to strike the measure said in a statement that the court had ''turned a blind eye'' to their arguments that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups did not follow constitutional procedures when they circulated petitions to sponsor the amendment.\par \par \uc2 ''The fight continues against the vile forces who have no regard for innocent life,'' said the statement, signed by two activists, Peggy Forrest and Kathy Forck, and two Republican lawmakers, State Senator Mary Elizabeth Coleman and State Representative Hannah Kelly.\par \par \uc2 The measure, which the secretary of state certified in August, is one of 10 in states across the country that would establish protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like those that were identified in the federal Constitution in Roe v. Wade, which the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned in 2022.\par \par \uc2 In the two years since, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups have prevailed in seven \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures across the country, including in conservative-leaning states like Kansas and Ohio. In Missouri, they collected more than 380,159 signatures on petitions to place the measure on the ballot, a record for any initiative in the state and more than twice the number required.\par \par \uc2 With the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe motivating Democrats to the polls, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and Republicans have fought to stop the ballot measures from appearing before voters. The Arizona Supreme Court last month rejected a bid to strike the question from the ballot there. Nebraska's Supreme Court heard arguments on Monday on a similar bid, and a trial seeking to strike the measure in South Dakota is scheduled for late this month.\par \par \uc2 The fight has been especially contentious in Missouri, a Republican-controlled state that was the first to officially ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court reversed Roe.\par \par \uc2 The two sides had battled in competing lawsuits for 18 months, with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and the state's Republican attorney general and secretary of state trying to block the measure, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups resisting what they called biased interference from politicians.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups pitched their final challenge last week, arguing in their suit that the petitions that proponents had voters sign to get the measure on the ballot did not inform them of all the laws that the amendment might reverse.\par \par \uc2 Judge Christopher Limbaugh of Cole County Circuit Court, who was appointed by the state's Republican governor after serving as his general counsel and is a cousin of the late conservative radio host \uc2 Rush Limbaugh\uc2 , sided with the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups late Friday. But he said he would not ask the state to strike the measure until the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups exhausted any appeals.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Jay Ashcroft\uc2 , a Republican, struck it anyway. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups had asked the State Supreme Court to hold him in contempt.\par \par \uc2 The court did not rule on that request. But shortly before the state's 5 p.m. deadline to print ballots, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure was again listed among the 2024 ballot initiatives on the secretary of state's website.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240911ek9b0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
