\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Harris Put Trump on Defense Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  -- and Kept Him There\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bidgood \par \uc2 772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For Trump, anger seemed to be the feeling of the night.\par \par \uc2 Debating \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that if he were elected president, he would appoint two or three anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Supreme Court justices who would ''automatically'' overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday night, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pressed him to answer for it.\par \par \uc2 ''They did exactly as he intended,'' she said, referring to the justices, before laying out the consequences of the post-Roe rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in searing and painful detail.\par \par \uc2 ''One does not have to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree, the government and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , certainly, should not be telling a woman what to do with her body,'' Harris said.\par \par \uc2 It was the first big confrontation of their first and only scheduled debate, which unfolded on a small stage in Philadelphia where Trump seemed to do his best to avoid so much as looking at his opponent.\par \par \uc2 And it set the tone for a night in which Harris repeatedly pushed Trump onto turf where he was deeply uncomfortable, leaving him defensive as she pressed her case.\par \par \uc2 As Harris drew on emotion by talking about women suffering miscarriages or young incest victims being denied care, Trump grew emotional -- although it was anger that seemed, for him, to be the feeling of the night.\par \par \uc2 After Harris said Trump would be willing to sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, something he has previously indicated a willingness to support, he accused her of lying. He tried to change the subject to student loans, before changing it back to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He claimed he had been a leader on ''fertilization'' and I.V.F. He forced Harris to deflect when he sought to pin her down on whether she would limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the seventh or eighth months of pregnancy, but she soon turned the issue back on him and pressed him to say whether he would veto a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Harris kept Trump on defense for much of the rest of the evening, on everything from his criminal cases to the size of the crowds at his rallies. In what was her one chance to define herself, she flipped the scrutiny back to Trump with a barrage of prepared attacks. Each time, she stuck close to the same theme, casting herself as an agent of progress and Trump as a figure of the past who could do real damage to people's lives.\par \par \uc2 Trump made his key argument against Harris, too.\par \par \uc2 ''She's going to do all these wonderful things,'' Trump said. ''Why hasn't she done it? She's been there for three-and-a-half years.''\par \par \uc2 The problem for him was that he did not do so until his closing statement, in the final moments of the debate.\par \par \uc2 Look what they made her do\par \par \uc2 In the moments after the debate wrapped, a childless cat lady endorsed Vice President Harris. Yes, Taylor Swift weighed in on the 2024 election, doing so in a caption on an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  photo of the musician and Benjamin Button, who is one of her cats.\par \par \uc2 You can read more from my colleagues here.\par \par \uc2 The debate, by the numbers\par \par \uc2 My colleagues carefully tracked speaking time during the debate. Here is some of what they found:\par \par \uc2 Trump spoke more than Harris -- 43:03, to her 37:31 -- but Harris spent more time on the attack.\par \par \uc2 Harris spent 17:25 minutes attacking Trump, while Trump spent 12:54 minutes attacking Harris.\par \par \uc2 The economy was discussed onstage more than any other issue, at more than 10 minutes. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was next, at 7:48\par \par \uc2 See more here.\par \par \uc2 A night of attacks and taking the bait\par \par \uc2 Here's what else to read about the debate.\par \par \uc2 The debate was fill of contentious exchanges over the economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 The question was about immigration. By the time Trump got to talking, he brought up cats and his crowd sizes.\par \par \uc2 Harris said something that might have surprised some viewers: She owns a handgun. ''We're not taking anybody's guns away,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 False claims about inflation, crime and immigration. And a claim about ''transgender operations on illegal aliens that are in prison'' that lacked context. See how my colleagues fact-checked the debate.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald Trump after Tuesday's debate in Philadelphia. Vice President Kamala Harris and Mr. Trump sparred in deeply personal terms, with Ms. Harris often succeeding in forcing Mr. Trump off message. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Graham Dickie/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240911ek9b0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
