\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Can Afford to Disrespect His Pro-Life Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 1,033 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Babylon Bee, which is like The Onion for conservative Christians, last month ran a despairing story about the presidential options anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters have before them. ''Pro-Lifers Excited to Choose Between Moderate Amount of Baby Murder and High Amount of Baby Murder,'' said the headline. It was a dark joke, but it spoke to something real: a disquiet among some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists over Donald Trump's attempts to distance himself from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enabled by his Supreme Court appointees. Albert Mohler, the president of the Southern Baptist Theological Seminary, told my Times colleague Astead W. Herndon that declining evangelical enthusiasm for Trump could be a ''grave danger'' to his campaign.\par \par \uc2 As someone who wants Trump to lose, I hope he's right, but I'm skeptical. ''One of the things that Trump has done is reveal what you might call the G.O.P.'s dirty little secret, and that is that it's never really been only about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Robert P. Jones, the president of the Public Religion Research Institute. Conservative activists, he argued, have long seen themselves as part of a moral crusade against the killing of babies, but Republican voters, even white evangelical ones, tend to have more complicated views. In P.R.R.I. surveys, he said, white evangelicals are more likely to identify the economy, crime and immigration as critical issues than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''The bond between Trump and rank-and-file Republicans and between Trump and white evangelical Protestants has really not been \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Jones.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, a second Trump presidency would be catastrophic for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He obviously doesn't care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is happy to take whatever position suits him at any given moment. But many of the people he will sweep into office with him are devoted to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Part of the purpose of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's Project 2025 was to make sure there was a deep bench of MAGA apparatchiks ready to staff a second Trump administration, freeing him from the legal and bureaucratic roadblocks that stymied his first administration. The same people Heritage selected to defend Trump at all costs have thought deeply about how to use the levers of the federal government to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, in his spasmodic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positioning, Trump has annihilated the expectation that Republicans show deference to the social conservatives who've been crusading against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a generation. On his vanity social media site, Truth Social, he wrote that he would be ''great for women and their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' He's come out against six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. He removed a plank from the Republican Party platform calling for an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment to the Constitution. He's promised that his administration would provide free in vitro fertilization, a procedure that the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  voted to oppose in June.\par \par \uc2 In doing all this without losing significant support among Christian conservatives, he's demonstrated how little leverage the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has over him.\par \par \uc2 Part of the reason Trump is less constrained on this issue than his predecessors is that he's transformed the Christian right just as he has the broader conservative movement, dethroning serious-seeming figures while promoting those once regarded as flamboyant cranks. In Republican politics, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and Alex Jones now have far more influence than erstwhile conservative stalwarts like Paul Ryan and \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 . Similarly, in the religious realm, the ex-president has elevated a class of faith healers, prosperity gospel preachers and roadshow revivalists over the kind of respectable evangelicals who clustered around \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . ''Independent charismatic leaders, who 20 years ago would have been mocked by mainstream religious right leaders, are now frontline captains in the American culture wars,'' writes the scholar Matthew D. Taylor in his fascinating new book, ''The Violent Take It by Force: The Christian Movement That Is Threatening Our Democracy.''\par \par \uc2 The churches Taylor is writing about exist outside the structures and doctrines of denominations like the Southern Baptists. They're led by flashy spiritual entrepreneurs who fashion themselves as modern apostles and prophets with supernatural spiritual gifts, and they represent one of the fastest-growing movements in American Christianity. Among many of these churches, Trump remains the anointed one, chosen by God to restore Christian rule to the United States. These Christians care a great deal about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they appear to care at least as much about Trump. Many of them see him as a modern-day version of the Persian emperor Cyrus, a heathen who, in the sixth century B.C.E., rescued God's chosen people from Babylonian captivity. In this framework, Trump's piety is irrelevant; all that matters is his strength.\par \par \uc2 A key figure in the development of this Christian Trumpism is the evangelical influencer and self-proclaimed prophet Lance Wallnau. As Taylor writes, Wallnau claims that God told him to throw his support behind Trump after meeting him at Trump Tower in 2015. ''No evangelical leader has done more to theologically backstop the presidency of Donald Trump than Lance Wallnau,'' writes Taylor.\par \par \uc2 Wallnau is not concerned about Trump's shifting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions and is dismissive of those who are. ''So all the Christians out there going, 'He's waffling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' -- it's because we suck at political stuff,'' Wallnau said on his podcast in July. ''Let's just admit it.'' As he sees it, if the ex-president is distancing himself from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, the fault lies with Christians who failed to make anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views popular enough to campaign on. Trump doesn't need to offer concessions to the voters Wallnau represents. All they require of him is that he win.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY THALASSA RAASCH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A27. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240911ek9b00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
