\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Astor \par \uc2 1,804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris supports a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and wants to prevent states from banning the procedure before fetal viability. She has regularly spoken out in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including making the first official visit to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic by a president or a vice president.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's Supreme Court appointees enabled the overturning of Roe v. Wade, ending federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He has said that he would not sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban but that states should be allowed to enact any restrictions they choose.\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris wants to enshrine the protections of Roe v. Wade in federal law now that the Supreme Court has overturned it.\par \par \uc2 ''When I am president of the United States, I will sign a law restoring and protecting reproductive freedom in every state,'' she wrote in July. To do that, she would need not just Democratic majorities in Congress but also 50 senators willing to get rid of the filibuster, which requires 60 votes to pass most legislation.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris said last year that she and President Biden envisioned a law mirroring Roe. As modified by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  v. Casey, Roe broadly protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a fetus could survive outside the womb but allowed bans after that point so long as they had exceptions for medical emergencies. ''We're not trying to do anything that did not exist before June of last year,'' she told CBS News.\par \par \uc2 As a senator, she was a sponsor of a bill called the Women's Health Protection Act that would have gone somewhat further than Roe by prohibiting some state-level restrictions, such as requiring doctors to perform specific tests or to have hospital admitting privileges in order to provide abortions. She reiterated her support for it in 2022.\par \par \uc2 She also argued, while running for president in 2019, that states with a history of restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in violation of Roe should be subject to ''pre-clearance'' for new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, meaning those laws would have to be federally approved before they could take effect. Her campaign did not respond to a request to confirm whether she would still support this if Congress codified Roe. (Without such codification, the proposal is moot.)\par \par \uc2 In the absence of congressional majorities capable of codifying Roe, Mr. Biden's cabinet took administrative actions to try to limit the effects of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and Ms. Harris has indicated support for those actions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  told hospitals in 2022 that a law pertaining to emergency rooms, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, obligates doctors to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they believe it is needed to stabilize a patient. (That guidance is subject to legal challenges on which the Supreme Court has so far declined to rule.) In April, the same department announced a rule to shield many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients' medical records from investigators and prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has said that he believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are a state issue. If elected again, he would allow states to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they see fit, including potential monitoring of pregnancies or criminal charges for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, he told Time magazine.\par \par \uc2 ''It's irrelevant whether I'm comfortable or not,'' he said. ''It's totally irrelevant, because the states are going to make those decisions.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump appointed Justices \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  to the Supreme Court. Their votes were critical to overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 He has boasted about that fact on a number of occasions, saying he accomplished what no Republican president before him could and calling himself ''proudly the person responsible'' for the overturning of Roe. He has repeatedly, falsely, claimed that ''all legal scholars'' supported overturning it.\par \par \uc2 ''After 50 years of failure, with nobody coming even close, I was able to kill Roe v. Wade, much to the 'shock' of everyone, and for the first time put the pro life movement in a strong negotiating position,'' he wrote on social media last year, adding, ''Without me there would be no 6 weeks, 10 weeks, 15 weeks, or whatever is finally agreed to.''\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL BAN\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris opposes a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and has said she would veto it if Congress passed one.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said in April that he would not sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, shortly after saying that states should be allowed to set their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Many states, since Roe was overturned, have enacted laws that ban nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 But he has long made contradictory statements on the matter, suggesting as recently as March that he might support some version of a federal ban. (In his first term, he endorsed a ban after 20 weeks of pregnancy.) And in a Time magazine interview published in April, he deflected when asked whether he would veto a bill that defined life as beginning at fertilization.\par \par \uc2 His campaign did not give a yes-or-no answer when asked if he would support enforcing the Comstock Act, an 1873 law that prohibits mailing materials used in abortions, and that some Trump allies want him to use to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally without a formal ban. ''President Trump has long been consistent in supporting the rights of states to make decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' a spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 In August, when asked the same question about enforcing the Comstock Act, he told CBS News, ''We will be discussing specifics of it, but generally speaking, no, I wouldn't.''\par \par \uc2 He has framed his caution around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a political matter because ''you have to win elections.''\par \par \uc2 IN VITRO FERTILIZATION\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris supports access to in vitro fertilization -- a fertility treatment that can be threatened by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures that treat embryos as people with legal rights, since it usually involves creating numerous embryos and destroying or indefinitely freezing unused ones.\par \par \uc2 The matter gained urgency after the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  ruled in February that frozen embryos were children, upending I.V.F. in the state. Ms. Harris condemned the ruling. (The Alabama Legislature subsequently passed a law that gave I.V.F. clinics immunity but did not address embryos' legal status.)\par \par \uc2 ''Individuals, couples who want to start a family are now being deprived of access to what can help them start a family,'' she said at an event in Michigan in February.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  ruling, Mr. Trump said he supported access to I.V.F. because ''we want to make it easier for mothers and fathers to have babies, not harder.'' However, he has not endorsed currently proposed legislation that would protect it.\par \par \uc2 In August, he suggested that he would support a federal mandate that insurance companies cover I.V.F. He did not give any details on how this proposal would work.\par \par \uc2 MEDICATION \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris supports access to mifepristone, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug that conservative litigants and Republican legislators have sought to restrict since the overturning of Roe. Nearly two-thirds of all abortions in the United States are medically induced with a regimen that includes mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The Biden-Harris administration has defended mifepristone. Its solicitor general successfully urged the Supreme Court to reject a lawsuit that had sought to sharply limit access to the drug. The court unanimously upheld access in June, ruling that the plaintiffs in the suit did not have standing; anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have signaled that they may try again with different plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to blunt the effects of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  advised pharmacists that they might violate civil rights laws if they refused to dispense drugs like mifepristone, misoprostol and methotrexate that can be used for abortions but also other medical purposes; Ms. Harris has indicated that she supports this guidance.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department also issued a legal opinion that the Postal Service could deliver \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs to states with bans without violating the Comstock Act, an 1873 law that prohibits mailing materials used in abortions. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have expressed interest in enforcing a strict interpretation of that law, which Ms. Harris's campaign has condemned.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump appointed the federal judge, Matthew Kacsmaryk, whose ruling last year voided the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone. An appeals court panel that was composed mainly of Trump appointees allowed it to stay on the market but upheld other parts of Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling that made the drug harder to obtain.\par \par \uc2 In June, the Supreme Court unanimously rejected the challenge to mifepristone, ruling that the plaintiffs did not have standing to bring the suit; anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have signaled that they may try again with different plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said during the presidential debate in June that he agreed with the Supreme Court's ruling, which he described as having ''approved the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.'' But because it was based on standing, the ruling did not address the merits of the case.\par \par \uc2 His campaign did not respond to requests to confirm whether he would support rescinding F.D.A. approval for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL FUNDING FOR \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris supported Mr. Biden's cancellation of the global and domestic ''gag rules,'' which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had used to cut off funding for groups that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services or referrals. When they were in effect under Mr. Trump, the rules blocked such groups from receiving foreign aid or domestic funding from Title X, a federal grant program that supports family planning for low-income Americans.\par \par \uc2 Versions of the global rule blocking certain foreign aid, called the Mexico City policy, have been enacted by every Republican president and rescinded by every Democratic one since the 1980s. Mr. Trump enacted the domestic rule for the first time since the Reagan administration.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris also opposes the Hyde Amendment, which bans Medicaid funding for most abortions. Her campaign did not confirm whether its inclusion in budgets would be a deal breaker for her; Mr. Biden also opposes the amendment but has signed budgets that include it rather than force a standoff with congressional Republicans over funding the government.\par \par \uc2 When he took office in 2017, Mr. Trump reinstated the so-called Mexico City policy, which blocked certain forms of foreign aid to organizations that provide abortions or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referrals.\par \par \uc2 Republican presidents have routinely done that, but Mr. Trump went further: He extended the policy to block the organizations from receiving not only family-planning funding but also broader health aid, including money for clean water, nutrition programs and H.I.V., malaria and tuberculosis prevention.\par \par \uc2 He placed similar restrictions on Title X funding for domestic organizations for the first time since the Reagan administration.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump supported an unsuccessful Senate effort to make permanent the Hyde Amendment, which bans Medicaid funding for most abortions.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16, A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240910ek9a0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
