\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Taking the Fight to Trump On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  at the Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Gold and Nicholas Nehamas \par \uc2 1,529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The candidates are all but certain to clash over the issue, which \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  sees as a major strength. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may try to avoid political blowback for his approach.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and their aides scheme out their strategies for this week's much-anticipated debate, one big question is how the two candidates will approach a great strength for Democrats and a major weakness for Republicans: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has made the issue a guiding light, and it has benefited her party at the polls since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022. Mr. Trump, who for years took credit for that decision, has tried to dodge its political blowback, making contradictory statements meant to help him appeal to voters turned off by total or near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in many Republican states.\par \par \uc2 The candidates are all but certain to clash over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday at the debate, the first time that they will directly confront each other and contrast their visions for the reproductive future of America.\par \par \uc2 A rising share of voters in battleground states, especially women, say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be critical to their decision in November, according to recent New York Times/Siena College polls. Roughly as many voters list \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their top issue as those who chose immigration, though the economy remains the leading priority for Americans. For women under 45, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranks as the most pressing concern. And those surveys also found that more voters said they trusted Ms. Harris over Mr. Trump on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is expected to lean into that advantage during the debate, drawing attention to Mr. Trump's role in appointing conservative Supreme Court justices who were instrumental in eliminating the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Harris's approach: '\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this'\par \par \uc2 In an effort to mobilize the Democratic base and sway some moderate Republican women and independents, the vice president has argued pointedly that the end of Roe and the resulting fallout are Mr. Trump's fault.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this,'' Ms. Harris often proclaims at her rallies, a line of attack she is likely to press on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 She frequently refers to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions enacted in 22 states since the fall of Roe as ''Trump \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.'' She holds him accountable for legal threats to in vitro fertilization and accuses him of wanting to ban mifepristone, a commonly prescribed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug. She also highlights the accounts of pregnant women who have suffered serious harm after being denied access to medical care, saying their stories represent a wider Republican assault on American freedoms.\par \par \uc2 Those were arguments that President Biden, an 81-year-old Catholic who has long been reluctant to even utter the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' failed to pursue at his debate against Mr. Trump in June. Ms. Harris is decades younger and talks about the issue with ease.\par \par \uc2 ''The fact that she's done this work over her entire career means that she understands it in a unique way,'' said Jessica Mackler, the president of Emily's List, a group that works to elect women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and has energetically backed Ms. Harris for years. ''She's long understood that this is deeply personal. It's intuitive for most voters. That's the reason it has so much power.''\par \par \uc2 How Trump has shifted on the issue\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is likely to seize on Mr. Trump's years of contortions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Since beginning his third campaign for the White House, the former president has been all over the map with his public and private comments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He has sought to persuade social conservatives and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists of his loyalty to their cause, while also trying not to lose support from moderates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, he won after promising that he would appoint Supreme Court justices who would overturn Roe. Now, both on the campaign trail and in interviews, he has frequently boasted about his role in appointing them. But in an effort to contain the political fallout, he has also falsely claimed that Democrats wanted to see Roe overturned as much as Republicans did.\par \par \uc2 After months of hedging, Mr. Trump said in the spring that he believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws should be left to the states, noting that he was ''proud'' of his role in ending Roe. But he has also repeatedly voiced opposition to a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban enacted in Florida, his adopted home state. And days after claiming that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were best left to the states, he denounced an Arizona court ruling that reinstated a 160-year-old ban and then successfully leaned on Republicans in the state to repeal it.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, Mr. Trump in just 24 hours embodied his contradictions on the issue. First, he suggested in an interview that he might support a Florida ballot measure that would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for up to 24 weeks, drawing the anger of social conservatives already frustrated by his attempts to distance himself from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.\par \par \uc2 The next day, he told Fox News that he would personally vote against the ballot measure, which would leave in place the same six-week ban that he has repeatedly insisted is too stringent.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Trump, responding to Democrats' attacks at their convention over his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said on social media that his administration would be ''great'' for women's ''\b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Asked how Mr. Trump might respond to criticisms of his positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Karoline Leavitt, a campaign spokeswoman, sent a statement that accused Ms. Harris of having a ''radical record'' and said that Mr. Trump looked ''forward to the opportunity to hold Kamala accountable for her dangerously liberal lies.''\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, Mr. Trump has accused Ms. Harris of being overly liberal on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falsely claiming that she and other Democrats support abortions ''after birth.'' No state law permits executing newborn children. He made the same claim during his debate against Mr. Biden in June.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden struggled to address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on that stage. Asked about it, he gave a muddled answer in which he tried to pivot to immigration and then gave an incorrect explanation of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Will the muted mics play a role?\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has emerged as a far more effective messenger on the issue. In March, she made the first official visit to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic by a president or vice president.\par \par \uc2 Still, some Democrats worry that the debate rules may hamper the ability of Ms. Harris, a former prosecutor, to go after Mr. Trump on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the candidates will have their microphones turned off when they are not speaking.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has had some of her strongest debate moments in sharp back-and-forth exchanges. In something of an attempt to lower expectations, her campaign has publicly expressed concerns that she will not be able to take full advantage of a powerful emotional issue if she cannot challenge and fact-check Mr. Trump in real time.\par \par \uc2 ''It is unfortunate that she won't be able to go after him with that sort of prosecutorial line of questioning to hold him accountable,'' Ian Sams, a senior spokesman for the Harris campaign, told MSNBC on Thursday. ''We know he's going to try to mislead the country about where he stands.''\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign had fought to have the mics on at all times but ultimately agreed to the original rules that Mr. Biden had secured for the first debate.\par \par \uc2 What's at stake in 2025 and beyond\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has promised that, if elected, she would push for a federal law that would enshrine the protections once guaranteed by Roe. But without a stunning series of Democratic victories in November, Congress would almost certainly be unable to pass such legislation.\par \par \uc2 She has also reached out to more moderate voters with some of her language from the stump, framing the issue as protecting a woman's personal liberty from government overreach.\par \par \uc2 ''One does not have to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree the government should not be telling her what to do with her body,'' Ms. Harris said at a recent campaign event.\par \par \uc2 Molly Murphy, a pollster for the Harris campaign, said that Americans, especially younger women and people of color, were responding to the message that Mr. Trump would go further in restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a second term.\par \par \uc2 The former president has signaled that he is open to letting states monitor the pregnancies of women and prosecute those who have abortions. And Project 2025 -- the conservative governing blueprint drawn up by allies of Mr. Trump that Democrats are trying to link to the former president -- contains detailed proposals for curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It reminds voters about how bad it's been,'' Ms. Murphy said. ''And it reminds them that this is very much at stake in this election.''\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik and Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik and Reid J. Epstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240909ek990003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
