\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 12 Pennsylvania Voters On Harris and Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Healy, Kristen Soltis Anderson and Adrian J. Rivera \par \uc2 2,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 No state is more coveted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than Pennsylvania, since their best strategies for an Electoral College victory hinge on winning it. With polls showing a tied race there, we spoke with 12 Pennsylvanians for our new Times Opinion focus group: voters who supported \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or Mr. Trump in 2020 and are not hard-core partisans. While the group leaned toward Ms. Harris, her support was pretty soft. Few of the participants could articulate a strong argument to vote for her on her merits. They are eager for a candidate to rally behind, given their worries about the country, especially the economy, but they haven't heard enough specifics from her about how she would lower costs. ''It feels like 'break my arm or break my leg,''' George, a 63-year-old 2020 Biden voter, said of his choices. He was open to considering Mr.\par \par \uc2  Trump but added: ''I just can't fathom voting for a felon who says on Day 1 that he's going to be a dictator and has a list of 5,000 people who are going to be replaced in the government. At the same time, I see how liberal she was and how she has flip-flopped. You want to talk about fracking in Pennsylvania? We're all from fracking.'' Those who liked Mr. Trump saw him as a businessman, while those who leaned against him thought of him as a child. That suggests that the Harris team may be onto something by making him seem small rather than a big threat. Ms. Harris is viewed as fairly unknown and untested. ''I don't feel like I've seen Harris throughout Biden's presidency. So she's just OK,'' said Lania, a 34-year-old 2020 Biden voter. Kay, a 61-year-old Biden voter, said Ms. Harris ''was almost nonexistent in the last four years.'' The risk to Ms. Harris is that voters like Lania and Kay won't be committed voters by Election Day, while the risk to Mr. Trump is that his history puts him beyond the pale, as George indicated. The economy and, to some degree, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  look like the driving issues in Pennsylvania, and Mr. Trump had the edge in the group on economic issues while Ms. Harris was way ahead on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What one word describes how you feel about the upcoming presidential election?\par \par \uc2 Lania: Scared.\par \par \uc2 Robert: Concerned.\par \par \uc2 David: Embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 Max: Anxious.\par \par \uc2 Myles: I have to say ''scared,'' too.\par \par \uc2 Dorris: Worried.\par \par \uc2 George: Conflicted.\par \par \uc2 Oscar: Uncertain.\par \par \uc2 Kay: Frustrated.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Kay, why ''frustrated''?\par \par \uc2 Kay: I feel about the same as I did in 2020. I don't have an affinity for either candidate. I'm frustrated that in the last four years, neither party could come up with someone more viable than what they're presenting.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Lania, why ''scared''?\par \par \uc2 Lania: Being a woman in a same-sex marriage, it kind of gets scary. A lot of people look at a vote for Trump as saying, ''I can say whatever I want to anybody on Earth.'' And my family and I, we've been running into people who just don't know what to say out their mouths most times. We have a 10-year-old, we have a 7-year-old, and we have a 7-monthold. How do you raise your children in the world that is coming up now? How can you teach your children to be respectful but also to defend themselves?\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Lania, what kinds of things do you hear people say?\par \par \uc2 Lania: I'm biracial. My wife's African American. And our children are all biracial. We have a child who asked one of our kids' friends, ''Why are you friends with a white kid?'' or ''Why are you friends with a Black kid?'' I had to deal with that growing up. And I thought the world was going back to a better place. We're trying to raise them to love everyone and love everyone for who they are and not what they look like, what color they are, what gender they want to be or whatever sexuality. But society is going backward.\par \par \uc2 Myles: I worry whether the election results will be accepted by both parties.\par \par \uc2 George: Neither candidate is viable. One is too liberal. The other is too conservative.\par \par \uc2 Max: The end can't come soon enough. You have a lot of folks who vote based on their perspective at a given moment in time. In 2016 my father-in-law's life was falling apart. He lost his job, tried to start different businesses that all failed. A lifelong Democrat, he was pushed hard to the right, and he was a superpro-Trump guy. Now, fast-forward to 2024, his business is on fire, making millions of dollars, and he's right back to being a liberal Democrat. And to some extent, I can support that.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Did anybody vote for Biden in 2020 who's now not quite sure if you'll vote for Harris this time around?\par \par \uc2 George: It feels like ''break my arm or break my leg.'' I voted for Biden because I think he's a great statesman, world-stage player. I felt that he wasn't dangerous to the U.S. economy. I just can't fathom voting for a felon who says on Day 1 that he's going to be a dictator and has a list of 5,000 people who are going to be replaced in the government. At the same time, I see how liberal she was and how she has flip-flopped. You want to talk about fracking in Pennsylvania? We're all from fracking. Are those just things that she's going to say to get elected? I don't have faith in her, either.\par \par \uc2 Kay: I voted for Biden because I thought he was a little bit more than the lesser of two evils. Harris, I just don't know enough about her, but I don't think Trump can string five words together to make a sentence. He's a global embarrassment. Everybody says if I don't vote, it's a vote for Trump. So I guess I have to vote. I'm not going to love it. I'm not going to enjoy it. I'm not going to have fun. There might be wine afterward.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: I want to go through a few issues that will probably be big factors for some voters. I want to hear what you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  believe on these issues, and who you think you agree with more. Let's start with immigration. How many of you think you agree more with Trump on the issue of immigration? [Six people raise a hand.] How many of you think you agree more with Harris on that issue? [One person raises a hand.] James, why do you prefer Harris to Trump on immigration? James: I just don't agree with Trump when it comes to anything. So no matter what her stance is, I'm going to agree with it more than Trump's.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: And what would you say her stance is on the issue?\par \par \uc2 James: She don't want to build no walls. She wants to keep them open.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: David, tell me why you side more with Trump on this issue.\par \par \uc2 David: There's good people, and there's bad people. And you could be a good immigrant or a bad immigrant, but the truth is, we have to take care of our people that are here. And you got to come in the right way. George: I went with Trump for two reasons. I think Trump was ruthless when he was president. But he didn't build much wall. Immigrants didn't come flocking by the thousands from South America because the perception was that he was tough on immigration, though. The perception with Biden and Harris is ''Come on, come on, come on.''\par \par \uc2 Moderator: On energy policy, how many agree more with Trump on the issue of energy? [Three people raise a hand.] How many agree more with Harris on this issue? [Four people raise a hand.]\par \par \uc2 Oscar: We were energy independent under Trump. I know that under Biden, he killed the pipeline deal. And if we have so much energy in the U.S., I think we should use it instead of depending on other countries for it.\par \par \uc2 Dorris: I'm with Trump on this issue because he would have never made a deal with Iran to take them off the top terrorist list, to do energy deals with them. And now Iran is supporting the war against Israel. Moderator: Bill, you were one of the people who put your hand up for Harris on this.\par \par \uc2 Bill: From a climate change perspective, I think Trump thinks we should forget about worrying about the environment, the climate. Drill here, drill there, drill everywhere -- he doesn't care. Harris is a little more holistic.\par \par \uc2 Max: I raised my hand for Harris. The U.S. still is energy independent. I just want to make sure everyone knows that. There are alternative energy sources, and we should be exploring all of them and not just catering to one specific industry that contributed the most to your coffers.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: The next issue I want to ask about is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On this issue, do you think you agree more with Trump or with Harris? [Two people raise a hand for Trump; eight people raise a hand for Harris.] Lania: When I was young, I was against abortions. The right was like, ''Oh, we're taking them away.'' It's not just elective abortions people are having. People are having abortions because they don't want to carry a child full term just for it to come out deceased.\par \par \uc2 Carrie: I 100 percent agree more with Harris because I don't believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and I 100 percent believe in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it was one of the best decisions of my life. And I feel like having that option is important for women and men in this country. Kay: Nobody on this planet is pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think it's the most personal decision any woman can make. But for me, I was born in the '60s, and women just a little bit older than me fought for the right for a woman to decide what was proper for herself and her own body. Nobody else should own that decision. Oscar: This is the only issue that I side with Harris on. I think it should be left up to the woman to decide. It's a personal choice. That's all.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Dorris, you said you lean more toward Trump on this.\par \par \uc2 Dorris: Because I watched a personal interview with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and he said he would leave it up to each state. He didn't say he was against it. If you had a chance to give advice to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about what each should be doing to reach or talk to a voter like you, what advice would you give?\par \par \uc2 George:I would tell Harris to listen to people. Stop going to Philadelphia, stop going to Pittsburgh. Visit the middle of the state. Visit little towns, talk to people, find out what people want. I think she's disconnected from the average worker, if you want to call us that.\par \par \uc2 Robert: Trump doesn't need any advice from me. He knows what he's doing. And he's said it himself: Fight, fight, fight.\par \par \uc2 Lania: Trump, stop trying to make divisions between people. Just let everybody have their own opinions. Harris, just come out more. Come out of your shell. Bring the energy that everyone knows you have. Just bring the positivity that this world really needs.\par \par \uc2 Kay: I want Harris to be more specific about what you can do for me. We've gotten into this habit of mudslinging. It used to be that campaigns were more about what a candidate could do for you, what they could offer. So stick to the basics.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: How many of you think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will win in November? [Two people raise a hand.] How many of you think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will win in November? [Seven people raise a hand.] Dorris, you said you'd want to vote for Trump if it were today, but it sounds like you don't think he's going to win in November. Why?\par \par \uc2 Dorris: I'm going to vote for Trump, but I'm not sure who will win. It's a tossup. Trump says the wrong thing a lot. My concern is that he's going to say something before the election that make people not want to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Oscar, you also said you'd vote for Trump if it were today, but you didn't think he would necessarily win. Oscar: I think Trump will be the last G.O.P. president we've had. The country is going far left, and that's why I think Harris will win.\par \par \uc2 Moderator:Is there anyone who plans to vote for Harris or who would vote for Harris if the election were today but you're pretty iffy on whether you think she'll win in November?\par \par \uc2 Carrie:I feel like it's going to be recounts done multiple times across multiple states.\par \par \uc2 Bill: I'm not confident she would win. I feel like it's very close, and I think we're going to be in one of these situations where we don't know for a few days.\par \par \uc2 Myles: I agree with what Bill said. I think the debate coming up on Sept. 10 is going to be really important. Would you be more enthusiastic about voting for Harris if she'd picked Josh Shapiro?\par \par \uc2 Kay: Oh, I'd be all in if she picked Josh.\par \par \uc2 George: Oh, yeah. Walz is too liberal. He's also too old. Shapiro is more of a moderate. He's up and coming. He brings a lot of enthusiasm. He turned the United States on its ear when I-95 burned down and he had engineers telling him it was going to be six months. And he said, horse manure, and got it done in days.\par \par \uc2 Max: I agree with almost everything George said. I'm voting for Harris regardless because I'm a Never Trump guy. But it's a vote against Trump. It's not a vote for Harris. If Shapiro were on the ticket, it would be an incredibly easy decision, and it would pull a lot of moderate Republicans over the edge, I think.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: OK. Last question. How confident are you that the election results will accurately reflect the will of American voters?\par \par \uc2 Lania:I honestly think that it's always rigged. They already have who they're going to pick. It doesn't matter what votes. They can tally them up for 20 days, and they're still going to have whoever they have in their mind-set that they're going to make the president.\par \par \uc2 Moderator: Lania, who's ''they''?\par \par \uc2 Lania: Lord knows. I'm not even sure. But I know whoever is tallying these votes, they're throwing them in the trash, whatever ones that come in through the mail, I don't even think that somebody is looking at those votes.\par \par \uc2 George:I'm an I.T. guy. I understand technology. I dug a lot into the claims of election interference in the last election, with regard to the electronic machines. And I'm convinced that there is a safe chain of custody there. I'm also convinced that there's no squad of poll workers out there that are throwing things in the dumpster. There's no people in Philadelphia at the vote-counting facility that are throwing things out. I truly believe that votes are accounted for.\par \par \uc2 Myles:I agree with what George said, but I'm more confident. All of the lawsuits that were brought about after 2020 were thrown out. I feel confident in our system, and I've always felt that way. Our elections are fair and accurate.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page SR9. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240908ek980006i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
