\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Missouri Judge Rules Against Ballot Measure on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups hope an appeals court will reverse the ruling before the Tuesday deadline to print the state ballots.\par \par \uc2 A Missouri court late Friday moved toward striking a ballot measure in November that would ask voters whether to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Judge Christopher Limbaugh of Cole County Circuit Court sided with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and activists who said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups that gathered signatures to sponsor the ballot measure had not sufficiently explained its potential ramifications on the petitions they asked voters to sign.\par \par \uc2 With the state scheduled to print ballots on Tuesday, the judge said he would wait until then to issue an injunction instructing the secretary of state to remove the measure that was certified last month. That will give the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups a chance to appeal to a higher court.\par \par \uc2 The coalition behind the measure vowed to do so immediately, calling the ruling ''a profound injustice to the initiative process.'' They have expressed optimism that the appeals court will be more sympathetic to their arguments.\par \par \uc2 That court could decline to act, in which case the measure would be struck from the ballot. But if the appeals court or the state Supreme Court overturns Judge Limbaugh's ruling before Tuesday, he will not issue an injunction, and the measure, known as Amendment 3, will remain on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 ''Our fight to ensure that voters -- not politicians -- have the final say is far from over,'' Rachel Sweet, the campaign manager for Missourians for Constitutional Freedom, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Missouri is one of 10 states where state officials have certified similar ballot measures sponsored by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups to appear in November. The groups have been on a winning streak: Voters have already sided with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in all seven ballot measures that have appeared since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022, including measures in red states such as Kansas and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Missouri and South Dakota, however, are the first states where a yes vote would overturn a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Missouri was the first state to officially ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Roe fell, prohibiting it except to save the life of a pregnant woman. Supporters of the ballot amendment broke state records for the number of signatures collected, filing 380,159, more than twice the number required to place it before voters.\par \par \uc2 With polling suggesting that voters support the ballot measures across the country, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and lawmakers in Missouri and elsewhere have campaigned hard to try to keep the measures from getting on the ballot, proposing legislation that would make it harder to collect signatures or pass the measures, and encouraging voters who signed the petition to remove their names.\par \par \uc2 Now that state officials have approved the measures to appear on the ballot, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have sued to try to strike them.\par \par \uc2 The case before Judge Limbaugh -- a former general counsel to Gov. \uc2 Mike Parson\uc2 , a Republican, who appointed him, and a cousin of \uc2 Rush Limbaugh\uc2 , the conservative radio host who died in 2021 -- hinged on a requirement in the Missouri Constitution that requires that each initiative petition include ''an enacting clause and the full text of the measure,'' and identify all sections of the law or the Constitution that would be repealed if the amendment were passed.\par \par \uc2 The petitions that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups circulated included the full text of the measure. But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists contended that the amendment's language protecting ''a person's fundamental right to reproductive freedom'' could have far-reaching effects that the petitions did not mention. Their lawyer argued that the amendment -- which would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the fetus is viable outside the uterus, around 24 weeks, but permit the state to regulate it after that -- could affect state laws that ban minors from getting gender-affirming health care, and prohibit human cloning.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups said that it would be impossible to know what laws might be affected until those laws were challenged in court cases if the amendment passed.\par \par \uc2 But Judge Limbaugh said the petitions had failed to include mention of any laws the amendment might affect, calling this a ''blatant violation'' of the requirements.\par \par \uc2 In a statement late Friday, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and lawmakers who filed the suit seeking to strike the ballot measure said, ''There is no way to know if the proponents of this radical amendment would have gathered enough signatures to place this on the ballot if the truth about the staggering scope of laws Amendment 3 invalidates had been disclosed.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have sued to try to remove similar measures from the ballots in other states. In Arizona, the state's Supreme Court rejected that effort, ruling that signatures had been collected properly. On Monday, the Nebraska Supreme Court will consider a similar challenge there. A trial over whether to strike the measure from the ballot in South Dakota is scheduled to start at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who control state government in Missouri have worked hard over the last 18 months to defeat the prospective amendment. On Thursday, a separate court ruled against them in another case, saying that the language describing the ballot amendment that Secretary of State \uc2 Jay Ashcroft\uc2  had approved to appear on summaries posted at polling places was ''unfair, insufficient, inaccurate and misleading'' and ''gives voters the wrong idea of what the amendment will accomplish.''\par \par \uc2 The summary approved by Mr. Ashcroft, a Republican opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had stated that a ''yes'' vote would, among other things, ''enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time of pregnancy in the Missouri Constitution'' and ''prohibit any regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including regulations designed to protect women undergoing abortions.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had sued, noting that, like those proposed in almost every other state, the amendment would allow Missouri to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability.\par \par \uc2 Residents in February gathered signatures for a petition in support of a ballot initiative to end Missouri's near-total ban on \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY ED ZURGA/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A22. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240908ek980005c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip