\par\par \uc2 NONFICTION\par \uc2 Book Review Desk; SECTBR\par \b \uc2 Fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joanna Biggs \par \uc2 935 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 24\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In 1969, Honor Moore was granted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a Connecticut psychiatrist, and went on with her life. In 2024, she reckons with the fallout.\par \par \uc2 A TERMINATION, by Honor Moore\par \par \uc2 When Honor Moore was a 23-year-old graduate student, she had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was April 1969, and the state of Connecticut allowed a woman to end her pregnancy if a doctor determined that giving birth would threaten the life of the mother. During an interview with a psychiatrist in a toy-strewn office in New Haven, Moore said she would go crazy if she had a baby, and the psychiatrist gave her a letter permitting an obstetrician to carry out the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Waking up in the hospital recovery room, she found a bouquet of flowers sent by friends and a furious doctor -- he'd instructed her not to tell anyone.\par \par \uc2 Her friends later visited anyway, bringing her a sandwich. And Moore continued her studies, spending the summer in the Berkshires as press agent for a glitzy play.\par \par \uc2 There are details Moore doesn't remember -- did the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cost $2,000? Did her gynecologist's name begin with a ''B''? She can't be sure -- because she didn't begin writing ''A Termination,'' her slim, searching memoir of her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its effects, until she was in her 70s.\par \par \uc2 In May 2022, when it became clear that American women would no longer be trusted with decisions about their pregnancies, Moore finished the book in a fever. What had her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant to her? At 23, Moore did not know that she would go on to write six highly praised books of poetry, memoir and biography.\par \par \uc2 She had graduated from Harvard, worked in theater for a summer and won a place at Yale Drama School. She wanted to write, and although no one was stopping her, she had yet to begin.\par \par \uc2 Her mother, who had given birth nine times, sent her into the world with one instruction: Don't come home pregnant. Her father, Paul Moore, the progressive Episcopal bishop in Washington and eventually New York, turned up monthly to take her to the movies but remained aloof. Perhaps she knew his views already: not in favor, but maintaining the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a ''decision of conscience.''\par \par \uc2 When Moore did become pregnant, she realized that however she counted the days she could not be sure whether the father was the photographer with whom she'd ''had sex that I didn't want'' or the professor she was in love with, who said he would have married her when he later learned of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She describes the situation as ''a knot from which there are so many exits, there is none.'' In not wanting the photographer's child, she cannot have the professor's child. In wanting her imagined future life, she can't wholly abandon her former life. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is revealed as a problem of love, born of sex.\par \par \uc2 A month before Moore terminated her pregnancy, a group of radical feminists calling themselves Redstockings gathered in Washington Square Methodist Church for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  speak-out. The idea was to talk about a common -- but illegal -- experience openly. Four years afterward, the Supreme Court finally accepted that the Constitution protects a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Moore didn't understand what she did as political in 1969, and she wasn't yet a feminist. ''I didn't have a self,'' she writes of the 20-something Honor, and she comes to think of her decision to terminate part of her life and begin another as the first autonomous choice she ever made.\par \par \uc2 Feminism doesn't create a demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or turn it into contraception. (In fact, young Honor didn't take her pill consistently, and her desire not to become a mother at 23 is a twin of her desire to sleep with two men.)\par \par \uc2 Rather, feminism allows a right to be applied equally across race, class and geography, something the women of Alabama, Arkansas, Florida, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Iowa, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, Missouri, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Carolina, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas and West Virginia were forced to learn again in 2022.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to ''Happening,'' Annie Ernaux's slim memoir of a back-alley \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1963 (and the basis of a movie by the same name), the tone of ''A Termination'' is hot and the chronology looping. Rather than a call to arms, or even a lesson from history, Moore's memoir depicts her knot gradually and incompletely untangling over 50 years.\par \par \uc2 Her emotions remain mixed: shame, fear of exposure, sadness and a sense of freedom. In her head the aborted fetus has grown into an adult man who asks her why she didn't have him. But both Moore's and Ernaux's experiences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  show that being able to end a pregnancy can be a necessary condition of women's flourishing.\par \par \uc2 Moore remembers reciting her ''matrilineage'' in a circle with a women's theater group in the 1970s: ''I am Honor, daughter of Jenny, granddaughter of Fanny and Margarett,'' she declared. Moore wrote a biography of Margarett (the painter Margarett Sargent) and a memoir of Jenny, but she has not become a biological mother herself. After she recounted her inheritance, everyone in the circle recited the same sentence: ''I am a woman giving birth to myself.''\par \par \uc2 ''A Termination'' reveals what hard work that birth is.\par \par \uc2 A TERMINATION | By Honor Moore | A Public Space | 132 pp. | Paperback, $20\par \par \uc2 Honor Moore (PHOTOGRAPH BY SPENCER OSTRANDER) This article appeared in print on page BR24. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240908ek9800052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
