\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Senate Democrats Betting On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Boost\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The party's candidates are likely to benefit from running alongside ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But the deeply conservative nature of many of these states poses a serious challenge.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2  revved up the Democratic faithful in a restaurant named for Thomas Edison, she was looking for a little electricity in her campaign to unseat Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida.\par \par \uc2 So she turned the attention of the crowd at Edison's Lab Restaurant and Bar in Fort Myers, where the inventor had a winter estate and laboratory, to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum in Florida that could decide the fate of her bid.\par \par \uc2 ''The man has never run in a presidential election when our voters turn out,'' she said of Mr. Scott. Perhaps more important, she added to the boisterous crowd, ''he has never run against the millions of women and men that are ready to come out to the ballot box to protect reproductive freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mucarsel-Powell, a former medical school dean who was swept into the U.S. House by the 2018 blue wave, and then swept right back out two years later, is an underdog. Mr. Scott, a wealthy fixture of Florida politics who served for eight years as governor, has the high ground in the red-tinted state as he seeks his second term in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But she has one thing on her side: Floridians will be deciding by referendum whether to overturn the state's unpopular six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Beyond Florida, Senate candidates from across the political spectrum in Missouri, Montana, Nevada, Nebraska, Maryland and Arizona will appear on the ballot alongside measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The track record of such measures, which have passed everywhere they have been introduced since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, would seem to bode well for candidates who support them.\par \par \uc2 Yet on a brutal Senate map for Democrats, their candidates in conservative states, like Ms. Mucarsel-Powell and Lucas Kunce in Missouri, remain long shots. Even incumbent Democrats like Senators \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  of Montana and \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  of Nevada have not been able to establish clear leads on the back of their states' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums.\par \par \uc2 In a presidential election, there is just so much else diluting the impact.\par \par \uc2 ''We know these ballot amendments do motivate a lot of voters,'' cautioned Angela Kuefler, a longtime Democratic pollster who has worked on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives. ''We don't know what that looks like in a presidential year -- 2024 will be the great experiment.''\par \par \uc2 Mike Berg, a spokesman for the National Republican Senatorial Committee, said that Democrats' latching of their candidacies to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums would not work because other issues were more important. ''Groceries are unaffordable, violent crime is out of control, and Democrats encourage a full-blown invasion of our southern border,'' he said, using hyperbole on immigration politics that is typical of the Trump era.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mucarsel-Powell readily concedes that some Floridians will vote for Mr. Scott on the same ballot that they support Amendment Four, the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point of fetal viability, as well as Amendment Three, a referendum to legalize marijuana. (Mr. Scott opposes both.) Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  has been inconsistent, suggesting recently that he might vote against Florida's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and then saying that he would oppose the referendum to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to make sure that they don't go out and just put the yes on Three and yes on Four,'' Ms. Mucarsel-Powell said in an interview. ''They need to make sure that they also fill out the candidates that are going to support it, and that's been a problem before. I do realize that.''\par \par \uc2 But Chris Hartline, a spokesman for the Scott campaign, argued that many Florida Republicans had a small-government, libertarian streak. They will feel more than free to vote to keep government out of reproductive issues and drug control, he said, but that will not make them Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The same could be said in other Republican states like Montana, Missouri and Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Democrats involved in the campaigns say that, at most, the referendums could increase their vote share by two to three percentage points. That would potentially be enough to put some candidates over the top -- think Mr. Tester -- but not enough for Democrats in deeper holes, like Mr. Kunce, a Marine veteran who is challenging Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  in solidly red Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Like the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights referendums in other Republican states, Missouri's measure has a good chance of passing. But Mr. Hawley, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is heavily favored to beat Mr. Kunce, who supports them.\par \par \uc2 To keep their majority, Democrats must prevail in every race in which they are defending seats, in Maryland, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Montana, Arizona and Nevada -- and must also win the presidency to keep the tiebreaking vote in a 50-50 Senate.\par \par \uc2 If they could defeat at least one Republican incumbent, they would gain some wiggle room, but their targets are in states expected to vote for Mr. Trump: Florida, Texas, Missouri and Nebraska. (In Nebraska, Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 , a Republican, is facing a surprisingly tough challenge from an independent union organizer, Dan Osborn.)\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mucarsel-Powell, like Mr. Kunce, has argued that the Republican she hopes to knock out of office is vulnerable -- awkward, unpopular and yoked to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Mr. Scott won his two terms as governor and his Senate seat by extremely narrow margins -- never more than 1.2 percentage points -- and only after he spent about $160 million of his own money over 14 years.\par \par \uc2 That personal wallet remains large. Mr. Scott, whose health care empire was found liable for the largest Medicare fraud in history (he was not criminally charged), remains among the richest members of Congress, and Democratic leaders in Washington expect him to dip into his pocket again.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Washington see the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum less as an issue that can put Ms. Mucarsel-Powell over the top and more as a rallying cry to draw national money from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups into the state to counter Mr. Scott's wealth.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's campaign and the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  are taking notice. On Tuesday, the Harris campaign began a bus tour for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Palm Beach County, not far from Mr. Trump's seaside mansion, Mar-a-Lago. The party chairman, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , and Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota joined for the kickoff, as did Ms. Mucarsel-Powell.\par \par \uc2 ''He calls it the winter White House,'' Ms. Klobuchar said of Mar-a-Lago. ''I call it his retirement home. He is the one who got us in this mess.''\par \par \uc2 Day 2 on Wednesday was in Jacksonville, but Ms. Mucarsel-Powell and Mr. Harrison peeled off to Fort Myers.\par \par \uc2 ''The fact that they're here, in Lee County, means they aren't counting anybody out,'' said Jim Rosinus, the chairman of the Democratic Party of Lee County, a heavily Republican area that includes Fort Myers. ''Florida is in play.''\par \par \uc2 Yet seasoned Democratic strategists are leery of putting too much hope in the Florida referendums to help the party's candidates. For one thing, because referendums in the state need 60 percent of the vote to pass, their proponents have tried to steer away from partisan politics, knowing that the support of Democrats, Republicans and independents will be required.\par \par \uc2 For another, ''it's that tribalism thing, my team right or wrong,'' said Kari Lerner, a former state legislator in New Hampshire who returned to her native Fort Myers and is now running against Representative Byron Donalds, the Republican who represents the area.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, Mr. Tester's campaign pointed to a past statement by his Republican opponent, \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2 , that he wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''all to end tomorrow'' and Mr. Sheehy's opposition to the state's referendum to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But Mr. Tester has not made the referendum, which he supports, the centerpiece of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 Other Democratic candidates for Senate, like Representative \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  in Arizona, have made their states' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums one of many top issues and have generally emphasized that Democratic control of the chamber would stave off a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, where two competing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums have muddied the issue, Mr. Osborn is trying hard not to affiliate with either major party as he challenges Ms. Fischer. He has declined to endorse either referendum, one of which would protect legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the point of fetal viability, while the other would limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 ''Everywhere I go, I hear from Nebraskans who agree that it's not the job of a politician to promote or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Mr. Osborn said in a statement. His aides said he would vote for the referendum protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Underdog Democrats are all in. In Missouri, Mr. Kunce's campaign pointed to Kentucky, a Republican state where 52 percent of voters rejected an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum in 2022 and where a Democratic governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , won re-election in 2023. In Michigan that year, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure helped Democrats as Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  won re-election and her party took complete control of the Statehouse.\par \par \uc2 Roy Temple, a former Democratic Party chairman in Missouri, declared that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum ''fundamentally alters the political landscape in Missouri.''\par \par \uc2 But Democrats in Washington are looking elsewhere for long-shot Senate pickups, especially the Texas contest between Senator Ted Cruz, a Republican, and Representative \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Ms. Mucarsel-Powell's pitch goes far beyond her support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum. A native of Ecuador, she is the only Latina Democrat running for the Senate this cycle. She hails from Dade County, in South Florida, where the erosion of Democratic support in 2016 helped give Mr. Trump his victory and ushered in the idea that Florida is no longer a swing state.\par \par \uc2 And this is the first election cycle for Mr. Scott since he wrote his own conservative policy manifesto, renounced by his own Republican leadership, that included a proposal to sunset Medicare and Social Security, which many older Floridians depend on.\par \par \uc2 Yet Ms. Mucarsel-Powell's pitch always seems to circle back to the referendums, especially the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  one, and the hope that Mr. Scott will finally face his Waterloo.\par \par \uc2 ''People are really motivated to change Florida,'' she said. ''With women coming out to vote, with these two amendments bringing them out, with independents being really engaged, that's going to cross the line.''\par \par \uc2 John Pacenti contributed reporting from Palm Beach County, Fla.\par \par \uc2 John Pacenti contributed reporting from Palm Beach County, Fla.\par \par \uc2 A campaign event this week in Ft. Myers, Fla., for Debbie Mucarsel-Powell, who is challenging Senator Rick Scott, a Republican.; Ms. Mucarsel-Powell is one of several Democratic Senate candidates in red states who hope that \uc2  referendums will help. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ZACK WITTMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240907ek970000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
