\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Deal A Setback To Oklahoma Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Oklahoma asked the justices to weigh in after a lower federal court ruled the state was not entitled to millions of dollars in federal family-planning grants after it refused to refer patients to a national pregnancy hotline.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Tuesday turned down a request from Oklahoma to restore millions in federal family-planning grants that the Biden administration withheld after the state announced that it would no longer provide access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counseling services.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma had sought emergency relief after a divided three-judge panel of an appeals court in July temporarily paused the funding as a lower-court dispute played out over whether state officials could refuse to refer pregnant women to counseling services that presented \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an option.\par \par \uc2 The court's brief, unsigned order gave no reasons, as is common when it acts on emergency applications. Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  and \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  dissented, saying they would have reinstated the grants.\par \par \uc2 The case was the latest instance in which the justices have addressed a dispute over restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after they overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022. Since then, 22 states, including Oklahoma, have either banned or limited access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The dispute centers on when states should receive federal grants focused on family planning. In establishing the program in 1970, Congress directed the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to lay out eligibility requirements. Among them: Applicants must offer counseling and referrals for all manner of issues, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma's health department was among the grant recipients for 2022.\par \par \uc2 But after the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2  told grant recipients that they must continue to offer counseling and referrals for all options, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Months later, Oklahoma raised concerns about the stipulation, arguing that it stood at odds with its strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 The federal agency responded by suggesting that Oklahoma provide such referrals through a national call-in phone line.\par \par \uc2 Even as the state accepted the grant in March 2023, agreeing to disclose the call-in number as a condition, shortly after, it ''decided to stop sharing'' that information, the appeals panel wrote.\par \par \uc2 The agency then terminated the grant.\par \par \uc2 The state challenged the decision, asking a federal judge to temporarily force the government to provide the funds. When the judge ruled against Oklahoma, the state appealed to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the 10th Circuit, in Denver.\par \par \uc2 The state presented three arguments, saying that for one, Congress's spending power did not allow it to delegate the grant eligibility requirements to the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 . It added that the requirements violated a federal law known as the Weldon Amendment, which prohibits a federal or state agency from distributing money if it discriminates against a ''health care entity'' that does not provide or pay for abortions. Finally, the state argued that the federal agency had acted arbitrarily and capriciously.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the appeals panel rejected all three arguments, saying that the agency had acted in its power and that Oklahoma had ''likely acted knowingly and voluntarily'' when it accepted the terms of the grant.\par \par \uc2 The panel added that the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 ' proposal that Oklahoma provide a national call-in number was not the same as referring someone for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In asking the Supreme Court to intervene, Oklahoma called the appeals court ruling ''unfortunate,'' arguing that it ignored Supreme Court precedent on the congressional spending power and the language of the Weldon Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Oklahoma argued that withholding grant funding would be ''devastating'' to the state because the money offered critical public health services in about 70 city and county health departments across Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 ''In many instances, particularly in rural Oklahoma, the county health department is one of the only access points for critical preventative services for tens or even hundreds of miles,'' they wrote in their brief.\par \par \uc2 Adam Liptak contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Adam Liptak contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240904ek940003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
