\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In North Carolina, the Math for a Supermajority May Come Down to 1\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 1,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's one of several states where legislators of one party have overridden vetoes from a governor of the opposing party. Several tossup races will determine if that continues.\par \par \uc2 This fall, as Democrats and Republicans vie for control of state legislatures across the country, much of the attention has focused on states like Michigan, Pennsylvania and Arizona, where one or two seats could tip the balance.\par \par \uc2 But in a handful of states, the legislature is dominated by one party, while the governor's office is held by another. In those states, a high-stakes effort is underway to either preserve an existing supermajority -- which confers the ability to override a governor's veto -- or to break it.\par \par \uc2 Nowhere has the battle been more magnified than in North Carolina, a heavily gerrymandered state where Republicans hold the 60 percent minimum required for an override in both chambers, even though registered Democratic voters outnumber Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Last year, after State Representative Tricia Cotham unexpectedly switched her party affiliation from Democrat to Republican, Republican leaders were able to enact a 12-week limit on most abortions, overriding Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republican legislators cannot afford to lose a single seat, in either chamber, if they want to continue to override his vetoes.\par \par \uc2 ''Remember, one legislator delivered the supermajority,'' Beth Helfrich, a Democrat running for the state House of Representatives, said during a recent town hall in Davidson, N.C. ''My math says one can break it.''\par \par \uc2 Similar situations are playing out in other states.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas, a Democrat, has vetoed bills on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , criminal justice and other issues, only to be overridden by the Republicans' two-thirds supermajority in that state's Legislature. Democrats need to net two seats in the House, or three in the Senate, to break that pattern.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, Gov. Joe Lombardo, a Republican, vetoed a record 75 bills in 2023, frustrating Democrats, who have a two-thirds supermajority in the House, but are one short in the Senate. Republicans do not want to lose that seat.\par \par \uc2 Republicans with their supermajority in North Carolina have been able to loosen campaign finance rules, strip the governor's power to appoint members to state boards and block efforts to update energy efficiency rules for new homes, all over Mr. Cooper's objections.\par \par \uc2 To preserve that advantage, Republicans there are spending $5.3 million on television ads to bolster candidates in 10 House districts, including Ms. Cotham's, where the Democratic nominee, Nicole Sidman, a director at a Charlotte synagogue, has outraised Ms. Cotham by 4 to 1. At the national level, the Republican State Leadership Committee, pinpointing North Carolina as among the states where Democrats want to ''dismantle Republican supermajorities,'' are investing a record $38 million in state legislative races this year.\par \par \uc2 On the flip side, The States Project, a Democratic-aligned group, is spending $70 million on legislative races in nine states, including ones for several House seats in North Carolina. The Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee is highlighting 14 North Carolina House and Senate races as part of a $10 million push to convince voters of the importance of controlling state legislatures, said Jeremy Jansen, the group's political director.\par \par \uc2 ''We have an opportunity here in North Carolina to be able to give power back, or at least give some semblance of a balance of power back, by giving the veto pen to the governor,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 One notable tossup, according to an analysis of partisan leanings by the \uc2 John Locke Foundation\uc2 , a conservative think tank, is the 25th district in Nash County, a rural and working-class area east of Raleigh, where State Representative Allen Chesser, a first-term Republican, is being challenged by Lorenza M. Wilkins, an executive at an anti-hunger nonprofit.\par \par \uc2 That is also Mr. Cooper's home district. Brenda Brown, the Republican mayor of Nashville, who lives around the corner from where the governor grew up, gushed about the Coopers: ''Just good people.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Ms. Brown, a retired school principal, enthusiastically endorsed Mr. Chesser, saying he had notched vital grants, such as $5 million in state funding to modernize the county jail.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chesser, 39, joined the Army after Sept. 11 and worked as a police officer before working in security and technology. A former high school soccer player, he co-founded a Christian youth soccer league with his wife.\par \par \uc2 ''I played the position of sweeper -- the very last defender,'' he said over breakfast at a restaurant in Middlesex. ''That's kind of my political philosophy as well. I am a true believer in individual rights and freedoms. Limited government in the truest sense.''\par \par \uc2 He sponsored a bill allowing people with concealed carry permits to bring handguns into churches that also serve as schools. Calling himself ''100 percent pro-life,'' he voted to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and give parents more control over school curriculums.\par \par \uc2 Two of his bills were signed by Mr. Cooper: one enabling patients with rare diseases to receive experimental drugs or medical treatments not approved by the Food and Drug Administration, and another easing the rules for families to adopt foster children.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm a bit of a unicorn,'' said Mr. Chesser, who defeated a two-term Democratic incumbent in 2022. ''I never campaigned on bringing the supermajority to Nash County.''\par \par \uc2 But Owen H. Strickland II, mayor of Bailey, population 564, said that preserving the Republican supermajority was ''vitally important,'' since Democrats are favored to retain the governorship.\par \par \uc2 ''Whether it's $5 or $5 million for a local bill to help us with whatever, I have to be able to pick up the phone and say, 'Hey, I need this done,''' said Mr. Strickland, a former Democrat. ''The only way it's going to get done is if you have a supermajority.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chesser's challenger, Dr. Wilkins, 51, holds a doctorate in business administration and boasts a wide-ranging r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 including stints as a locomotive engineer and human resources manager.\par \par \uc2 In an interview at the Nash County Democratic headquarters in Rocky Mount, Dr. Wilkins said his top priority was public education. Unlike Mr. Chesser, he is leery of North Carolina Opportunity Scholarships, which provide state-funded vouchers for private schools. He also sounded the alarm on Project 2025, a package of conservative proposals.\par \par \uc2 ''What's within that document will start to filter down to the state level,'' he said. ''You want to remove the Department of Education. What is that going to do to impoverished kids and school districts?''\par \par \uc2 A graduate of \uc2 North Carolina Central University\uc2 , a historically Black school, and a resident of a district that is 41 percent Black and 8 percent Hispanic, Dr. Wilkins worries about a supermajority bent on gutting diversity, equity and inclusion initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Wilkins has been endorsed by state and national labor, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and environmental groups -- as has Ms. Helfrich, a former English teacher and school administrator who is running in the 98th district, an affluent and highly educated pocket north of Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 The seat in the 98th district is held by Representative John R. Bradford III, one of several moderate Republicans who ignored Mr. Cooper's entreaties to break with his caucus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He turned his sights on Congress, but lost in the Republican primary, and Melinda Bales, a former mayor of Huntersville, is the Republican pick for his old seat.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Helfrich has raised $243,000 -- versus $91,000 for Ms. Bales -- with nearly 40 percent of her donors living outside the state.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Helfrich has stressed that gerrymandered maps and politically motivated redistricting in North Carolina and other states damage democracy. She is also tapping into anger over the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Indeed, Ms. Cotham's mother was trounced unexpectedly in a Democratic primary for re-election to the Mecklenburg County Board of Commissioners, in what was widely viewed as a proxy vote against her daughter.\par \par \uc2 In an interview at the home of her father, a retired theater professor at \uc2 Davidson College\uc2 , Ms. Helfrich recounted listening to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  override vote. She has five children, but has also experienced miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 ''I think a lot of people end up asking the question, what can I do?'' said Ms. Helfrich, 43. ''And lo and behold, what I can do is put together a coalition and build enough momentum in this one district.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bales declined to be interviewed.\par \par \uc2 A native of Eastern Tennessee, Ms. Bales, 54, logged 12 years as an elected commissioner and mayor in fast-growing Huntersville. Her platform centers on education, transportation and economic development.\par \par \uc2 Within a half-mile of her house, few residents said they had heard of Ms. Bales.\par \par \uc2 Betsy Neumann, 39, who described herself as being in the middle, politically, said she worried about Republicans continuing to ''take freedoms away from other people,'' citing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 But Dawn Gilpatrick, 60, who described herself as ''a pretty strong Republican,'' praised Ms. Bales's efforts to bring more companies to Huntersville to increase the tax base. She wants Ms. Bales to join Republicans in pushing for voter ID restrictions and school vouchers.\par \par \uc2 At a hot dog picnic in Mint Hill, hosted by the Mecklenburg County Republican Party, Ken May, the founding director of the Hornet's Nest Men's Republican Club, praised Ms. Bales as ''not fiery, not blustery. She works across the aisle and is super friendly.''\par \par \uc2 Then Mr. May excused himself to introduce one of several local and statewide candidates in attendance: Ms. Cotham.\par \par \uc2 Wearing a red dress to match a sea of MAGA hats, Ms. Cotham noted that while this was her seventh campaign, ''this is my first time running as a Republican, and I am so excited!''\par \par \uc2 She vowed to tackle crime and beef up the economy. Then she mentioned Governor Cooper.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a glorious day when you get to override his veto,'' she beamed.\par \par \uc2 Allen Chesser, a state representative running for re-election, said, ''I am a true believer in individual rights and freedoms.''; Brenda Brown, the Republican mayor of Nashville and retired school principal, endorsed Mr. Chesser in the tossup race.; Lorenza M. Wilkins, an executive at an anti-hunger nonprofit group, is the Democratic candidate challenging Mr. Chesser.; Beth Helfrich, a Democrat running for the North Carolina House, campaigning in Huntersville.; State Representative Tricia Cotham, center, switched her affiliation from Democrat to Republican. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CORNELL WATSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in 