\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Rural Voters In North Carolina May Be Deciders\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eduardo Medina, Emily Rhyne, Noah Throop/the New York Times, Mark Boyer and Shuran Huang \par \uc2 1,164 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Residents in Wilson County worry about the rising costs of goods and housing. But mostly they just want to get along.\par \par \uc2 The most rural of the battleground states this year is North Carolina. About 3.4 million people, or roughly a third of the state's population, reside in a rural area, more than in any other state besides Texas.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have seen their support slip in rural areas, ceding ground to Republicans. As such, rural voters in North Carolina could determine which way the state goes on Election Day, as Democrats hope to curb their losses in these communities and Republicans seek to solidify their grip.\par \par \uc2 But in interviews with more than 30 people in Wilson County, about 50 miles east of Raleigh, where backcountry roads weave in and out of tobacco fields, many residents told us that they felt both parties often overlooked their concerns, about high prices, underfunded schools and rapid growth from the state capital that is stretching into town.\par \par \uc2 The ascension of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as the Democratic presidential nominee has excited many Democrats in Wilson County, which narrowly voted for President Biden in 2020 and has about 78,000 residents, 40 percent of whom are Black. But voters across the county say that the animus that has plagued national politics feels draining, especially in a small place where people value being able to get along.\par \par \uc2 The Struggle for Rural Votes\par \par \uc2 The new chair of the state Democratic Party, Anderson Clayton, has prioritized reconnecting with rural voters since taking office in 2023, arguing that Democrats cannot rely only on cities and suburbs to win.\par \par \uc2 David Sherrod, a conservative in Wilson who worked at a farm most of his life and is now a mechanic, said that ''the politics of both parties have flaws,'' including not engaging enough with voters outside major cities. His support, though, is fully behind former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I don't agree with everything he does,'' Mr. Sherrod said. ''But I feel listened to.''\par \par \uc2 Even a Democratic voter like Jamar Jones, 29, said he sympathized with neighbors who felt fed up with both camps.\par \par \uc2 ''You should be able to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and you can own a gun,'' Mr. Jones said. ''If the two parties are going to be polar opposites on every issue, then it's just a scam.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sherrod, however, is the kind of voter that North Carolina Democrats have struggled to persuade. Since the '90s, Republicans have mostly won the state's rural counties, partly through a message about being left behind. Mr. Trump has continued that trend, even though many voters we spoke to in Wilson acknowledged his flaws.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Wilson described feeling alienated and worn down by the emphasis on race and identity in politics. Some white, conservative-leaning voters said they were tired of the Democrats' focus on race. Mr. Trump has tried to appeal to those voters by stoking resentments about their economic fortunes, at times using racially charged language.\par \par \uc2 But many Black and Hispanic voters in Wilson said that addressing racial inequality was important for the country, and that Ms. Harris represented a more racially inclusive America.\par \par \uc2 ''It matters to me that there's a woman of color with an immigrant background running for president,'' said Flor Herrera-Picasso, 33, who remembers her Mexican American family encountering racism while she was growing up in Wilson.\par \par \uc2 ''Obviously it's not going to mean equality for all yet,'' she said, ''but it's a step in the right direction.''\par \par \uc2 Jaded by Rising Costs\par \par \uc2 Downtown Wilson was a sleepy scene decades ago. Now, it has a park decorated with oversize whirligigs, full bars on weekends and, by 2026, a new $63 million stadium that will be the home of the Carolina Mudcats, a Minor League Baseball team.\par \par \uc2 All of that development, though, has increased concerns over inflation and rising housing costs. Residents bemoaned the prices of fertilizer, electricity bills and chitlins, or sizzled pork intestines. For many people in Wilson, the math just doesn't add up.\par \par \uc2 Owning a home feels out of reach for John Reed, of Nash County, which is next to Wilson. He says he worries that he may be stuck renting.\par \par \uc2 ''The whole thing makes me feel jaded,'' said Mr. Reed, 33, who is finishing his seminary degree and says he supports \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , despite him ending his candidacy on Friday.\par \par \uc2 L.G.B.T.Q. Issues and \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stir Debate\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats we talked to said they worried about preserving gay rights. Many conservatives voiced concerns about the acceptance of transgender people in schools and sports, an issue that has resonated with large swaths of the Republican base.\par \par \uc2 Across the political spectrum, however, voters expressed divergent views on one of the top issues in this election: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Morris Worelds, 72, said he was voting for Ms. Harris because of his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and dedicating his vote to his four granddaughters.\par \par \uc2 ''To me, Wilson is still a country town, but some things like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are irrelevant to country or city,'' Mr. Worelds said.\par \par \uc2 Despite their differing opinions, many Wilson residents said they valued getting along with their neighbors, in part because there was no political bubble to hide in. Cecilia Coleman and her sister, Megan Coleman, said that at Sylvia's Family Restaurant, which their parents own, the salesmen who deliver them products were Republicans and their clientele were mostly Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ''Never any problems,'' Cecilia Coleman said.\par \par \uc2 This month, hundreds gathered at the Wilson County Fair Truck and Tractor Pull, where drivers in modified vehicles compete to see who can drag a heavy metal sled the farthest on a dirt field.\par \par \uc2 Watching it all was Kimberly Wade, 46, an independent who said her thoughts about the presidential race could be summarized in one word: ''Iffy.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wade, who is undecided, says she has long grown tired of the hyperpartisan attacks clawing away at the country. Ms. Wade's husband is disabled, and her biggest concern is increasing disability benefits.\par \par \uc2 But she said one overarching thought would guide her through November: ''I just want my community to be OK.''\par \par \uc2 FLOR HERRERA-PICASSO, the daughter of Mexican immigrants, said it matters to her that there's a woman of color with an immigrant background running for president.; MORRIS WORELDS plans to vote for Kamala Harris, and is dedicating his vote to his four granddaughters. (A12); DAVID SHERROD, said of former President Donald J. Trump: ''I don't agree with everything he does. But I feel listened to."; CECILIA AND MEGAN COLEMAN said that at their parents' restaurant, Sylvia's, the salesmen who deliver them products were Republicans and their clientele were mostly Democrats. ''Never any problems,'' Cecilia Coleman said. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SHURAN HUANG) (A13) This article appeared in print on page A12, A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240829ek8t0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\