\par\par \uc2 POLITICAL MEMO\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Barrier-Breaking Bid For Harris Carries Urgency 2016 Didn't\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,178 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first-female-president energy of 2016 has been replaced by a more serious tone, with Democrats warning gravely of the new frontiers in the post-Roe era.\par \par \uc2 They still wear suffragist-white outfits and cheer on the prospect of ''Madam President.''\par \par \uc2 But eight years after Hillary Clinton became the first woman to lead a major party's presidential ticket, Democrats are sending American women a more sober and urgent message even as they try to elect another barrier-breaking candidate.\par \par \uc2 Republican policies, they argue, have had disastrous and once-unthinkable consequences for the health and autonomy of women and their families since the overturning of Roe v. Wade. A second term for former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , they warn, would be even more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ''Simply put,'' Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said this week from the stage of her party's convention, ''they are out of their minds.''\par \par \uc2 From the women who described harrowing pregnancies and their difficulties receiving medical care to Ms. Harris's finale on Thursday night, the tone and emphasis were a radical departure from the optimistic feminism and chants of ''I'm with her'' that dominated Mrs. Clinton's 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a time where the rights of women are fundamentally under attack as it relates to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I.V.F., when and how to have a family,'' said Senator Laphonza Butler, Democrat of California and a close Harris ally. ''It's not about minimizing the importance of race or gender. It is about appreciating that in this moment in the history of our country, this election is bigger than anybody's race or gender.''\par \par \uc2 Much of what has transpired in the last eight years was unfathomable to the Democrats caught up in their excitement about Mrs. Clinton's campaign. The idea that Mr. Trump -- a man who had bragged about sexual assault -- would win, and that his Supreme Court nominees would help erase the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seemed remote.\par \par \uc2 ''In 2016, people felt they had the luxury of equality in the law and reproductive freedom in the law, and I think many people didn't see risk ahead of them,'' said Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, Democrat of New York.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision to wipe out Roe two years ago unleashed a cascade of far-reaching \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in many states across the country. Democrats quickly harnessed shock and fury over the decision into political momentum in key races that year and since. At the convention this week, Democrats offered their clearest signs yet of just how central the issue will be to their fall campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''The abrogation of our rights has been so severe and so aggressive in red states across the country that the wake-up call has been received,'' Ms. Gillibrand said. The senator, who ran for president in the 2020 campaign on a message anchored in issues of women's equality, said that the moment required a ''fighter,'' and added of Ms. Harris: ''It doesn't matter that she's a woman, to be honest. It matters that she's a fighter. And it's just great that she also happens to be a woman.''\par \par \uc2 In ways overt and subtle, plenty of speakers also highlighted the history-making potential of Ms. Harris, who is already the first Black woman and first person of South Asian descent to accept a major party's nomination. If elected, she would be the first female president in American history.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Clinton cast Ms. Harris's bid as a continuation of her efforts to shatter what she has called the ''highest and hardest glass ceiling.''\par \par \uc2 ''On the other side of that glass ceiling is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  raising her hand and taking the oath of office as our 47th president of the United States,'' Mrs. Clinton told the cheering crowd on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Harris herself does not embrace such explicit messaging, instead favoring subtle references to the encouragement she received as a child to ''be anything and do anything,'' as she said this week.\par \par \uc2 As she formally accepted her party's nomination in a dark suit on Thursday night, she said she did so ''on behalf of every American, regardless of party, race, gender or the language your grandmother speaks.''\par \par \uc2 For some voters, representation is a powerful and energizing motivator. Others recoil from discussion of identity.\par \par \uc2 ''Sometimes I feel like, when you talk about 'first woman,' it's like 'first woman' becomes the focus,'' said Mayor Cherelle Parker of Philadelphia, the first woman to lead the city. ''That is extremely important. But don't forget about the work that went in to ensure that she could be prepared to meet every opportunity.''\par \par \uc2 Many Democratic women credit Mrs. Clinton's candidacies with helping Americans grasp what a female presidential nominee might look and sound like, giving Ms. Harris running room to define herself more broadly this time around. (Of course, Mrs. Clinton -- a former first lady, senator and secretary of state -- hardly reduced her campaign to her gender, either.)\par \par \uc2 ''The focus today is, it seems to be on a discussion really about a much more holistic view of identity, and I think that's important for where we are as a country,'' said Gov. \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2 , the first woman and first openly L.G.B.T.Q. person to be elected governor of Massachusetts, who also emphasized that ''representation really matters.''\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Clinton, she added, ''broke that glass ceiling.''\par \par \uc2 In a campaign in which Mr. Trump, a white man, has already tried to question Ms. Harris's racial identity, Democrats are bracing for sexist and racist attacks on Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , the former first lady, warned her party against complacency, saying that there were many ''who are ready to question and criticize every move Kamala makes, who are eager to spread those lies, who don't want to vote for a woman.''\par \par \uc2 ''We cannot indulge our anxieties about whether this country will elect someone like Kamala, instead of doing everything we can to get someone like Kamala elected,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 So far, Mr. Trump has struggled to press a consistent and effective message against Ms. Harris, but leading Democrats do not bank on that lasting.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, at least, Mr. Trump -- who relies on the support of social conservatives and has said he is ''proudly the person responsible'' for overturning Roe v. Wade -- seemed to acknowledge that on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the Democrats had put him on defense.\par \par \uc2 ''My Administration,'' he insisted on Truth Social, ''will be great for women and their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Lisa Lerer contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Lerer contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 At the convention, Democrats made clear \uc2  rights would be a central issue. Kaitlyn Joshua, above, spoke there about being denied care for a miscarriage in Louisiana. Left, reacting to Vice President Kamala Harris speaking in North Carolina. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY RUTH FREMSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES; ERIN SCHAFF/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240825ek8p00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png