\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Providers, a Tough Business Gets Tougher\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Miller \par \uc2 2,344 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After the fall of Roe v. Wade, some clinics thought they could expand their businesses in states that still allowed them to operate. It hasn't quite worked out that way.\par \par \uc2 Providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services has always been a difficult business, with tiny-to-nonexistent profit margins and often-challenging logistical obstacles. And the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson two years ago, which eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has made the landscape of reproductive services even tougher.\par \par \uc2 The fall of Roe v. Wade has pushed many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to explore alternative markets for their services. Some clinics are moving to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights states or expanding their presence there, hoping to meet the needs of a more concentrated customer base. According to a recent analysis by The New York Times, out-of-state travel for abortions more than doubled in 2023 compared with travel in 2019, and made up nearly a fifth of recorded abortions.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Myers, a professor of economics at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  who tracks clinic openings and closures nationwide, estimated that at least 12 new clinics opened directly in response to Dobbs and another six relocated after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned in their states.\par \par \uc2 Many clinics are finding out, however, that blue states can often be almost as hostile to their presence as red ones.\par \par \uc2 In September 2022, Dr. Matthew Reeves signed a lease for a new branch of the DuPont Clinic in Beverly Hills, Calif., as he sought to expand his \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care clinic beyond its original location in Washington, D.C. In January of that year, as the future of Roe v. Wade looked increasingly uncertain, Dr. Reeves and Jennefer Russo, the chief medical officer of DuPont Clinic, had begun to think about opening an additional practice in a state that allowed access to abortions\par \par \uc2 ''Dobbs was percolating and it became apparent that the Supreme Court was likely to overturn Roe v. Wade,'' Dr. Russo said in an interview earlier this year. ''Our thinking was that patients could come to L.A. if they lived west of the Mississippi.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , which has roughly 600 affiliate health centers across the nation, has the most brand recognition. But independent clinics like Dr. Reeves's perform just over half of the nation's abortions and 61 percent of abortions after the first trimester, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network. (Most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics also perform other health care services, like OB-GYN exams and supplying birth control.)\par \par \uc2 The move to California was not an easy one. Dr. Reeves and Dr. Russo spent nearly a year scouring Los Angeles County for office space, only to have most landlords back out once they realized the clinic provided abortions after 20 weeks of pregnancy. The doctors eventually found a spot in Beverly Hills, where the City Council had unanimously affirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's legality shortly before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But as the clinic filed the necessary paperwork to obtain an operating license and began extensive renovations, the problems began. An anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, Survivors of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Holocaust, started a series of protests: papering the building with fliers of aborted fetuses, projecting the words ''murder mill'' onto its facade and marching outside the seven-story building, which was home to dozens of other health care businesses.\par \par \uc2 On June 12, 2023, roughly two months after the protests began and about four months before the clinic was supposed to open its doors, the doctors received a letter notifying them that their landlord, Douglas Emmett, was rescinding the lease. According to the letter, the DuPont Clinic was in breach of section 6.2 of its lease, which stated, ''Tenant will not do anything in the Premises that unreasonably obstructs or interferes with other tenants or occupants of the Building.''\par \par \uc2 ''We'd invested almost $2 million at this point,'' Dr. Russo said, adding that \uc2 DuPont\uc2  had filed lawsuits against both the landlord and the city of Beverly Hills. Douglas Emmett declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 ''The landlord problem in Beverly Hills is symptomatic of a larger problem we're seeing in access states,'' said Bonyen Lee-Gilmore, the vice president of communications at the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''For a lot of years, blue states and cities could get away with just proclaiming their pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  values through resolutions or public statements, but now we need them to put it into action to protect access. And they're falling short.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2 , a professional association of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, released its 2022 statistics on violence and disruption against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. The report showed a sharp increase in violence and disruption in states that were protective of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''As clinics closed in states with bans, extremists have simply shifted their focus to the states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal and protected,'' Melissa Fowler, the organization's chief program officer, said in a statement accompanying the report.\par \par \uc2 Those who have opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been quick to claim victory. ''America has moved into a new era,'' said Tim Clement, the director of outreach for Survivors of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Holocaust, which led the protests in Beverly Hills. ''Nobody thought Roe would be overturned. That was the very thing that sparked a lot of people to get into the fight.''\par \par \uc2 Others in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement acknowledged that, in the last two years, they had shifted their efforts to focus more directly on states like Michigan, Connecticut and New Mexico, where governors and legislatures had taken steps to strengthen the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. ''In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed, we are trying to reach out in a lot of different ways: social media, billboards, community groups, talking to friends and neighbors,'' said Carol Tobias, the president of National Right to Life. ''That started as soon as Dobbs came down.''\par \par \uc2 'Are You Kidding Me?'\par \par \uc2 An estimated 92 percent of abortions take place during the first trimester and the majority of those can be administered with medication, increasingly via telehealth. Since Dobbs, scores of health care clinics have added medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. But a procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  requires a clinic.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the logistical problem of finding a suitable space and a welcoming landlord, many clinics face another challenging issue: Most of their clients can't pay them.\par \par \uc2 A recent study from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rights organization, found that 73 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers had incomes under the poverty line. ''They're on the edges of the economy,'' Tracy Weitz, a professor of sociology at \uc2 American University\uc2 , said. ''You can't really raise your price for that population.''\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the C.E.O. of Whole Woman's Health, which operates six clinics in four states as well as one virtual clinic, said only two of them were currently profitable. ''Whenever I talk to people about my margins,'' she said, ''they're like, 'Are you kidding me?'''\par \par \uc2 Procedure rates -- which can run from $600 to many thousands for the rare abortions that take place late in a pregnancy -- certainly haven't kept pace with medical inflation, even though everything else about running a health care business, from insurance to equipment to payroll, has become more expensive.\par \par \uc2 The reimbursement landscape is also difficult for clinics. Patients who need help covering the costs of abortions generally have three options: private insurance, state-supported Medicaid programs and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. Three of the 36 states (and Washington, D.C.) where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, limit the use of private insurance to cover services in their state-regulated plans. Twelve states also ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage through their Affordable Care Act exchange plans.\par \par \uc2 Federal Medicaid funds cannot be used to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so 17 states provide coverage with their own state-run Medicaid programs. But many of these state-run programs reimburse at low rates. A new study from KFF found that state Medicaid reimbursement rates did not increase significantly after the first trimester -- when procedures become more complicated and, therefore, more expensive. This means that clinics are being significantly underpaid for the services they're providing.\par \par \uc2 Finally, many patients rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds -- nonprofits that raise money to help patients cover procedures and travel costs. Some clinics, like Dr. Reeves's, even employ staff to help patients gain access to this money. But the funds are in high demand and typically cover only a clinic's baseline costs.\par \par \uc2 Some of these challenges are on display at the Red River Women's Clinic, which relocated to Minnesota from North Dakota last year, after North Dakota banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The clinic charges $700 for a first-trimester procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the owner, Tammi Kromenaker, said was standard across the state. For this, Minnesota's Medicaid program reimburses at 28 percent. A medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs $750, and the state Medicaid program reimburses only at 20 percent. Ms. Kromenaker said the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds allowed her to break even. But this year, partly because of the rise in Medicaid patients, she's running at a loss.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states have significantly increased their Medicaid reimbursement rates since 2017, including New York, New Mexico and Illinois. California has proposed raising payment rates for medication and procedural abortions. But Ms. Weitz doesn't see widespread policy change on the horizon. ''Nobody wants to change those rates,'' she said. ''People are uncomfortable with the idea that someone might make a profit with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Fierce Local Resistance\par \par \uc2 Over the last two years, clinics trying to open in Fontana, Calif.; Lancaster, Pa.; and Danville, Ill. -- all in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been legal for 50 years -- have faced fierce local resistance. In Pennsylvania, free-standing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics must sign a ''transfer agreement,'' a contract with a hospital within 30 minutes that can offer emergency care if needed. Last September, a county commissioner in Lancaster warned local hospitals against signing such an agreement with the new clinic.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Reed, the C.E.O. of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , \uc2 Keystone\uc2 , said at least one hospital cited an ''onslaught of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  calls,'' in its refusal to form a partnership. (Such partnerships are required for clinics to operate in Pennsylvania.) The state department of health eventually provided the clinic with a waiver, but only to offer medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both Ms. Reed in Lancaster and Jon Dunn, the president and C.E.O. of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Orange and San Bernardino Counties, believe that local officials purposefully slow-rolled their building permits, forcing months of delays over cosmetic details like sconces and the height of parking signs. Mr. Dunn said that 13 months after the organization leased a downtown property in Fontana, the city passed a moratorium on issuing new building permits. The organization had already spent $1 million on renovations and city-mandated upgrades and it subsequently sued the city, saying the moratorium violated the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 In July, the \uc2 Fontana City Council\uc2  extended the moratorium for another year. In an email, a spokesman for the city said that the moratorium affected ''more than 70 proposed projects'' and that pending the results of a land-use study, health care providers might eventually be allowed to open in the area.\par \par \uc2 ''The decision to overturn Roe has emboldened anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians in a way we hadn't seen before, with more aggressive tactics, even in blue states like California,'' Mr. Dunn said. ''Those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are more dug in and more committed to stopping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in their areas.''\par \par \uc2 In Danville, Ill., the City Council narrowly passed an ordinance in May 2023 banning the shipping and receiving of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and equipment -- a move that the attorney general of Illinois called a violation of state law and largely ''symbolic.''\par \par \uc2 Soon afterward, an Illinois man named Philip Buyno repeatedly drove his truck into the building that LaDonna Prince, the owner and director of Affirmative Care Solutions, had purchased with plans to open a clinic there. He had reinforced his vehicle with wood planks and loaded it with several containers of gasoline. Mr. Buyno, 73, was eventually sentenced to five years in prison for attempted arson.\par \par \uc2 But the incident became a lesson for Ms. Prince. She has since installed bollards, a privacy fence and costly ballistic panels ''just in case someone would drive down the road and decide to shoot up part of the building,'' she said. Given the extent of the damage, the clinic has not yet opened and Ms. Prince said the threat of physical harm remained ''a huge deterrence'' to hiring staff.)\par \par \uc2 Clinic owners say these realities can make it difficult to insure their properties. ''Premiums are higher because of the nature of work that we do,'' Ms. Prince said. And a year after the attack on her building, her claim still has not closed, she said.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Pepper, the C.E.O. of Choices clinics, which recently expanded from Tennessee to Carbondale, Ill., said new clinics like her own couldn't just open like they were any other business. ''We're moving into somebody's house. We want to behave as guests,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pepper said she introduced herself to the nearby neighbors, a \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and a Freddy's, and let them know how to reach out if they had questions. And she suggested to her neighbors that an influx of potential new shoppers could be a boon for them. ''I hope they're spending money at those businesses,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 From top: Affirmative Care Solutions in Danville, Ill., a planned clinic that has been closed since a man rammed his truck into the building a year ago; Jennefer Russo, the chief medical officer of DuPont Clinic; Melissa Reed, the C.E.O. of Planned Parenthood, Keystone, in Pennsylvania; and LaDonna Prince, the owner and director of Affirmative Care Solutions. All have faced opposition to opening or expanding clinics. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY LEE KLAFCZYNSKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JENNA SCHOENEFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; HANNAH YOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (BU8) This article appeared in print on page BU1, BU8. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240825ek8i0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
