\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Arizona Court Sides With Republicans Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Language in Voter Pamphlets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court ruled that informational brochures the state sends to voters can use the term ''unborn human being'' to describe an embryo or a fetus.\par \par \uc2 Two days after Arizona's secretary of state certified the signatures needed to put before voters a ballot measure establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution, the Arizona Supreme Court sided with Republican anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers in a dispute over how to describe the measure to voters.\par \par \uc2 The court said on Wednesday that informational pamphlets the state sends out to registered voters describing ballot measures can use the term ''unborn human being'' to describe a fetus or embryo. Reversing a lower court decision that the term violated state law requiring ''impartial'' language in the publicity pamphlets, the high court said in a brief order that the language ''substantially complies'' with the requirement for impartiality.\par \par \uc2 The words ''unborn human being'' will not appear on actual ballots; the language on the ballots is determined by the secretary of state, \uc2 Adrian Fontes\uc2 , who is a Democrat.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups -- who celebrated when Mr. Fontes announced late Monday that they had gathered a record number of signatures to put the question on the November ballot -- said they were ''deeply disappointed'' by the court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''This decision is beyond disgraceful,'' Athena Salman, the director of Arizona campaigns for \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , said in a statement. ''Muddying the waters and relying on lies are the only tools left for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists, whose agenda is demonstrably at odds with voters in our state.''\par \par \uc2 ''Unborn human being'' and ''unborn child'' are the terms long used by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, who seek to establish that life begins at conception, and to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from that point.\par \par \uc2 Polls show that most Arizona voters support the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure, Proposition 139, and Democrats are hoping that it will drive higher turnout to their advantage in a battleground state: Arizona will be crucial not only in the presidential race but also in the party's fight to keep its narrow majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Voters have sided with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in all seven states where ballot questions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have appeared in the two years since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that found a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Constitution. That includes purple and red states such as Kansas and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 As that winning streak has widened, Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have sought to make it harder to get measures on the ballot, or to pass them. In Montana, one of three states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are waiting for official certification of the signatures they gathered for a ballot amendment, the Supreme Court ruled last month that the Republican secretary of state wrongly tried to change election rules to disqualify signatures from inactive voters.\par \par \uc2 With measures now officially on the ballot in November in eight states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are trying to shape the official state language that voters will see as they prepare to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 Last month in Florida, for example, a state panel largely appointed by Republicans approved language for a financial impact statement required to accompany a proposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment. The statement says that the amendment would result in ''significantly'' more abortions and fewer live births, and could force the state to pay high legal costs to resolve lawsuits that might result from it, and that an increase in abortions ''may negatively affect the growth of state and local revenues over time.''\par \par \uc2 Arizona's governor, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , is a Democrat, but the justices on the high court were all appointed by Republicans. In April, the court ruled that a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  passed in 1864, before Arizona was a state, was still in effect and should be enforced. The Legislature then voted to repeal the ban, leaving in place a prohibition on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Joanna De La Cruz, a spokeswoman for It Goes Too Far, a group opposing the measure, defended the language in the publicity pamphlet as ''impartial and accurate'' and argued that the language on the ballot itself is misleading.\par \par \uc2 ''It would serve voters well to have had the actual language of the amendment be more clear so they knew they were choosing between the current 15-week limit and unlimited, unregulated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Prop 139,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The amendment, similar to those passed by voters in Ohio and Michigan and on the ballot in Missouri and Florida, asks voters to establish a ''fundamental'' right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prohibiting the state from restricting or banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability -- the point where a fetus can survive outside the uterus, generally around 24 weeks of pregnancy -- except to protect the pregnant woman's health.\par \par \uc2 After viability the state could restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unless in the ''good faith judgment of a treating health care professional,'' it was necessary to protect the health or life of the pregnant patient.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  signs were left at the Arizona Statehouse in Phoenix in April, above. Informational pamphlets can refer to an ''unborn human being,'' but the language will not appear on the ballot. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CASSIDY ARAIZA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240816ek8g00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
