\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access To Be on Ballot In Arizona in Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,218 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Arizona, a swing state, and Missouri will be among the states voting on whether to establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitutions. Democrats have used the issue to turn out voters.\par \par \uc2 Arizona voters will decide in November whether to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution, a measure that could strongly influence turnout in a battleground state crucial to the presidential election and control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona secretary of state's office said it had certified 577,971 signatures collected by a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, 50 percent more than required to put the constitutional amendment on the ballot in November. It is the largest number of certified signatures for any ballot measure in state history.\par \par \uc2 A similar question will appear on the ballot in Missouri, after the secretary of state there said on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had collected 254,871 valid signatures, more than enough to place the measure on the ballot. Missouri, the first state to enact an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022, could become the first state where a citizen-sponsored measure overturns a near-total ban.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have prevailed in all seven states where the question of how to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been put directly to voters in the two years since the demise of Roe, the 1973 decision that said the United States Constitution protected a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The run of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights successes has put Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups on the defensive. They have mounted ''decline to sign'' campaigns, filed lawsuits trying to prevent signatures from being certified, and sponsored legislation trying to make it harder for ballot measures to pass. Before the high court overturned Roe, almost every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure had been sponsored by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side.\par \par \uc2 Measures to establish or protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are already on the November ballot in six other states: Florida, South Dakota, Colorado, New York, Maryland and Nevada. But only Arizona and Nevada are seen as presidential battleground states, where Democrats are hoping that support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will drive higher turnout in their favor. (South Dakota and Missouri are the only two of those states with near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.)\par \par \uc2 Democrats have leveraged Americans' unhappiness over the court's decision to overturn Roe into gains in elections up and down the ballot over the last two years. The party's support for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pulled in more young women in particular, a demographic that Democrats hope will prove influential in November. The Democratic nominee for president, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , has campaigned energetically on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is also crucial in the fight for control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , a Democratic representative, is facing off against Kari Lake, the Republican nominee, for an open seat. In the House, where Republicans are trying to hold on to a narrow majority, Democrats are hoping the ballot measure, known as Proposition 139, will help them flip two seats in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a huge win for Arizona voters who will now get to vote yes on restoring and protecting the right to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, free from political interference, once and for all,'' Cheryl Bruce, the campaign manager for Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access, the coalition sponsoring the measure, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Joanna De La Cruz, a spokeswoman for It Goes Too Far, a group opposing the amendment, said in a statement that proponents had overstated support for the measure: ''This is a far fewer number of signatures than proponents originally touted.''\par \par \uc2 The group argues that the law in Arizona, which now allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until 15 weeks of pregnancy and for medical emergencies after that, ''is settled'' and that the amendment is ''extreme.'' Proponents, she said, ''fail to tell voters that basic safety precautions now in place to protect girls and women will be unenforceable under the language of the amendment.''\par \par \uc2 The proposed amendments in Missouri and Arizona are similar to those that voters have approved in other states, including Michigan, another battleground state, and Ohio, which has a more conservative electorate. Each would establish a ''fundamental right'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prohibiting the state from banning or limiting the procedure before viability, or when the fetus can survive outside the uterus -- generally around 24 weeks of pregnancy -- unless those limits are to protect the life of the patient and do not infringe on the pregnant woman's ''autonomous decision making.''\par \par \uc2 The states could regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, but would have to continue to allow the procedure in cases where, in the ''good-faith judgment of a treating health care professional,'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to protect the life or health of the pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 ''It's about giving Missourians the ability to make their own personal private health care decisions without interference from politicians,'' Rachel Sweet, the campaign manager for Missourians for Constitutional Freedom, said at a news conference after the signatures were certified.\par \par \uc2 While the Missouri ballot amendment is unlikely to have the national political ramifications that Arizona's could, a win for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Missouri would restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access not only for the state, but also for women in the swath of nearby Southern states that have near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. ''The impact will be regional,'' said Mallory Schwarz, the executive director for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Action Missouri.\par \par \uc2 But Stephanie Bell, a spokeswoman for Missouri Stands With Women, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, argued that the amendment would eliminate safety provisions, including the right to sue doctors for negligence during pregnancy, labor or delivery.\par \par \uc2 ''Missourians are smart and they don't like their freedom and safety being stolen from them, and once they learn the real truth about this amendment will vote it down,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Adrian Fontes\uc2 , a Democrat who is Arizona's secretary of state, noted as he certified the signatures that more legal challenges are inevitable. ''It's going to create a bunch of lawsuits, so that's got to happen,'' he said, just before affixing the seal of the state to the paperwork making the ballot measure official.\par \par \uc2 Still, the certifications will make it more difficult for opponents to remove the measures from ballots.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Republicans are betting that they, too, can use a ballot question to drive turnout in their favor. The Republican-led legislature approved a measure that will ask voters to make it a state crime to unlawfully cross the border from Mexico, placing another hot-button issue directly before voters.\par \par \uc2 In a CBS News poll in May, 65 percent of likely Arizona voters said they would support a ballot measure establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while 21 percent said they would not and 14 percent said they were unsure.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 St. Louis University\uc2  poll of likely voters in Missouri in February found that a plurality of voters -- 44 percent -- supported the proposed ballot measure there, while 37 percent opposed and 19 percent were unsure.\par \par \uc2  rights supporters in Arizona collected 577,971 signatures, 50 percent more than required to put the measure on the ballot. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROSS D. FRANKLIN/ASSOCIATED PRESS) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240814ek8e00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
