\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Manhattan Clinic Will Stop Performing Abortions After 20 Weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Fahy \par \uc2 1,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The shift came as \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's New York chapter revealed plans to close four clinics across the state, as financial issues mount.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  announced this week that its only Manhattan clinic would stop performing abortions after 20 weeks of pregnancy, a significant shift in a state that has maintained and even expanded access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the two years since the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The clinic, Planned Parenthood's Manhattan Health Center, can no longer afford the ''deep sedation'' required to perform abortions beyond the 20-week mark, Wendy Stark, the president of Planned Parenthood of Greater New York, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The move was the latest sign of financial struggles for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's New York chapter, which also plans to close four clinics around the state, including its sole clinic on Staten Island, Ms. Stark said. The chapter has already instituted executive pay cuts and consolidated job functions.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Stark confirmed that the Manhattan clinic plans to stop providing abortions past 20 weeks of pregnancy on Sept. 3, a change that was first reported by The City. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in New York State through the 24th week of pregnancy, and later in cases where a fetus is not viable or a woman's life or health is at risk.\par \par \uc2 The Manhattan Health Center, located downtown on Bleecker Street, is currently the only \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in New York that performs abortions after 20 weeks. Less than 2 percent of all of the abortions performed at the chapter's clinics occur after that benchmark, according to a spokeswoman, Senti Sojwal.\par \par \uc2 While other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in New York will still offer the procedure after 20 weeks, the decision by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was worrisome news to those tracking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the country.\par \par \uc2 ''Certainly, it's a concern when any clinic or health care provider has to stop providing any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,'' said \uc2 Rachel K. Jones\uc2 , a researcher for the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''It's usually not a good sign, right?''\par \par \uc2 Travel across state lines for abortions -- trips for both procedural abortions and to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- more than doubled in 2023 compared with 2019, according to a New York Times analysis, and made up nearly a fifth of recorded abortions in the country.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jones said that while New York has not been the primary destination for people who travel to other states to end pregnancies, abortions have steadily increased in New York since 2017, contributing to rising costs.\par \par \uc2 New York received at least 6,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients from out of state last year, according to data from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . Roughly 10,000 abortions are performed in the state each month, the group said.\par \par \uc2 A significant share of abortions in New York take place at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics, which logged 30,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  visits statewide last year, Ms. Sojwal said.\par \par \uc2 Abortions performed at later stages of pregnancy are the most politically contentious in the national debate over the procedure and are quite rare.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the latest year for which data is available, more than 93 percent of abortions in the United States were performed before 13 weeks of pregnancy, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , and less than 1 percent took place after 21 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Abortions after that point are usually performed because the fetus has a fatal condition that could not be detected sooner or because the mother's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 Dennis Poust, executive director of the New York State Catholic Conference, said in a statement that the group welcomed \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's decision to stop performing abortions after 20 weeks at its Manhattan clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''To the extent that this economic decision by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  limits these incidents, we rejoice,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Stark said the Manhattan clinic would work to provide deep sedation in-house, rather than by hiring outside specialists as it currently does. Such a shift could enable it to again offer abortions after 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The financial crunch at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , Ms. Stark said, is the result of a widening gap between the cost of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and the reimbursement offered by both private and public insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 Most significantly, she said, Medicaid reimbursements do not cover the cost of care, and as a result, ''our financial health has been stretched to its limit.''\par \par \uc2 The situation is dire enough that the chapter also plans to close clinics in three towns upstate, Goshen, Amsterdam and Cobleskill, as well as its Staten Island clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''We think this is a big opportunity for New York State to step up to the plate and increase reimbursement for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in this moment when our \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ecosystem across the state is faltering,'' Ms. Stark said.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  pushed back against that characterization in a statement, maintaining that ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal and accessible to anyone in New York.'' The governor, a Democrat, noted that she had allocated $35 million to an \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Provider Support Fund designed to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the state.\par \par \uc2 ''We will continue working with advocates and our partners in government to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is always protected in New York,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 According to Amy Paulin, a Democratic state legislator who chairs the Assembly's committee on health, 50 percent of abortions at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in New York are covered by Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had asked the New York Legislature for $13 million in funding to be included in the state's budget, Ms. Paulin said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The Assembly and the Senate included the funding in their budget proposals, she said, but it was not included in the final $237 billion budget.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Paulin said the legislature would have to address the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  funding issue when lawmakers returned to Albany in January. She said that all branches of the state government, including the governor, had actively worked to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in New York.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not that we haven't been generous and supportive,'' Ms. Paulin said. ''But there has been a shift that we need to now recognize.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240807ek870004x\par }\page {\par