\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris's Running Mate On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Immigrants, Israel and Climate Change\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Astor \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As governor of Minnesota, he has enacted policies to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, provide free meals for schoolchildren, allow recreational marijuana and set renewable energy goals.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of Minnesota, the newly announced running mate to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , has worked with his state's Democratic-controlled Legislature to enact an ambitious agenda of liberal policies: free college tuition for low-income students, free meals for schoolchildren, legal recreational marijuana and protections for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 ''You don't win elections to bank political capital,'' Mr. Walz wrote last year about his approach to governing. ''You win elections to burn political capital and improve lives.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans have slammed these policies as big-government liberalism and accused Mr. Walz of taking a hard left turn since he represented a politically divided district in Congress years ago.\par \par \uc2 Here is an overview of where Mr. Walz stands on some key issues.\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz signed a bill last year that guaranteed Minnesotans a ''fundamental right to make autonomous decisions'' about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care on issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception and fertility treatments.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already protected by a Minnesota Supreme Court decision, but the new law guarded against a future court reversing that precedent as the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  did with Roe v. Wade, and Mr. Walz said this year that he was also open to an amendment to the state's Constitution that would codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Another bill he signed legally shields patients, and their medical providers, if they receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Minnesota after traveling from a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care,'' he said on CNN in March, adding: ''I think old white men need to learn how to talk about this a little more. And I think the biggest thing is, listen to women, listen to what they're saying.''\par \par \uc2 Climate change\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz's administration has set a goal for Minnesota to rely on 100 percent renewable electricity by 2040. He signed legislation that directs officials to prioritize the creation of renewable energy facilities in the same communities that previously had fossil-fuel plants.\par \par \uc2 During his re-election campaign for governor in 2022, he said that he wanted electric vehicles to account for 20 percent of cars on Minnesota roads by 2030, and that he wanted the state to reach net-zero carbon emissions by 2050. His administration has adopted stricter limits on tailpipe emissions, which effectively require auto manufacturers to make more electric or hybrid vehicles or otherwise improve fuel efficiency.\par \par \uc2 He recently announced a $200 million grant from the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  to reduce emissions in the food system, including those generated from farming, supermarkets and organic waste processing facilities.\par \par \uc2 He has argued that it is possible to ''create a clean energy future where we can protect our water, protect our land and do that in a manner that grows the economy.'' Read more about how he has championed climate issues as governor.\par \par \uc2 Democracy\par \par \uc2 He has condemned the attempts by former President Donald J. Trump to overturn the 2020 election, including his repeated lie that he won Minnesota. ''That is an affront to democracy,'' Mr. Walz said in May. ''That is absolutely a line that cannot be crossed.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Walz signed a bill establishing automatic voter registration, letting 16- and 17-year-olds preregister to vote so that they are on the rolls when they turn 18, and allowing people to put themselves on a permanent list for absentee ballots.\par \par \uc2 He signed another bill this year that prohibits voter suppression, including any action that ''results in a disparate burden'' on a minority group, even if the burden is unintentional.\par \par \uc2 That legislation provided funding for local governments to open polling locations on college campuses and established criminal penalties for using ''deepfakes'' to try to influence an election.\par \par \uc2 Economic policy\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz and Minnesota's Democratic legislative majorities have enacted progressive economic policies such as providing free college tuition for low-income students, offering free meals for schoolchildren, and instituting paid medical and family leave.\par \par \uc2 His budgets have provided for tax rebates for low- and middle-income Minnesotans and have funded a child tax credit for low-income families, alongside tax increases on gas and on investment income for wealthy taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Mr. Walz signed a bipartisan measure that set aside $1.9 billion in bond funding for local construction and renovation projects, such as housing, roads and water infrastructure. In 2023, he approved an additional $2.6 billion in infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 Immigration\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz supports a path to citizenship for some undocumented immigrants. In a 2021 letter to Democratic leaders in Congress, he urged such a path for ''Dreamers'' brought to the United States as children, for essential workers, for people whose countries are in crisis and for the families of those immigrants.\par \par \uc2 As a member of Congress years ago, he voted for stricter screening of refugees, but changed his position when he ran for governor. During his 2022 re-election campaign, he denounced his Republican opponent for saying that accepting immigrants without enough resources threatened ''Minnesota's fabric of life.'' Mr. Walz said, ''He's 100 percent wrong morally, and he's 100 percent wrong economically and culturally.''\par \par \uc2 He signed a bill last year that made undocumented immigrants eligible for Minnesota driver's licenses, which he argued would make roads safer by reducing unlicensed driving. He does not have a clear public record on border-security policies.\par \par \uc2 Israel and Gaza\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz has not spoken extensively about Israel or Gaza.\par \par \uc2 In the immediate aftermath of the Oct. 7 attacks, he condemned \uc2 Hamas\uc2  while saying he supported a deal to allow humanitarian aid into Gaza, writing on social media: ''The vast majority of Palestinians are not \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does not represent the Palestinian people. We cannot let terrorists like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  win.''\par \par \uc2 But he has been largely silent since then, even after calls from protesters in Minnesota for the state to divest from Israeli companies.\par \par \uc2 Tim Walz with President Biden last year at a power plant in Fridley, Minn. The governor has aimed to prioritize the environment and also grow the economy. Left, Vice President Kamala Harris with Mr. Walz in St. Paul, Minn., in March. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY PETE MAROVICH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JENN ACKERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240807ek8700044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
