\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Block Is Upheld on Disputed Utah  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen and Anna Betts \par \uc2 825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Utah cannot enforce its near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while a challenge to the law proceeds in the courts, the State Supreme Court ruled on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Utah Supreme Court upheld on Thursday a suspension of the state's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning the procedure remains legal while a court challenge to the law proceeds.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruled to overturn Roe v. Wade, it cleared the way for two Utah laws to come into force: a ban on most abortions after the 18th week of pregnancy, which was passed in 2019 and is currently in effect, and a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban passed in 2020 that would prohibit the procedure at any time during pregnancy, with very limited exceptions, including for cases of rape or incest or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban took effect in 2022, but the Planned Parenthood Association of Utah almost immediately filed a lawsuit in the state seeking to block the ban. The organization argued that the ban violated several provisions in the State Constitution, including those that guarantee a right to determine family composition and a right to gender equality.\par \par \uc2 A trial court issued a preliminary injunction in July 2022 blocking the state from enforcing the near-total ban while the case proceeded. Utah state officials appealed, but the State Supreme Court ruled against them on Thursday and left the injunction in place.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Kathryn Boyd, president and chief executive officer of Planned Parenthood Association of Utah, hailed the ruling, saying: ''While we celebrate this win, we know the fight is not over.''\par \par \uc2 Katie Daniel, state policy director of SBA Pro-Life America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, expressed disappointment, but said that she was hopeful the ban would eventually take effect, because ''this law is the will of the people and it is clear that the unborn deserve protections.''\par \par \uc2 In court filings, the state had argued that the Utah Constitution does not protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the injunction imposed ''severe irreparable harm on the state side of the balance, given the profound state and public interest at stake -- the preservation of human life, both the mother's and the unborn child's.''\par \par \uc2 The state also challenged Planned Parenthood Association of Utah's standing to file the lawsuit, and argued that the trial court had abused its discretion and erred in issuing the injunction.\par \par \uc2 But the State Supreme Court rejected those arguments on Thursday in a 4-1 decision, with only Chief Justice Matthew B. Durrant dissenting. In an 89-page decision, the court found: ''On the evidence the parties presented to the district court, we cannot conclude that the court abused its discretion or went against the clear weight of that evidence when it decided that maintaining the status quo would not be adverse to the public interest.''\par \par \uc2 The case now returns to a lower court, and is expected to take months to adjudicate.\par \par \uc2 Utah is one of a handful of states where polls show that less than a majority of voters -- 45 percent in a recent survey -- believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. The other states in that category in the survey were Arkansas, Idaho, North Dakota and South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals in Utah typically do not provide abortions outside of extenuating circumstances; instead, 95 percent of all recorded abortions are provided in licensed clinics, according to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There are currently four health clinics which provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, ranging from surgical procedures to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Three are in the Salt Lake City area, and the other is in Logan, about a half an hour's drive south of the Idaho border.\par \par \uc2 In March 2023, the Gov. \uc2 Spencer Cox\uc2 , a Republican, signed a bill that had easily passed the Republican-controlled legislature that would have, among other provisions, made it a crime to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anywhere other than a hospital. But after \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  sued, Judge Andrew H. Stone of Utah's Third Judicial District in May 2023 temporarily blocked the law, known as H.B. 467, from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 Then, in an unexpected development this year, the legislature repealed the most contentious elements of the clinic bill, in hopes that it would spur the State Supreme Court to rule more quickly on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference Thursday after the ruling, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and activists, dressed in black, vowed to push for a special session later this summer in order to enact a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. If successful, that would mirror what happened last year in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The Utah Supreme Court agreed that a near-total \uc2  ban from 2022 cannot be enforced while a legal challenge proceeds. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RICK BOWMER/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240802ek820002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
