\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Mother of Harris's Stepchildren Takes Aim at a Resurfaced Vance Remark\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Rogers \par \uc2 1,164 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JD Vance's 2021 insult of ''childless cat ladies'' has sparked bipartisan outrage at a moment when women's choices are seen as a galvanizing political force.\par \par \uc2 In a 2021 interview with Fox News, JD Vance lamented that the United States was being run by ''childless cat ladies'' like Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , women who he claimed had no ''direct stake'' in the country's future.\par \par \uc2 This week, Ms. Harris received a powerful gesture of support from one of her biggest advocates: the mother of her stepchildren.\par \par \uc2 ''For over 10 years, since Cole and Ella were teenagers, Kamala has been a co-parent with Doug and I,'' Kerstin Emhoff said in a statement released by the office of the second gentleman, Doug Emhoff. ''She is loving, nurturing, fiercely protective and always present. I love our blended family and am grateful to have her in it.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday morning, Ella Emhoff chimed in as well: ''How can you be 'childless' when you have cutie pie kids like Cole and I,'' she wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , urging her mother to ''say it louder for the people in the back.''\par \par \uc2 Although the comments by Mr. Vance are several years old, dating to when he was running for the Senate, they are landing differently in a new political climate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance is now former President Donald J. Trump's running mate, and his words have resurfaced at a moment when many women, in polls and at the ballot box, are defending their right to make their own choices -- about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , birth control, access to fertility services or not having children at all.\par \par \uc2 In the Fox interview, Mr. Vance said Ms. Harris and other Democrats, including Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , ''don't really have a direct stake'' in the future because they are ''people without children.'' (Mr. Buttigieg and his husband, Chasten Buttigieg, welcomed twins in August 2021.)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance said the country is run by ''a bunch of childless cat ladies who are miserable at their own lives and the choices that they've made.''\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign did not respond to a request for comment on the remarks, which re-emerged earlier this week. But Mr. Vance's sister, Lindsay Lewis, called the criticism a machination of the media and the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 ''JD was raised by some of the strongest women I know and went on to marry an incredibly strong woman in Usha,'' Ms. Lewis said in a statement sent through her brother's office. ''JD is a testament to the women in his life, and the attacks from the media and Democrats that assume anything otherwise is vile.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris married Mr. Emhoff in 2014, when Cole and Ella were teenagers. Ms. Harris, whose parents had filed for divorce by the time she was eight, wrote in Elle magazine in 2019 that she was initially wary of being a ''temporary fixture'' in the children's lives.\par \par \uc2 She said they built the relationship over time. ''I was already hooked on Doug, but I believe it was Cole and Ella who reeled me in,'' she wrote. Years later, the trio decided on a name for Ms. Harris that sounded warmer than the term ''stepmom.'' They came up with ''Momala.''\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , a Democrat of New Hampshire who gave advice to Ms. Harris on balancing work and parenting when the vice president was a senator, called Mr. Vance's comments ''inane'' and ''insidious.''\par \par \uc2 ''Vice President Harris's children already know how strong, warm, loving and talented she is,'' Ms. Hassan said.\par \par \uc2 The outrage over Mr. Vance's remarks has been bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 ''I've followed JD Vance's evolution over the last few years and knew of the comment and was repulsed by it then and now,'' said Alyssa Farah Griffin, a former Republican strategist and Trump administration official who now co-hosts ''The View.'' ''But so many women in my life -- left, right, and center -- are just discovering it and couldn't be more turned off. To suggest women without kids have less value in society is offensive.''\par \par \uc2 Meghan McCain\uc2 , a conservative commentator and the daughter of Senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , wrote on social media that Mr. Vance's comments would have a ripple effect, angering families who have had to turn to fertility procedures to have children -- or people who don't want their reproductive status commented on or judged at all.\par \par \uc2 ''I have been trying to warn every conservative man I know,'' she said. ''These JD comments are activating women across all sides, including my most conservative Trump-supporting friends. These comments have caused real pain and are just innately unchristian. This is not who we are.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fueled Democratic victories across the country in the nearly two years since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, which guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has emerged as the most outspoken defender of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the Biden administration, while Mr. Vance, who has described himself as ''100 percent pro-life,'' has supported a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and opposed exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Now, as a vice-presidential candidate, Mr. Vance has tried to modify his past views.\par \par \uc2 ''You have to believe in reasonable exceptions because that's where the American people are,'' Mr. Vance told Fox News this week. Americans say at record levels that they support at least some access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris and other Democrats have said the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade had consequences that could also affect access to fertility treatments like in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year in Alabama, the state's Supreme Court ruled that frozen embryos could be considered ''extrauterine children'' under state law, prompting several fertility clinics in the state to suspend I.V.F. treatments. The governor had to sign an emergency order protecting the practice.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance signed onto a letter in June supporting access to in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday afternoon, the Harris campaign sent an email to supporters, wishing a ''Happy World IVF Day to Everyone Except JD Vance.''\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  data on fertility clinic success rates, in 2021 there were about 167,689 egg or embryo-banking cycles performed in which all the eggs or embryos were frozen for future use. Approximately 2.3 percent of all infants born in the United States every year are conceived using assisted reproductive technology.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Farah Griffin said that questioning women's choices about motherhood is a losing political strategy.\par \par \uc2 ''If I were the Harris campaign,'' Ms. Farah Griffin said. ''I would air that clip in suburban media markets in swing states from now until Election Day.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris with her stepchildren, Ella and Cole Emhoff, in 2021 during the inauguration ceremonies. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RUTH FREMSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240726ek7q0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
