\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Eager for First Female President, But Wary of Same Old Hurdles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei, Jenna Russell, Richard Fausset and Christina Morales \par \uc2 1,838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters eager to elect the first female president pointed to anger over a loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but also acknowledged a fear that sexism would remain difficult for Ms. Harris to overcome.\par \par \uc2 In the eight years since Hillary Clinton failed to win the American presidency, the work force for the first time grew to include more college-educated women than college-educated men. The #MeToo movement exposed sexual harassment and toppled powerful men. The Supreme Court overturned the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Will any -- or all -- of it make a difference for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris seems almost certain to become the Democratic Party's presidential nominee after President Biden's decision not to seek re-election. As such, she faces, fairly or not, some of the same electability questions that Mrs. Clinton confronted in a nation that, unlike many of its peers around the globe, has yet to pick a woman as its leader.\par \par \uc2 A presidential contest pitting Ms. Harris against former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  would represent a rematch of sorts: Mr. Trump would again have to run against a woman who held a top administration position and served in the Senate. He defeated Mrs. Clinton in 2016 in spite of her winning the popular vote by a wide margin.\par \par \uc2 But the dynamics would be unquestionably different. Ms. Harris has neither the political legacy nor the baggage of Mrs. Clinton. Mr. Trump, having served a turbulent term in office, is now a known quantity. Ms. Harris is Black and of South Asian descent.\par \par \uc2 And the country is not the same as it was eight long years ago.\par \par \uc2 ''Women are angrier, and that could be motivating,'' said Karen Crowley, 64, an independent voter and retired nurse in Concord, N.H., who would not vote for Mr. Trump, did not feel like she could support Mr. Biden and now planned to back Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 Among the motivations Ms. Crowley cited were the demise of Roe v. Wade and comments and actions by Mr. Trump that many women see as sexist and misogynistic. ''A woman president might be more possible now,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But for female voters and activists eager to break that elusive glass ceiling, there was also fear that sexism would remain difficult for Ms. Harris to overcome.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a patriarchy out there,'' Ms. Crowley said. ''She's smart and she's a prosecutor, but there are a lot of old white men who will want to stop her. The only thing wrong with her is that she's a woman.''\par \par \uc2 Discussing the gender of a politician can feel reductive and regressive, especially when it does not seem as relevant in other countries. The United Kingdom has had three female prime ministers. Mexico elected its first female president this year.\par \par \uc2 Yet when a woman runs for office in the United States, many voters still mention her gender unprompted in interviews, identifying it as a concern -- often not for themselves, they say, but for the wider electorate.\par \par \uc2 Julia Blake, 80, of La Jolla, Calif., said she had spent a lot of time arguing with her book club friends about whether a woman could be elected president. One after the next -- professional women, with doctorates and master's degrees -- they said they thought the answer was no. Ms. Blake was indignant with them.\par \par \uc2 ''I said, 'If women think a woman can't win, and they repeat that year after year, we will never get a female president,''' said Ms. Blake, who supported Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota and also donated to Ms. Harris during the Democratic primary in 2020. ''I don't think they're giving women enough credit.''\par \par \uc2 To be sure, party affiliation, not gender, remains most important for many voters. ''I would not vote for her,'' said Naomi Villalba, 74, a Republican from Dallas who supports Mr. Trump but thinks Ms. Harris a better choice for Democrats than Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden won 55 percent of the female vote in 2020, compared with Mrs. Clinton's 54 percent in 2016, according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . Mr. Trump's support among women grew slightly to 44 percent in 2020, up from 39 percent in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of having Ms. Harris atop the Democratic ticket energized some voters looking to elect a female president. But it also resurfaced old fears about the fact that Mr. Trump had lost to a man (Mr. Biden) but defeated a woman (Mrs. Clinton).\par \par \uc2 If ultimately not successful, Mrs. Clinton's candidacy did change the idea of what was possible, said Christina Wolbrecht, a political scientist at the \uc2 University of Notre Dame\uc2  who studies women's voting patterns. Ms. Klobuchar and Senators Kirsten Gillibrand of New York and Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts were taken seriously as candidates during the 2020 election, as was \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , the former governor of South Carolina who challenged Mr. Trump this year.\par \par \uc2 ''That suggests to me that post-\uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , people are increasingly comfortable with the idea of a woman president,'' Dr. Wolbrecht said.\par \par \uc2 Forty-two percent of women felt it was at least somewhat important to elect a woman as president in their lifetime, according to a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  report last year. In the poll, 39 percent of respondents, both male and female, said a female president would be better at working out compromises and 37 percent said a woman would be better at maintaining a respectful tone in politics. (More than half said that gender did not matter on those measures.)\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris appears to have a special bond with Black women in particular, who comprise a key part of the Democratic base and have been especially enthusiastic in their past support for her.\par \par \uc2 Laurie Nsiah-Jefferson, director for the Center for Women in Politics and Public Policy at the \uc2 University of Massachusetts Boston\uc2 , said that much had changed for women since 2016. Concerns over Mr. Trump's positions on issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  transformed from remote possibility to concrete reality after he took office, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''When he was elected, we were disappointed, we were upset -- there were marches, demonstrations, all kinds of things -- and we had a good idea what was going to happen,'' Dr. Nsiah-Jefferson said. ''But now we know what happened.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has already signaled that he considers his gender worth highlighting: At one point during the Republican National Convention last week, he walked out to ''It's a Man's Man's Man's World,'' by James Brown.\par \par \uc2 But Dr. Nsiah-Jefferson thinks that Ms. Harris will also lean in to the fact that she is a woman. ''She's going to talk about the way in which politics and policy impacts on women,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Some voters would like to lose the gender talk altogether.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to take the emphasis off the gender identification stuff and put it on the person themselves and their own abilities,'' said Marilyn McDole of Oregon, Wis., who attended a weekend re-election rally in Stoughton, Wis., for Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 . ''Because that's so stigmatizing and damaging to women. That's not fair.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris, Ms. McDole added, has ''got experience up the wazoo.''\par \par \uc2 Several Democratic voters, however, said that a female nominee would help amplify perhaps the party's strongest issue: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Katy Sorenson, 69, a former commissioner in Miami-Dade County, Fla., said the overturning of Roe had been a ''galvanizing phenomenon.'' ''It's not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; it's problem pregnancies that have so many women concerned about what they're going to do, and can they get the health care they need,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In Raleigh, N.C., Mary Lucas, 36, said that Ms. Harris gave her new motivation to campaign. ''My immediate reaction is, 'How do I get involved?''' Ms. Lucas said.\par \par \uc2 Women also pointed to societal shifts that might make Ms. Harris's run different. Dr. Liz Bradt, 64, a retired veterinarian and the chairwoman of the Salem Democratic City Committee in Salem, Mass., said younger people seemed less likely to make judgments based on rigid definitions of male and female.\par \par \uc2 ''Where my generation is like, 'Male or female, where's the check box?' I think the younger generation is more accepting of different genders,'' Dr. Bradt said. ''That will make a difference.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Dr. Bradt, who campaigned for Mrs. Clinton in New Hampshire, expects a tough road ahead for Ms. Harris. ''It's going to be hard to see what she has to go through,'' she said. ''I fear for her, like I feared for Hillary.''\par \par \uc2 Though Mrs. Clinton won the most votes in 2016, some voters said they found her off-putting. Among them was Dr. Maria E. Laurencio, 73, a retired anesthesiologist in Coral Gables, Fla., who was a lifelong Republican until she pinched her nose and voted for Mrs. Clinton.\par \par \uc2 ''Women were not sympathetic to Hillary because a lot of them said she stood by the president,'' Dr. Laurencio said about former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's extramarital affairs. ''Hillary tended to be a little arrogant and not so likable, even though she was so prepared.''\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Dr. Laurencio changed her registration to vote for Mr. Biden. Now, she intends to support Ms. Harris. ''For me, anything that prevents Mr. Trump from getting to the presidency again, I will go along with,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 And more women are now veterans of political campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Luisa Wakeman, 57, a flight attendant in suburban Cobb County, Ga., said women like her were relatively new to politics when they campaigned against Mr. Trump in 2016. Now, their informal and largely female-led networks in the area have matured into durable, battle-tested electoral machines.\par \par \uc2 ''I think like many people, I'm feeling invigorated,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 And she said she was impressed by Ms. Harris's qualifications. ''It's not just because she's a woman,'' she said, ''but I'm excited that she will make history.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Catie Edmondson in Stoughton, Wis., Eduardo Medina in Durham, N.C., and Amy Harmon and Jeremy W. Peters in New York. Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Catie Edmondson in Stoughton, Wis., Eduardo Medina in Durham, N.C., and Amy Harmon and Jeremy W. Peters in New York. Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris appears all but certain to become the Democratic nominee for president. Much has changed since 2016, including the overturning of Roe v. Wade. Democratic voters said a female nominee might improve access to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ERIN SCHAFF/THE NEW YORK TIMES; ANNA ROSE LAYDEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Having Ms. Harris atop the Democratic ticket energized some voters, but it resurfaced old fears that Donald J. Trump lost to a man (President Biden) but defeated a woman (Hillary Clinton). (PHOTOGRAPH BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A13) This article appeared in print on page A1, A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240724ek7o0003c\par }\page {\pa