\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Has Abandoned Pro-Life Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick T. Brown \par \uc2 1,237 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I am far from the only young conservative whose interest in politics was sparked by the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In high school and college, we would stake out early-morning spots on the National Mall for the annual March for Life, write postcards to our elected officials and pray rosaries outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, many of us found ourselves in the Republican Party, often picking up other conservative causes along the way -- low taxes, limited government, strong defense, border security. The bundle of preferences could be a bit ungainly at times, but we found progressive voices quick to shout down attempts for pro-lifers to work with Democrats. So the G.O.P. became home.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to feel that way now. While traditional social conservatives and the Republican Party might be allies on some key issues, it appears that it is no longer just one political party that wants us to shelve our convictions in the name of political expediency. It's both.\par \par \uc2 This shift has been a long time coming, but recent events have snapped it into focus. A secularizing America, plus the shifting composition of the Republican Party, means many G.O.P. voters are less churchgoing than prior generations. Many young conservatives, in particular, seem more enthusiastic about owning the libs than strengthening the family.\par \par \uc2 That might help explain why Republican politicians seemed so unprepared for the aftermath of the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision, which overturned Roe v. Wade. They could have prepared by focusing on politically tenable compromise and a wider array of supports for pregnant women and families. Instead, their political strategy in 2022 largely consisted of trying to change the subject, then blaming pro-lifers for poorer-than-expected midterm election results. Meanwhile the G.O.P. has been an unreliable partner in countering the state referendums that have expanded legal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Michigan, Ohio and other states.\par \par \uc2 Now a rewritten Republican Party platform rubber-stamped by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loyalists largely backtracks from its goal of protecting the unborn nationwide. Sweeping language declaring a constitutional right to life has been replaced by a somewhat garbled mention of due process, specifying that states are ''free to pass laws'' that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A mention of the traditional understanding of marriage is also gone. The platform even omits standing against taxpayer funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offering only a mention of opposing ''late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' -- a category often understood as excluding the roughly 99 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures that occur before 21 weeks of pregnancy. The new platform makes sure to include positive mentions of birth control and in vitro fertilization yet neglects any reference to pregnancy resource centers or child care.\par \par \uc2 The former president likes to encourage conservatives to place political victory over moral commitments: ''You must follow your heart on this issue, but remember, you must also win elections,'' he said in an April video.\par \par \uc2 His running mate, Senator J.D. Vance of Ohio, previously described himself as ''100 percent pro-life,'' supporting a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even making a case against exceptions for rape and incest. He later encouraged conservatives to do a better job making the ''moral argument'' against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which indeed they should. Yet this month on ''Meet the Press,'' he echoed Mr. Trump's approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, which is used in a majority of abortions nationwide. In Mr. Vance's acceptance speech Wednesday night, the unborn made no appearance at all.\par \par \uc2 There is a sound strategic case and even, perhaps, a constitutional one against pursuing a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, particularly while politics around the issue are so unsettled. Officeholders or candidates can express their skepticism that a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would be prudent or achievable, as the former governor \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  did repeatedly during her unsuccessful run for the nomination.\par \par \uc2 But there is a fine line between acknowledging politics as the art of the possible and setting aside core convictions in the pursuit of electoral success. Mr. Trump has repeatedly congratulated himself for having returned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions to the states. Yet I have found no high-profile statements from the nominee supporting state laws that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On the contrary, he criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''hardliners.'' In 2023 he called the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in his home state, Florida, a ''terrible thing.''\par \par \uc2 All the while, the number of abortions has not fallen since the Supreme Court's decision to strike down Roe, fueled in part by the rising prevalence of medication abortions. And yet at the national level, the G.O.P. seems to hope the issue will simply fade away. Eric Trump recently suggested that conservatives who are disappointed by the new party platform should ''stop worrying about the little spot on the wall in the basement'' when the country is facing the equivalent of holes in the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life organizations that insist the G.O.P. remains as pro-life today as it was in the recent past are deluding themselves. And the socially conservative groups that cheer the former president's commitment ''to the causes that millions of Americans hold so dear -- protecting life and promoting the family'' -- may be signing up for their own political obsolescence. Politics will always require some triangulation and prudence, but too much accommodation is a path to electoral irrelevance.\par \par \uc2 Many religious conservatives made common cause with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign in 2016 with an eye to the ideological makeup of the Supreme Court. He and his camp may feel that their end of the bargain has been upheld. Roe, after all, is consigned to the history books. Now, just like the G.O.P. establishment they supplanted, they seek to harness social-conservative votes for other policy ends.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life conservatives should be cleareyed about this new phase of politics. The Republican nominee seems all too content to sell out those of us who got into politics to advance policies that protect what we see as human life from its earliest stages. Pro-life groups should not be too quick to offer Mr. Trump rhetorical cover or accommodate Mr. Trump's redefinition of common sense on the issue. At this point, the best-case scenario for pro-lifers may be hoping that a second Trump administration hires some social conservatives for key administration roles -- but that is far from a given.\par \par \uc2 Getting rid of Roe was a tremendous legal victory for conservatives. Building on it will require political courage and savvy. But if it results in higher \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, conservative leaders expressing support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill and the continued diminishing of pro-life influence on the Republican Party, it will have cost a bitterly high price.\par \par \uc2 Patrick T. Brown (@PTBwrites) is a fellow at the Ethics and Public Policy Center, a conservative think tank where he writes the newsletter ''Family Matters,'' and is a former Republican Senate staff member.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY JESSE RIESER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A23. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240720ek7k0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
