\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Where Vance Stands on Key Political Issues, From  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  to the Middle East\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Nagourney \par \uc2 1,221 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Like \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the Ohio senator has been skeptical of American intervention overseas and argues that raising tariffs will create new jobs.\par \par \uc2 Senator J.D. Vance of Ohio, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's newly chosen running mate, has made a shift from the Trump critic he was when he first entered politics to the loyalist he is today. It was a shift both in style and substance: Now, on topics as disparate as trade and Ukraine, Mr. Vance is closely aligned with Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at where the senator stands on the issues that will most likely dominate the campaign ahead and, should Mr. Trump and Mr. Vance win in November, their years in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even in the case of incest or rape, but says there should be exceptions for cases when the mother's life is in danger. He praised the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision overturning Roe v. Wade. As he ran for Senate in 2022, a headline on the issues section of his campaign website read simply: ''Ban \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has said that he would support a 15-week national ban proposed by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina. He has also said the matter is ''primarily a state issue,'' suggesting states should be free to make more restrictive laws. ''Ohio is going to want to have a different \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from California, from New York, and I think that's reasonable, he said in an interview with \uc2 USA Today Network\uc2  in October 2022.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has been one of the leading opponents of U.S. support for Ukraine in the war with Russia. ''I think it's ridiculous that we're focused on this border in Ukraine,'' he said in a podcast interview with \uc2 Stephen K. Bannon\uc2 , the former Trump adviser and longtime ally. ''I've got to be honest with you, I don't really care what happens to Ukraine one way or the other.''\par \par \uc2 He led the battle in the Senate, unsuccessfully, to block a $60 billion military aid package for Ukraine. ''I voted against this package in the Senate and remain opposed to virtually any proposal for the United States to continue funding this war,'' he wrote in an opinion essay for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  early this year challenging President Biden's stance on the war. ''Mr. Biden has failed to articulate even basic facts about what Ukraine needs and how this aid will change the reality on the ground.''\par \par \uc2 Immigration\par \par \uc2 Immigration was the subject of Mr. Vance's first advertisements in his 2022 campaign for the Senate in Ohio. While most ads are written by consultants, Mr. Vance said he wrote this one himself: ''\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's open border is killing Ohioans,'' he says, looking straight into the camera. ''With more illegal drugs and more Democrat voters pouring into this country.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance's views on immigration largely echo Mr. Trump. He wants to finish construction of the border wall and proclaimed that he would ''oppose every attempt to grant amnesty'' to immigrants who arrived here illegally. He favors what he called a merit-based system for immigrants seeking to settle here.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance argues that undocumented immigrants are a source of cheap labor that undercuts wages for American-born workers in states like Ohio. ''If you cannot hire illegal migrants to staff your hotels, then you have to go to one of the seven million prime-age American men who are out of the labor force and find some way to re-engage them.''\par \par \uc2 Jan. 6\par \par \uc2 ''I think the election was stolen from Trump,'' Mr. Vance said during the Republican primary for his Ohio seat, putting him firmly in the camp of election deniers. Mr. Vance dismissed the idea that Mr. Trump played a role in instigating the demonstrators who stormed the Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021. And he said that if he had been Mr. Trump's vice president then, he would have bowed to Mr. Trump's demand and rejected electoral votes from several swing states won by Mr. Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And he is skeptical that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who hid in a stairway as members of the mob streamed through the hallways, was in danger because he refused to block, in his role as president of the Senate, the vote validating Mr. Biden's victory.\par \par \uc2 ''I think politics and politics people like to really exaggerate things from time to time,'' he said on CNN. ''Look, Jan. 6 was a bad day. It was a riot. But the idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endangered anyone's lives when he told them to protest peacefully, it's just absurd.''\par \par \uc2 Tariffs\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has called for ''broad-based tariffs, especially on goods coming in from China,'' because they pose an unfair threat to American jobs and commerce, he said. ''We need to protect American industries from all of the competition,'' he said on \uc2 CBS\uc2 's ''Face the Nation.''\par \par \uc2 In that stance, Mr. Vance is again largely in line with Mr. Trump, who has proposed a 10 percent across-the-board tariff on goods coming into the country. That idea has come under fire from Democrats and many economists, who warn that it would be inflationary. But Mr. Vance rejected the criticism as overblown.\par \par \uc2 He said in the \uc2 CBS\uc2  interview that applying tariffs is like saying ''we're going to penalize you for using slave labor in China and importing that stuff in the United States.'' And, he added: ''You end up making more stuff in America, in Pennsylvania, in Ohio and in Michigan.''\par \par \uc2 Climate\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has said climate change is not a threat and has said he is skeptical of the scientific consensus that warming of the earth's atmosphere is caused by human activity. ''It's been changing, as others pointed out, it's been changing for millennia,'' he told the American Leadership Forum. Mr. Vance is a strong supporter of the oil and gas industry -- which is dominant in his home state -- and has voiced opposition to wind and solar energy, and electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Middle East\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has been a steadfast supporter of Israel throughout the country's war in Gaza, defending its wartime policies in the face of growing criticism over the civilian death toll.\par \par \uc2 When members of the Senate considered a bill providing military aide to both Israel and Ukraine, Mr. Vance led a group of senators proposing legislation to send money only to Israel. Echoing the words of Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  of Israel, he said the country needed to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2  after the terrorist group's deadly Oct. 7 attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 ''Israel has an achievable objective,'' Mr. Vance wrote in a memo he circulated among Senate Republicans before introducing the bill. ''Ukraine does not.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance assailed President Biden for delaying the shipment of weapons to Israel as tensions grew between the White House and Mr. Netanyahu's government.\par \par \uc2 He acknowledged the civilian casualties in Gaza -- ''our heart certainly goes out to them,'' he said -- but he maintained that the blame for that lies not with Israel, but with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240718ek7h0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
