\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Pitch  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights to Latino Men as Matter of Liberty\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 1,494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 These candidates say the issue cannot be avoided in 2024, and dismiss the notion that focusing on men, who typically prioritize the economy, is a risk.\par \par \uc2 When he was a child, Representative Gabriel Vasquez never heard his parents discuss politics, much less \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a topic that was off limits in many Mexican Catholic households like his. So he can see why some might think he is taking a risk by focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in campaigning to Latino men.\par \par \uc2 For decades, Democrats saw the issue as a losing one with Latinos, who tended to be more religious and to say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in all or most cases. Some strategists still consider it a risky subject among Hispanic men, who have typically expressed more concern about the economy.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Vasquez, who represents a border district in New Mexico, and several other Hispanic male Democratic candidates said there was no avoiding the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate this year. They point to polling and Democratic electoral victories in the two years since Roe v. Wade was overturned that capture how Latinos' views on the issue have reversed.\par \par \uc2 The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken on new urgency especially for Latinas, who have been stirred by accounts of pregnant women facing life-threatening situations and having to travel thousands of miles for reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vasquez said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could resonate with Latino men as much as it has with women, because at its core is personal freedom, a value that attracts many immigrants to the United States.\par \par \uc2 ''It is not about whether we are pro-choice or pro-life. It is about trusting the people that we love to make those decisions for themselves,'' he said in an interview. His parents disagree on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added, but they ''are both pro-personal decision.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vasquez's distinctive position as a messenger was on display last month when he was the only man who spoke at an event with a group of female Democratic lawmakers and activists in his district to mark the second anniversary of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 The district, which stretches along the entire southern border of New Mexico, has become a safe haven for women seeking abortions in a region where clinics have become scarce. Oklahoma and Texas, which border New Mexico, have effectively banned the procedure. In 2023, more than 70 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in New Mexico lived outside the state, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vasquez, a former Las Cruces city councilor, first emphasized his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights during his congressional campaign in 2022, when he beat Representative \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  by less than one percentage point. For their rematch this year, Mr. Vasquez is amplifying his message and looking to portray her anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position as extreme.\par \par \uc2 His campaign's first ad this year shows footage of Ms. Herrell saying that she wished ''we could have gotten rid of all abortions.'' Before Roe was overturned, Ms. Herrell was among 166 House Republicans who signed on to legislation in 2021 that amounted to a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with no exceptions for rape, incest or the health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 In an opinion essay in The Albuquerque Journal in May, Ms. Herrell sought to soften her stance, writing that she supports such exceptions and criticizing Mr. Vasquez as ''radical,'' contending that he supports ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the moment of birth.'' Mr. Vasquez has called that claim ''absurd.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Ms. Herrell said, ''I am proudly pro-life, but this is now a state issue, which is why I would not vote for a national ban.''\par \par \uc2 Similar fights are playing out in congressional races in Arizona, California and Nebraska, where Hispanic male Democrats have sought to frame \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a matter of personal freedom and liberty. Their Republican opponents have echoed the arguments on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that their party has pushed nationwide, trying to draw contrasts between what they say are their own measured views on restrictions, even as Republican-led states increasingly propose more sweeping bans. Many Republicans also say that concerns over the economy, along with crime and immigration, are likely to prove more important.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, Tony Vargas, a former Democratic state senator, is campaigning against Representative \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2 , a Republican who supported legislation banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2021, by saying that he wants to take away ''health care freedom.'' Mr. Bacon argued in a statement that Mr. Vargas had ''consistently misrepresented'' his position, and noted that he had not backed a similar measure this year because it had not included an exception for if a mother's life was endangered.\par \par \uc2 Like Mr. Vasquez, Mr. Vargas, the son of Peruvian immigrants, recalled that his Catholic household avoided discussing politics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But as Republicans moved to curb the right in Nebraska, more physicians and women in his district urged him to protect it. They included his mother and, to his surprise, he said, his father, whom he described as a gruff, no-nonsense machinist.\par \par \uc2 ''He said, 'Look, I'm going to defer to your mother on this,''' said Mr. Vargas, who is vying to become the first Latino to represent his state. ''It was that easy for him.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats have seized on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help energize voters since Roe was overturned, fueling victories in Ohio, Kansas and Virginia, among other states. As polling has found that Latinos have helped power some of those wins, more Democratic candidates and groups have sought to tailor their pitches specifically to Hispanic men, seeing them as an untapped bloc.\par \par \uc2 Nuestro PAC, which focuses on mobilizing Latino Democrats in key states, is raising funds for a $7 million TV campaign during the soccer tournament Copa Am\u233\'00\'E9rica, with ads produced by and for Latino men on issues including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But even Chuck Rocha, a founder of the group, warns candidates against leaning too far into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the expense of other issues, particularly the economy, which polls show continues to be the priority for Latinos.\par \par \uc2 ''Latino men do care about choice,'' Mr. Rocha said. ''They do care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they also care about economic stability.''\par \par \uc2 In the Central Valley of California, Rudy Salas, a former Democratic state lawmaker, is challenging Representative David Valadao, a Republican, on his \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights record for the second time. Mr. Salas, who lost in 2022 by less than three percentage points, had initially focused that campaign on Mr. Valadao's support of the 2021 House \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban bill. But as the race drew closer, Mr. Salas shifted his emphasis to kitchen-table issues. Mr. Valadao's allies took that as a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messages were less effective in the socially conservative, majority-Latino district, and Republicans expect other issues to matter more again this year.\par \par \uc2 ''Central Valley voters will decide this race based on the skyrocketing cost of living self-serving Sacramento politician Rudy Salas inflicted on them,'' said Ben Petersen, a spokesman for the National Republican Congressional Committee, the campaign arm of House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Salas has continued to criticize Mr. Valadao's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance in ads, and in a statement he denounced his support for ''extreme legislation.'' Mr. Valadao's campaign did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 In New Mexico, Mr. Vasquez's district, where Latino voters make up 56 percent of the voting population, could offer the clearest test of the resonance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with Hispanic men. Conversations with some two dozen Hispanic male voters there captured both the advantages and challenges for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Sitting on a bench in Old Mesilla, near Las Cruces, Carlos Mu\u241\'00\'F1oz, 64, a retired auto mechanic visiting from Boise, Idaho, said there should be more Hispanic male Democrats like Mr. Vasquez daring to run on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even if an older generation of Hispanics preferred not to talk about the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Women are entitled to their own body, and they are the ones who should make the choice, not the government,'' said Mr. Mu\u241\'00\'F1oz, a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 In Las Cruces, Sergio Soto, 58, a Republican who teaches animal science at \uc2 New Mexico State University\uc2 , called the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate ''noise that politicians have exploited.'' He preferred to focus on how much money he was able to keep in his pocket, he said, and with that in mind, he voted for Ms. Herrell in 2022 but was still undecided about whether he would do so again.\par \par \uc2 Representative Gabriel Vasquez, above, represents a border district in New Mexico that has become a safe haven for women seeking abortions in a region where clinics are scarce. He was the only man speaking at an event that observed the second anniversary of the end of Roe, with Representative Teresa Leger Fernandez, left, and other Democratic lawmakers and activists, below. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY RAMSAY DE GIVE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A9. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240715ek7f0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
