\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Arkansas Secretary of State Rejects Submissions For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Rights Amendment on Technicality\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Arkansas secretary of state said that the group collecting signatures to put an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights amendment on the ballot had failed to submit some of the necessary paperwork.\par \par \uc2 The Arkansas secretary of state on Wednesday rejected an effort to put an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights amendment on the ballot this November, saying the campaign had failed to submit some of the necessary paperwork.\par \par \uc2 Less than a week ago, organizers said they had successfully submitted more than 101,000 signatures in support of the effort, a few thousand more than the required minimum. Last Friday, the day of the deadline, they triumphantly dropped off boxes of petitions, cheering in the halls of the State Capitol.\par \par \uc2 But in a letter on Wednesday, John Thurston, the secretary of state, said the group had failed to submit a sworn statement both identifying paid canvassers by name and confirming that those canvassers had been properly educated on how to collect signatures.\par \par \uc2 ''The first part of our review is to ensure that the sponsor has complied with all statutory requirements for submitting a petition,'' Mr. Thurston wrote. ''Because you failed at this first step, it is my duty to reject your submission.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Thurston added that if he had eliminated the signatures collected by paid canvassers, that would have dropped the total signatures to 87,382, below the mandated 90,704 minimum.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the group, Arkansans for Limited Government, said its legal team was reviewing the letter.\par \par \uc2 In a statement later Wednesday, the group said that it had provided the necessary information to the secretary of state's office and called Mr. Thurston's decision ''an unfounded legal interpretation.''\par \par \uc2 ''We will fight this ridiculous disqualification attempt with everything we have,'' the group said. ''We will not back down.''\par \par \uc2 The effort had already faced steep opposition, and Arkansas remains one of the few states in the country where polls show that only a minority of voters support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, a sentiment driven by a deeply conservative and evangelical voting base.\par \par \uc2 The ballot initiative would have allowed for abortions up to 18 weeks after fertilization and exceptions after that time for rape, incest and instances where the fetus would not survive outside of the womb. Current law in the state allows for abortions only to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency, though no abortions were reported to have been performed in the state last year.\par \par \uc2 Arkansans for Limited Government had sought to appeal to libertarians and centrists wary of government overreach, and compromise on how far their proposed amendment would go in undoing existing law. And while national groups have not publicly embraced the group's push because it does not go up to 24 weeks like most other measures, it united several doctors and women frustrated by the state's ban.\par \par \uc2 Its opponents included an alliance of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and key allies of Gov. \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 , a Republican.\par \par \uc2 ''Today the far left pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crowd in Arkansas showed they are both immoral and incompetent,'' Ms. Sanders said in a social media post.\par \par \uc2 In his letter, Mr. Thurston noted that other ballot initiative groups had correctly submitted the right paperwork, though he did not specify which groups. Other initiatives that could be on the ballot and that relied on paid canvassers include one that would loosen medical marijuana regulations in the state.\par \par \uc2 ''As I have long said, changing the Arkansas Constitution involves a rigorous process, as it should, and it requires sponsors to adhere to all applicable laws and rules,'' said Attorney General Tim Griffin in a statement. He added: ''Failure to follow such a basic requirement is inexcusable: the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have no one to blame but themselves.''\par \par \uc2 With polls showing that a majority of Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have fought hard against citizen-sponsored ballot amendments that would establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state constitutions. They have run ''decline to sign'' campaigns in several states, tried to raise the threshold for approval of the amendments, and aggressively attempted to declare signatures invalid.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, organizers on Wednesday sued over efforts to invalidate some signatures, accusing the secretary of state of ''unlawfully blocking the verification of potentially thousands of valid signatures by qualified Montana electors, defeating the constitutional rights of Montanans entitled to have their signatures counted.''\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240711ek7b00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
