\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices in Kansas Deal a Setback To G.O.P.-Backed  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Limits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith \par \uc2 587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The rulings were the latest blow for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents in Kansas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the State Constitution have vexed Republicans for years.\par \par \uc2 The Kansas Supreme Court reaffirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the state's Constitution on Friday, striking down Republican-backed laws that banned a common second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure and created additional licensing requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''The state devoted much of its brief to inviting us to reverse our earlier ruling in this case that the Kansas Constitution protects a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Justice Eric Rosen wrote in one of the majority opinions. ''We decline the invitation.'' Justice Rosen, like five of the court's seven members, was appointed by a Democratic governor.\par \par \uc2 The rulings were the latest setbacks for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents in Kansas, a conservative state that has been the setting for some of the country's most important and divisive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debates. Most recently, in a closely watched vote in 2022, just after the fall of Roe v. Wade, Kansans rejected an attempt to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections from the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The decisions on Friday permanently blocked enforcement of a 2015 law that banned dilation and evacuation, the most common form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in pregnancy, and permanently blocked laws that created special licensing requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. Each of the rulings was 5 to 1. The majority included four justices appointed by Democrats and one justice appointed by a Republican. One Republican appointee dissented, and one Democratic appointee did not participate.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Danielle Underwood, a spokeswoman for Kansans for Life, said, ''Extremely liberal judges of the Kansas Supreme Court have now overturned basic health and safety standards for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities.''\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights celebrated the decisions.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an immense victory for the health, safety and dignity of people in Kansas and the entire Midwestern region, where millions have been cut off from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president and chief executive of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Though Republicans dominate the Kansas Legislature and routinely carry the state in presidential elections, the state's politics are nuanced.\par \par \uc2 Voters have for decades alternated between electing Republican and Democratic governors, and Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2 , a Democrat in her second term, supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Republicans win by overwhelming margins in many rural areas, but the state's most populous county, Johnson County in suburban Kansas City, has moved rapidly toward the Democrats over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Ms. Kelly did not immediately respond to a request for comment. Attorney General \uc2 Kris W. Kobach\uc2 , a Republican whose office defended the laws in court, said in a statement that ''the decision is as disappointing as it is unsurprising'' and criticized the justices for finding that the State Constitution included a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe in 2022, Kansas has increasingly become a destination for women from other states seeking abortions. Two bordering states, Missouri and Oklahoma, ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly all cases, and a third, Nebraska, has passed new limits.\par \par \uc2 Estimates compiled by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, showed a 206 percent increase in abortions in Kansas between 2019 and 2023, the third-highest increase of any state. Of the more than 20,000 women who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas in 2023, the estimates showed, 69 percent came from another state.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240706ek760003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
