\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Tense Term, Fractured Court Remade Nation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak and Alicia Parlapiano \par \uc2 1,821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amid signs of dysfunction and disarray, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  reasserted his authority, while the influence of Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  waned.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald J. Trump had a very good year at the Supreme Court. On Monday, the court ruled that he is substantially immune from prosecution on charges that he tried to subvert the 2020 election. On Friday, the court cast doubt on two of the four charges against him in what remains of that prosecution. And in March, the justices allowed him to seek another term despite a constitutional provision barring insurrectionists from holding office.\par \par \uc2 Administrative agencies had a horrible term. In three 6-to-3 rulings along ideological lines, the court's conservative supermajority erased a foundational precedent that had required courts to defer to agency expertise, dramatically lengthened the time available to challenge agencies' actions and torpedoed the administrative tribunals in which the Securities and Exchange Commission brings enforcement actions.\par \par \uc2 The court itself had a volatile term, taking on a stunning array of major disputes and assuming a commanding role in shaping American society and democracy. If the justices felt chastened by the backlash over their 2022 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, the persistent questions about their ethical standards and the drop in their public approval, there were only glimmers of restraint, notably in ducking two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases in an election year.\par \par \uc2 The court was divided 6 to 3 along partisan lines not only in Monday's decision on Mr. Trump's immunity and the three cases on agency power, but also in a run of major cases on homelessness, voting rights, guns and public corruption.\par \par \uc2 An unusually high proportion of divided decisions in argued cases -- more than two-thirds -- were decided by 6-to-3 votes. But only half of those decisions featured the most common split, with the six Republican appointees in the majority and the three Democratic ones in dissent.\par \par \uc2 The justices reached unanimous or lopsided rulings in other major cases, including ones letting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills remain widely available, allowing the government to disarm domestic abusers, restoring Mr. Trump to the Colorado ballot, endorsing the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2 's First Amendment rights and rejecting a challenge to the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Irv Gornstein, the executive director of Georgetown's Supreme Court Institute, said the court's liberals by some measures had a good term.\par \par \uc2 ''But most of those wins are an artifact of so many cases coming from the law-free Fifth Circuit,'' he said, referring to the federal appeals court based in New Orleans. ''The judges in that circuit seem to have some kind of competition to see who can write the most precedent-twisting, common-sense-defying decision.''\par \par \uc2 Pamela Karlan\uc2 , a law professor at Stanford, agreed, saying that ''the Fifth Circuit is making the Supreme Court seem more moderate than it is.''\par \par \uc2 Even when the justices agreed, though, they very often could not find consensus on the rationale. Indeed, they issued concurring opinions at a record rate, the highest since at least 1937 and probably ever. Some of those opinions revealed fractures on the right, particularly on the role history should play in constitutional interpretation.\par \par \uc2 Gregory G. Garre, a lawyer with \uc2 Latham & Watkins\uc2  who served as U.S. solicitor general in the administration of \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , said that ''there are signs of dysfunction'' among the justices.\par \par \uc2 ''The court is taking an extraordinarily small number of cases,'' he said, ''and taking an extraordinarily long time to decide them. And the justices are writing more and more individual opinions to express their own views. This is especially pronounced on the right side of the court and has to create some friction among the justices.''\par \par \uc2 There was a sense of disarray as the term ended. On Wednesday, the court briefly posted and then promptly withdrew an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision that would not be formally issued until the next day.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, it made 13 separate corrections to four sets of opinions. In one of them, blocking a Biden administration plan to combat air pollution, Justice \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  had repeatedly referred to nitrogen oxide as nitrous oxide.\par \par \uc2 That confusion, Professor Karlan said, ''would be just funny (in a laughing-gas sort of way) if the court weren't simultaneously kneecapping expert agencies that do know the difference.''\par \par \uc2 A look at how individual justices voted in divided cases issued after oral arguments brings trends at the court into sharp relief, according to data compiled and analyzed by Lee Epstein and Andrew D. Martin, both of \uc2 Washington University in St. Louis\uc2 , and Michael J. Nelson of \uc2 Penn State\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By that measure, the court is extraordinarily polarized. Two of the four most conservative justices to serve since 1937 are on the current court: Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  (The others were Chief Justices William H. Rehnquist and Warren E. Burger.)\par \par \uc2 In that same time span, two of the five most liberal justices are currently sitting: Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . (The others were Justices Thurgood Marshall, William J. Brennan Jr. and William O. Douglas.)\par \par \uc2 Overall, in divided cases argued in the last term, Democratic appointees voted for a liberal result 83 percent of the time and Republican ones 33 percent of the time -- a 50 percentage point gap.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr.\uc2 's leadership of the court was called into question in 2022 by his lonely concurring opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, one in which he failed to persuade any of his five conservative colleagues to join him in restricting but not eliminating the constitutional rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two years later, things are looking up for the chief justice. He assigned himself an unusually large proportion of the term's majority opinions in the biggest cases, including the ones on Mr. Trump's immunity from prosecution, the Jan. 6 prosecutions, the Second Amendment, the \uc2 Chevron\uc2  doctrine and administrative tribunals.\par \par \uc2 The chief justice was in the majority in divided cases 94 percent of the time, more than any other member of the court and tying his own record in the term that ended in 2020. No other chief justice since at least 1953 has been in the majority as often.\par \par \uc2 But it is Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  who has been setting the pace over time. Since he joined the court in 2018, he has been in the majority 89 percent of the time, a higher rate than any justice since at least 1953.\par \par \uc2 It was not long ago that some heralded Justice Thomas as the court's true leader, but this term's data rebuts that idea. He was, for instance, in the majority in divided cases just 63 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 Last term, Justice Alito was part of the pair most likely to disagree, voting with Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  just 21 percent of the time. This term, Justice Thomas was part of both pairs most likely to disagree, voting with Justices Sotomayor and Kagan just 9 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 At the other end of the spectrum, the two justices most likely to vote together were members of the court's liberal wing, Justices Kagan and Sotomayor, at 94 percent.\par \par \uc2 A look at the three justices most apt to vote together in divided cases confirms two bits of conventional wisdom and dispels a third. The court's three liberals -- Justices Sotomayor, Kagan and Jackson -- voted together 81 percent of the time, more than any other combination of three justices. They were trailed by three Republican appointees often said to represent the court's middle -- Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Kavanaugh and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  -- at 75 percent.\par \par \uc2 But agreement among the remaining three justices, who are often lumped together as the court's hard-right wing -- Justices Thomas, Alito and Gorsuch -- was much lower, at 59 percent. That refutes the story of a 3-3-3 court and highlights Justice Gorsuch's independence.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, he voted for liberal results in divided cases 45 percent of the time, often siding against the government and in favor of powerless litigants. Since he joined the court in 2017, he has voted for the government just 35 percent of the time, the lowest rate of any member of the court.\par \par \uc2 When Justices Thomas and Alito were both in the majority in divided cases, Justice Gorsuch voted with them less often than any other member of the court's conservative wing.\par \par \uc2 Put another way, none of the three members of the court appointed by Mr. Trump are as conservative as Justices Thomas and Alito.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett, the third Trump appointee, is particularly worth watching, Professor Epstein said. ''Some indicators show that Barrett -- though still way more conservative than her predecessor, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  -- is moving somewhat to the left,'' she said. ''This term she overtook Roberts as the Republican appointee casting the highest percentage of liberal votes in divided cases.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has expressed disappointment with his choices, and he may be inclined to nominate more extreme justices, perhaps drawing from the Fifth Circuit, should he gain another term. The four oldest members of the court are Justice Thomas, 76; Justice Alito, 74; Justice Sotomayor, 70; and Chief Justice Roberts, 69.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Trump was president, his administration did quite poorly in the Supreme Court in signed decisions in orally argued cases in which the United States, an executive department, an independent agency or the president himself was a party, prevailing only 42 percent of the time, the lowest rate since at least Franklin D. Roosevelt's administration. The Biden administration, by contrast, has been on the winning side 54 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 The term that ended on Monday was studded with more potential blockbusters than any in recent memory. The court defused a few of them, but the term ended with a series of earth-rattling explosions.\par \par \uc2 When the justices return in October, they will face what is, for now at least, a more usual docket. Among the cases the court has agreed to decide are ones on transgender care for minors and so-called ghost guns.\par \par \uc2 There is little reason to think the court will find consensus in those cases.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garre said the quarrels among the justices, and particularly the conservative ones, called to mind a remark ascribed to Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr., who is said to have compared the Supreme Court to ''nine scorpions in a bottle.''\par \par \uc2 ''Chief Justice Rehnquist once described the job of chief as akin to herding cats,'' Mr. Garre said. ''To pick up on Holmes's saying, maybe the better analogy these days is herding scorpions.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court term that ended on Monday was studded with more potential blockbuster rulings than any in recent memory. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A1, A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240703ek730003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
