\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Nevada Residents to Vote On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in November\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Taft \par \uc2 922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A measure seeking to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the State Constitution will appear on the ballot. It is one of nearly a dozen such initiatives that could shape other races this election.\par \par \uc2 Nevada residents will vote on whether to protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state this November, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups try to continue their winning streak with measures that put the issue directly before voters.\par \par \uc2 The Nevada secretary of state's office certified on Friday the ballot initiative to amend the State Constitution to include an explicit right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after verifying the signatures required. The group behind the measure, Nevadans for Reproductive Freedom, submitted 200,000 signatures in May, nearly 100,000 more than needed. The secretary of state's office told the group that it had verified just under 128,000 signatures.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's Dobbs ruling in 2022, which overturned Roe v. Wade and stripped the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 18 Republican-controlled states have banned the procedure in almost all circumstances or prohibited it after six weeks, before many women know they are pregnant. At least a dozen states, most of them led by Democrats, have passed new protections to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the decision.\par \par \uc2 The ruling has sparked a movement among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to enshrine the right to the procedure in state constitutions through ballot measures. They have been successful in putting them on the ballot in at least five other states this year: Florida, Colorado, New York, Maryland and South Dakota. Similar initiatives are also underway in states like Arizona, Arkansas and Nebraska -- which all face deadlines to submit signatures this week -- and come November, voters in as many as 11 states could get a chance to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal through 24 weeks of pregnancy. But organizers of the ballot initiative are seeking to amend the State Constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point of fetal viability -- also around 24 weeks -- because it is harder to change the Constitution than repeal state law.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't take anything for granted,'' said Lindsey Harmon, president of Nevadans for Reproductive Freedom. ''We know Nevada has always been overwhelmingly pro-choice, and there's no reason it should not be in the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendments that passed in Ohio and Michigan, the Nevada measure would allow the state to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, except if in the ''good faith judgment'' of the treating physician, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to ''protect the life or physical or mental health'' of the pregnant patient.\par \par \uc2 After motivating Republican voters for decades, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a winning issue for Democrats since the fall of Roe, who hope the ballot measures this year will drive turnout on Election Day and boost their odds up and down the ticket. Aside from key swing states like Arizona and Florida, Democrats also hope the measure in Montana -- which has yet to be certified but has gathered signatures well over the threshold -- will help Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 's competitive re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 Polls show a majority of people in most states say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. Voters in seven states have already considered ballot measures related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and in every case -- from states as politically different as California and Kansas -- they have sided with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups.\par \par \uc2 Krystal Minera-Alvis, communications director for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Nevada Right to Life, acknowledged the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's losses in those ballot initiatives.\par \par \uc2 ''Right now we're kind of 0 for 7,'' she said. ''We're not doing too great.''\par \par \uc2 Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is particularly strong in Nevada, another battleground state. According to the Public Religion Research Institute, 76 percent of residents are in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and according to a recent Emerson College poll, 58 percent of Nevadans said they would vote yes on the ballot measure. For the constitutional amendment to take effect, voters would have to approve it both this November and in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Minerva-Alvis said her group's primary goal is to get the word out about Nevada's existing law, which also allows abortions after 24 weeks if a physician deems it necessary to protect the pregnant patient's life or health. ''If the average Nevadan finds out what the law already is, what's already legalized, they would not vote for it.''\par \par \uc2 Among the races in Nevada is one for a Senate seat, in which the incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  is leading her Republican opponent, Sam Brown. Ms. Rosen has expressed support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, saying it ensures that ''every Nevadan can make their own health care decisions, free from government interference.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown, an Army veteran who has been endorsed by former President Donald J. Trump, has sought to position himself as a moderate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He has said he would not seek to change Nevada's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, nor support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Minera-Alvis said she and others in her movement would like him to be more assertive, and ''have more of a pro-life voice'' by pushing for a lower gestational limit in the future.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't take anything for granted in a post-Dobbs world,'' said Lindsey Harmon, president of Nevadans for Reproductive Freedom. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOHN LOCHER/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240701ek7100040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
