\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Social Conservatives Pressure Trump to Not Yield on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Goldmacher and Jonathan Swan \par \uc2 1,273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement sent a letter to Donald J. Trump amid worries he may try to weaken the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language in the party platform.\par \par \uc2 A group of some of the most powerful social conservatives in the country, fearful that Donald J. Trump may push to water down the Republican Party's official position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sent a pointed letter to the former president this month imploring him to keep strong anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language in the party platform.\par \par \uc2 The letter, which has not previously been reported but was reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , is the latest sign of the fierce behind-the-scenes lobbying underway over the language that will officially outline the party's principles. The Republican platform has not been updated in eight years and is especially outdated on the topic of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The letter urges Mr. Trump to ''make clear that you do not intend to weaken the pro-life plank.'' Specifically, it asks him to commit to keeping language in the platform that the party supports a ''human life amendment to the Constitution'' and legislation to ''make clear that the 14th Amendment's protections apply to children before birth.''\par \par \uc2 It was co-signed by 10 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders, including Marjorie Dannenfelser of \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2  of the Faith and Freedom Coalition, \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2  of the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2  and Penny Nance of Concerned Women for America. Ms. Dannenfelser delivered the letter via email to Mr. Trump's top adviser, Susie Wiles, on June 10, as the party prepares to hold its national convention in Milwaukee starting July 15.\par \par \uc2 While the conservative leaders praise Mr. Trump as ''the most pro-life president in American history,'' the subtext of their letter is the growing concern in social conservative circles that Mr. Trump may try to weaken the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language in the party platform to make himself appear more moderate on the issue. Democrats are expected to spend hundreds of millions of dollars over the next four months reminding voters of Mr. Trump's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  record as president.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Perkins, who is on the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 's platform committee, said that it was crucial for the party not to backtrack in its platform and that the Trump team had not sufficiently explained its plans to leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 ''This has probably been the worst communication we've had of any preconvention R.N.C. or campaign that I've been involved with,'' Mr. Perkins said in an interview. He warned that diluting language related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''because now it's once again somewhat politically tough'' would incur its own backlash. ''It is not going to sit well with the pro-life voters,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Mr. Trump, Danielle Alvarez, said the platform committee had yet to convene to discuss what language should be in the final document and the campaign had sent a message to platform members about its plans to present ''a streamlined platform in line with President Trump's principled and popular vision for America's future.''\par \par \uc2 Heightening the concerns of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders is the fact that the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  has decided to hold the meeting of its platform committee behind closed doors in Milwaukee a week before the convention. Past platform meetings dating back to at least 1984 have been televised, according to \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  archives. After those meetings next month, the full convention would then ratify the platform the following week.\par \par \uc2 Some conservative activists who have asked to attend as guests have been told they will not be allowed, including representatives of the Eagle Forum, which was founded by the social conservative leader \uc2 Phyllis Schlafly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is something that we've done for decades,'' said Tabitha Walter, the Eagle Forum's executive director. ''It's not that we want to disrupt their meetings. We want the delegates to feel supported and knowledgeable.''\par \par \uc2 Her group is among those pushing to keep what is known as the personhood amendment in the platform. It states that the G.O.P. believes the equal protections guaranteed by the 14th Amendment apply to fetuses.\par \par \uc2 ''There is intense concern about this plank of the platform -- it's brought up on a daily basis,'' Ms. Walter said. If Mr. Trump excludes the personhood amendment or waters it down, she said, ''that would alienate a good portion of the base of his voters.''\par \par \uc2 She continued, ''A lot of voters in the Republican Party make that their only issue they vote on.''\par \par \uc2 In the two years since the Supreme Court that Mr. Trump transformed decided to overturn Roe, he has grown ever more convinced that hard-line \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are electoral poison. Mr. Trump has long approached \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of political transaction rather than of morality. He supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for most of his adult life. Then he announced in early 2011 that he was anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as he considered running for president in the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 He won over skeptical social conservatives in the 2016 campaign by promising to implement anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and appoint justices to the Supreme Court who would overturn Roe. He fulfilled both promises and delighted evangelicals.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump is attempting yet another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebrand ahead of the 2024 election -- this time a swerve back to the left. He has rejected some calls by social conservatives for a national 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and he has attacked the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban passed by Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, calling it a ''terrible mistake.''\par \par \uc2 Now that he is the presumptive nominee, Mr. Trump settled on leaving the decision of how to legislate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits up to the states.\par \par \uc2 ''You have to get elected,'' he said during his debate on Thursday with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But the previous party platform, which Mr. Trump ran on in 2016 and 2020, embraces legislation that would impose a 20-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 When discussing potential vice-presidential candidates, Mr. Trump has often asked privately whether they are ''OK on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' He dismissed as unelectable any Republican who he heard did not support ''the three exceptions'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions -- in cases of rape or incest, or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to The Times, Ms. Dannenfelser, one of the co-signers of the June 10 letter, emphasized that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement remained ''all-in'' on electing Mr. Trump. She said they were undertaking a massive voter outreach program in battleground states to contrast Mr. Trump's message on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against President Biden's.\par \par \uc2 ''Our only request is that the G.O.P. platform retains a key principle as it has for 40 years asserting a constitutional right to life for the unborn under the 14th Amendment,'' Ms. Dannenfelser said in her statement.\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , president of Students for Life of America, who was not among the letter signers, wants the platform to explicitly call \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a state and federal issue.\par \par \uc2 ''He believes it is merely a states issue now,'' she said of Mr. Trump. ''That is deeply wrong. State lines should not determine whether you have human rights.''\par \par \uc2 Her organization is already lobbying R.N.C. members for stricter language.\par \par \uc2 ''In 2016, we had a deal from the president that he would'' appoint pro-life judges ''and he kept his word,'' she said. ''It is time the pro-life movement has a new deal.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240629ek6t00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
