\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Temporarily Opens Door to Emergency Abortions in Idaho\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 1,338 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A majority of the justices voted to dismiss the case, reinstating a lower-court ruling that paused the state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The ruling mirrored a version inadvertently posted a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court said on Thursday that it would dismiss a case about emergency abortions in Idaho, temporarily clearing the way for women in the state to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when their health is at risk.\par \par \uc2 The one-sentence, unsigned decision declared that the case had been ''improvidently granted,'' meaning a majority of the justices had changed their minds about the need to take up the case now. It reinstates a lower-court ruling that had halted Idaho's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and permitted emergency abortions at hospitals if needed to protect the health of the mother while the case makes its way through the courts.\par \par \uc2 The decision, which did not rule on the substance of the case, closely mirrored a version that appeared briefly on the court's website a day earlier and was reported by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 . A court spokeswoman acknowledged on Wednesday that the publications unit had ''inadvertently and briefly uploaded a document'' and said a ruling in the case would appear in due time.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr.\uc2  announced the court's decision from the bench, as is the custom for unsigned opinions.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , who in part disagreed with the court's decision and asserted that the justices should have addressed the case on its merits, read her dissent from the bench. Such a move is rare and signals profound disagreement.\par \par \uc2 The joined cases, Moyle v. United States and Idaho v. United States, focus on whether a federal law aimed at ensuring emergency care for any patient supersedes Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, one of the nation's strictest. The state outlaws the procedure with few exceptions unless a woman's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 The decision was essentially 6 to 3, with three conservative justices siding with the liberal wing, albeit with separate writings and reasoning, in saying they would drop the case.\par \par \uc2 The dispute was the first time the court grappled with the question of statewide restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many of which swiftly took effect after the court eliminated a constitutional right to the procedure two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The ruling handed a temporary victory to the Biden administration, which had turned to the federal law as one of the few, if narrow, ways to challenge state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and preserve access after the court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 It also amounted to a second win, however muted, for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in recent weeks. This month, the court rejected a challenge to the longstanding approval of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, saying that an umbrella group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medical organizations and doctors bringing the case lacked standing to sue. Even as the decision preserved availability of the pill, the court did not rule on the merits of the case.\par \par \uc2 Still, just as with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, the case involving emergency abortions -- and the underlying question of the state versus federal law -- will continue in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights pointed to that possibility even as they welcomed the outcome.\par \par \uc2 ''We are relieved for the moment, but hardly celebrating,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president and chief executive of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding, ''Women with dire pregnancy complications and the hospital staff who care for them need clarity right now.''\par \par \uc2 The attorney general of Idaho, \uc2 Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Labrador\uc2 , said during a news conference that he remained undeterred. ''We feel pretty strongly that we're going to win this case in the end,'' he said, adding that he expected the lawsuit or a parallel case in Texas could reach the justices again.\par \par \uc2 The decision, arriving hours before the first presidential debate, underlined the stakes for the coming election, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a priority for both parties. Access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is broadly popular, and the issue has galvanized voters eager to overturn antiabortion referendums at the polls.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, President Biden celebrated the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's Supreme Court order ensures that women in Idaho can access the emergency medical care they need while this case returns to the lower courts,'' Mr. Biden said. ''No woman should be denied care, made to wait until she's near death or forced to flee her home state just to receive the health care she needs.''\par \par \uc2 Idaho had asked the justices to intervene after an 11-member panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit temporarily blocked the law. In agreeing to hear the case, the justices had temporarily reinstated the ban.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal except in cases of incest, rape, some instances of nonviable pregnancies or when it is ''necessary to prevent the death of the pregnant woman.'' Doctors who perform abortions could face criminal penalties, prison time and loss of their licenses to practice medicine.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had asserted that the ban conflicted with federal law and that the federal law should override it. Idaho contended that the Biden administration had improperly interpreted the federal law in an effort to bypass state bans, effectively turning hospitals into legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sites.\par \par \uc2 The liberal justices, along with Justices \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr.\uc2 , all wrote or joined in concurring opinions. The court's remaining conservatives, Justices \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , dissented.\par \par \uc2 In her partial agreement and partial dissent, Justice Jackson wrote that she would have decided on the substance of the case and that the federal law at issue, known as the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, overrides Idaho's strict ban.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , in a concurring opinion, said the practical outcome of the court's decision would forestall harrowing consequences.\par \par \uc2 The federal law, she wrote, ''unambiguously requires'' that hospitals receiving Medicare funding provide whatever treatment is necessary to stabilize a patient, including pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jackson agreed. When the court allowed Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to temporarily go into effect, a ''monthslong catastrophe'' ensued that could have been averted, she wrote. Instead, she noted, ''Idaho physicians were forced to step back and watch as their patients suffered, or arrange for their patients to be airlifted out.''\par \par \uc2 Dismissing the case, she warned, was cause for concern, a path that simply allowed the court ''to avoid issues that it does not wish to decide.''\par \par \uc2 ''There is simply no good reason not to resolve this conflict now,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Even as Justice Alito sided with Justice Jackson in saying that the court should have heard the case on its merits, he came to the opposite conclusion as her. The Idaho \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban applied to emergency room care, he wrote.\par \par \uc2 To the contrary, he added, the federal law requires hospitals receiving Medicare funding ''to treat, not abort, an 'unborn child.'''\par \par \uc2 He expressed regret that the court lacked the desire to wrestle with a polarizing issue.\par \par \uc2 ''That question is as ripe for decision as it ever will be,'' Justice Alito wrote. ''Apparently, the court has simply lost the will to decide the easy but emotional and highly politicized question that the case presents.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett, joined by Chief Justice Roberts and Justice Kavanaugh, appeared to chart something of a middle ground.\par \par \uc2 The case should play out, for now, in the lower courts, she wrote, where a more complete picture of the facts could emerge.\par \par \uc2 The parameters of the Idaho law had ''significantly changed -- twice'' since the lawsuit began, she added, and the parties' positions had ''rendered the scope of the dispute unclear, at best.''\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2  rights and anti-\uc2  demonstrators outside the Supreme Court in April during oral arguments on Idaho's ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240628ek6s00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
