\par\par \uc2 SIDEBAR\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 'Friends' Seek to Sway Justices on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 . One Side Is Winning.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new study analyzed 50 years of friend-of-the-court briefs and found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents were more relentless than their adversaries, with some reflected in the justices' opinions.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court decided Roe v. Wade in 1973, establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it noted that it had received 14 friend-of-the-court briefs and listed them in a snug footnote at the beginning of the decision.\par \par \uc2 By 1992, when the court reaffirmed Roe's core holding in Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the number of such filings, which lawyers call amicus briefs, had swelled to more than 30, and the footnote reciting them had grown unwieldy, taking up more than a page.\par \par \uc2 In the decision that overturned Roe in 2022, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the court was flooded with more than 140 amicus briefs. The footnote had metastasized, spanning seven pages.\par \par \uc2 Those 50 years of amicus briefs tell a cumulative story, one explored in a new study published in The Missouri Law Review, ''The Rhetoric of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Amicus Briefs.'' Using corpus linguistics, a social-science tool that analyzes patterns of words in large databases, the study found that the briefs ''serve as a barometer revealing how various constituencies talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women, fetuses, physicians, rights and harms over time.''\par \par \uc2 The study, conducted by Jamie R. Abrams, a law professor at \uc2 American University\uc2 , and Amanda Potts, who teaches at \uc2 Cardiff University\uc2 , concluded that opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had in some ways been more effective, remaining ''resolutely intent on advancing fetal personhood.'' The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefs were nimble, they wrote, and were ''able to adapt and evolve in response to doctrinal shifts of the court.''\par \par \uc2 Overall, the authors wrote, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had pressed ''a more relentlessly human, emotional, personal attack to pursue its political agenda.''\par \par \uc2 The authors, self-described feminist scholars, wrote that supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ''simply could not counter these arguments within conventional advocacy strategies.''\par \par \uc2 None of this means that the many scores of amicus briefs were particularly influential. Indeed, the sheer number of briefs probably diluted the power of any individual one.\par \par \uc2 ''Normally I didn't even read amicus briefs,'' Justice \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2  told me in 2011, a year after he retired and a few years before he died in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , who died in 2016, said something similar in 2011 at Chicago-Kent College of Law.\par \par \uc2 ''My law clerks read all amicus briefs,'' he said. ''If there's one that has a hidden truffle in there somewhere, they call it to my attention.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Allison Orr Larsen, a law professor at William & Mary who has written extensively on amicus briefs, said some of the new study's findings ''highlight for me a problem in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  litigation that I think applies in amicus practice more generally: More amici than ever before claim to be factual experts, but in reality these 'experts' are motivated by advocacy and masked in neutral-sounding names.''\par \par \uc2 For example, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported last week that a questionable survey on gun safety cited in amicus briefs made its way into a concurring opinion from Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  in a major Second Amendment case in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Something similar happened in a 2007 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, Gonzales v. Carhart, which upheld the federal Partial-Birth \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban Act. Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , writing for the majority, supplemented his legal analysis with an observation drawn from an amicus brief.\par \par \uc2 ''While we find no reliable data to measure the phenomenon, it seems unexceptionable to conclude some women come to regret their choice to abort the infant life they once created and sustained,'' he wrote, citing a brief filed by \uc2 The Justice Foundation\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 In a 2008 article in The Duke Law Journal, Reva Siegel, a law professor at Yale, said the passage was remarkable.\par \par \uc2 ''The woman-protective argument that appears in Carhart seems to have entered the case not through findings of Congress or the lower courts, but rather through amicus briefs filed in the Supreme Court,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 That phenomenon is troubling, Professor Larsen said. ''The end result is that the studies and statistics and history presented by these groups are selected by people with a dog in the fight and presented to the justices without any serious adversarial testing or fact-checking,'' she said. ''It is a system ripe for abuse and risks tainting Supreme Court decisions with unreliable evidence.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court heard two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases this term, one on whether emergency rooms in states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans must sometimes perform the procedure and the other on whether to limit the availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. There were, as usual, scores of amicus briefs, though not as many as were filed in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 The first case will probably be decided this week. In the second, the court unanimously ruled earlier this month that the doctors and medical groups challenging the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the pills lacked standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Professor Abrams said that passages in Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's concurring opinion in that case reflected a rhetorical trend in amicus briefs by groups opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which she said have ''increasingly demonized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  caregivers over time from Roe to Dobbs.''\par \par \uc2 She pointed to a sentence that managed, she said, to use four loaded words in quick succession.\par \par \uc2 ''Just as abortionists lack standing to assert the rights of their clients,'' Justice Thomas wrote, ''doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot vicariously assert the rights of their patients.''\par \par \uc2 Professor Abrams reviewed the bidding. ''So-called 'abortionists' have 'clients,''' she said, ''and so-called 'doctors' who are opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have 'patients.'''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  activists in Washington in 1981. The Supreme Court has received hundreds of friend-of-the-court briefs about \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY HERBERT K. WHITE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240625ek6p0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
