\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 On Anniversary of Roe's End, Democrats Press  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Message\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,227 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Across the country this week, Democrats are focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues and emphasizing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's role in nominating the justices who helped overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Two years after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, unleashing a cascade of state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and prompting an angry political backlash, Democrats are marking the anniversary by highlighting the role former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  played in ending the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Through advertising, campaign events and news conferences, Democrats at every level of the party are fanning out across the country, working to remind voters that it was justices nominated by Mr. Trump who helped overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the sole person responsible for this nightmare,'' President Biden said in a statement on Monday, also filming a video for social media. ''My message to Americans is this: Kamala and I are fighting like hell to get your freedom back.''\par \par \uc2 The messaging push is unfolding during a tight presidential race, as Mr. Biden confronts weak approval ratings and the coalition that propelled his 2020 victory shows signs of fraying.\par \par \uc2 As they seek to reinvigorate their voters, Democrats are embracing variations of arguments that have fueled other victories in the past two years: that the Republican Party is ever more extreme and infringing, to an extraordinary degree, on some of the most personal health care decisions Americans can make.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hand-picked three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  because he intended for them to overturn Roe v. Wade -- and as he intended, they did,'' Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said at a campaign event on Monday in College Park, Md. ''In the case of the stealing of reproductive freedom from the women of America, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is guilty.''\par \par \uc2 She was also expected to speak in Phoenix to ''remind voters that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is responsible for overturning Roe and the chaos that has followed,'' and to ''highlight the threat a second Trump presidency would pose to reproductive freedom nationwide,'' according to the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 Her husband, Doug Emhoff, was headed to Flint and Clawson, Mich., with a similar message, and top Biden surrogates around the nation seized on the issue as they seek to frame the contrast in the election.\par \par \uc2 ''It'll be a binary choice on who's going to restrict your rights,'' Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of Minnesota, who is the chairman of the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2 , said in an interview on Sunday. ''This will just be a narrative of their extremism, but this one is baked in because it's real. It's not theoretical.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has said he is ''proudly the person responsible'' for overturning Roe v. Wade -- a line Democrats are eager to highlight -- and has suggested that, if elected, he would allow states to prosecute women who violate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. He has also said he believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should be left to the states, disappointing some on the right.\par \par \uc2 ''Some states will be more conservative and some will be more liberal,'' Karoline Leavitt, a spokeswoman for Mr. Trump, said in a statement, but Mr. Trump ''supports the rights of individuals to determine their laws.''\par \par \uc2 ''President Trump also strongly supports ensuring women have access to the care they need to create healthy families, including widespread access to IVF, birth control, and contraception, and he always will,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Democrats successfully deployed messaging around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in critical races during the 2022 midterm elections a few months after Roe was overturned and in a number of special elections since.\par \par \uc2 This year, campaigns including Mr. Biden's are highlighting women's personal experiences with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans championed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Biden campaign released a television ad highlighting the experience of Kaitlyn Joshua, who said in the spot that when she had a miscarriage early in pregnancy, she was turned away from two Louisiana emergency rooms, ''a direct result of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  overturning Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 An advocacy group started by Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois also announced a digital ad effort aimed at swing and independent women in Arizona, Florida and Nevada, highlighting the health risks facing pregnant women in the post-Roe era. The House Democratic campaign arm is running mobile billboards in five competitive districts, blaring positive comments that Republican incumbents made about the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health decision overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 And major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are also mobilizing.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  advocacy and political organizations said they planned to spend $40 million this election cycle. \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  also joined a coalition of other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and progressive groups, which plan to raise and spend $100 million to devise a federal strategy aimed at clawing back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In the two years since Roe fell, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizations have focused on combating state-level restrictions in court and promoting ballot initiatives to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the state level, along with engaging in key campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are also arguing that a second Trump administration would go further in restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access by imposing sweeping new federal restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Senator Tina Smith, a Democrat from Minnesota, introduced legislation that would repeal the Comstock Act, a Civil War-era statue that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters worry could be used to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication -- even in states where it remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Smith, a former Planned Parenthood executive, said the law offered a powerful argument for Democrats, underscoring the contrast between a second term for Mr. Biden or one for Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''It's such a clear choice on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,'' she said. ''Understanding how a future Trump administration could use the Comstock Act makes this a salient issue in our state too, where people might they think they are safe.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Pat Ryan, a Democrat running in a swing district in New York, said his party should explain Comstock as a ''hidden ticking time bomb'' for Mr. Trump. Mr. Ryan, who won his seat with an aggressive embrace of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is encouraging his fellow Democrats to lean into Comstock and the future threats it may pose to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 But in this election, Americans are weighing a broad range of other considerations, too, and polls show that on a number of key issues -- though not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy -- voters say that Mr. Trump would do a better job than Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 ''Polling has consistently shown Biden and the Democrats already have an issue advantage on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and yet Trump continues to lead in the battleground states,'' \uc2 Robert Blizzard\uc2 , a Republican pollster, said. ''They will certainly lean in on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but unless Democrats find a way to puncture Trump's legacy on the economy, they will continue to struggle.''\par \par \uc2 Voters are also assessing the personal characteristics of Mr. Biden, 81, the oldest American president in history, and Mr. Trump, 78, who is the first American president convicted of a crime.\par \par \uc2 Many of these issues are likely to come up at their debate on Thursday, the first of the general-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris discussed \uc2  rights at an event Monday at the University of Maryland in College Park, Md. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KEVIN DIETSCH/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240625ek6p0004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
