\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Montana, Step Cleared To Put  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  on Ballot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The measure would affirm the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the State Constitution. Democrats hope that it will help Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  in his bid for re-election.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in Montana announced Friday that it had submitted enough signatures to put a measure on the November ballot that would ask voters to affirm a right to reproductive freedom in the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Montana would join four other states -- Colorado, South Dakota, Florida and New York -- with similar ballot measures this fall. The signatures must be certified by county clerks, who send them to the secretary of state, who has until Aug. 22 to set the ballot questions. Organizers said they submitted more than 117,000 signatures, well clear of the 60,039 required.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Montana until viability, or roughly 24 weeks of pregnancy, because its highest court ruled in 1999 that the state Constitution's provisions on privacy protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the coalition, Montanans Securing \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say the explicit constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prevent the Republican-controlled Legislature or future courts from undoing the 1999 ruling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in six other states are collecting signatures for citizen-sponsored ballot measures for November. Those include presidential battleground states like Arizona, where Democrats hope that strong support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights can lift the fortunes of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Montana reliably votes Republican in presidential contests, but Democrats hope that turnout for the ballot measure could help Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  in a tough fight for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Polls have found that -- as in all but a handful of states -- a majority of Montana residents want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and Republican state officials have tried to block the citizen-sponsored ballot measure. The coalition that turned in signatures Friday was tied up in a court fight for months after the state's attorney general tried to strike the proposed language for the ballot measure as ''legally insufficient.'' The state's Supreme Court ruled that he had exceeded his authority.\par \par \uc2 The Montana Family Foundation says the amendment would allow ''unlimited abortions,'' protect sex traffickers who force women to get abortions, remove parental consent laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow ''radical, irreversible gender reassignment surgery.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents unsuccessfully made those same arguments against ballot measures in Ohio in 2023 and Michigan in 2022. Those measures succeeded by comfortable margins, with polls showing that voters crossed party lines to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Montana amendment is similar to the ones in Michigan and Ohio. It would establish an express right ''to make and carry out decisions about one's own pregnancy,'' including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It would prohibit the government from ''denying or burdening the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.'' After viability, the state could not deny or burden access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the treating health care professional determines it is necessary to protect the pregnant patient's ''life or health.''\par \par \uc2 Seven states have had ballot measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the two years since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, ending federal protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have prevailed in all.\par \par \uc2 Montana is one of those states -- it had a ballot measure in 2022 that would have required doctors to provide medical care after premature births and abortions where the fetus was ''born alive.'' Opponents of the measure argued that the law already requires doctors to provide lifesaving care, and that the measure was intended to stoke fears about abortions late in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Voters rejected it by a six-point margin.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240623ek6n0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
